\end{figure*}


\section*{Nesting-Driven Moiré Resonance}

Building on our interpretation of the moiré-induced charge density modulation, we note that a contrast inversion between STM images taken at opposite bias polarities--similar to Fig.~\ref{fig:Moire}(g)--is often considered a hallmark of charge density wave (CDW) contributions to the topographic signal, yet has been experimentally observed only in a few rare cases~\cite{Rodriguez1999,Mallet2001,Spera2020}. We further highlight a striking observation: the moiré wave vector $q_\textrm{M} = 2\pi/\lambda_\textrm{M} \approx \SI{0.27}{\AA^{-1}}$, as extracted from STM data, is not only collinear with but also closely matches the Fermi surface nesting vector $q_\textrm{N} \approx \SI{0.24\pm0.04}{\AA^{-1}}$ identified within the FBSS via ARPES (see Fig.~\ref{fig:Char}(d))~\cite{Jovic2018}. This near-coincidence naturally raises the question of whether Fermi surface nesting within the FBSS contributes to the observed moiré scattering features.

To explore this possibility, we measured d$I$/d$V$-maps at bias voltages $V_{\text{bias}} = \SI{-25}{mV}$, $\SI{-15}{mV}$, $\SI{5}{mV}$, and $\SI{15}{mV}$, mapping the LDOS near the Fermi level. The corresponding 2D Fourier transforms (FFTs) of these LDOS maps are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Spectro}(a). As anticipated, the FFTs reproduce the pattern associated with the static moiré modulation seen in the STM topography [Fig.~\ref{fig:Moire}(c)]. More intriguingly, the FFTs reveal four distinct peaks, indicated by red arrows, that are faintly predicted by our structure factor model [inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:Moire}(d)] but were not clearly resolved in previous STM data [inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:Moire}(b)]. Unlike the static moiré peaks, these features exhibit a pronounced energy-dependent momentum shift: as the bias voltage increases from $\SI{-25}{mV}$ to $\SI{15}{mV}$, the peaks shift from $0.87q_\textrm{M}$ to $1.08q_\textrm{M}$, a currently unexplained energy dispersion that is about twice as steep as expected for quasi particle interference of the FBSS.

To examine the bias dependence of the moiré peak intensity, we acquired a dense STS grid and integrated the signal at the moiré wavevector $q_\textrm{M}$ across the entire dataset. The resulting black curve in Fig.~\ref{fig:Spectro}(b) reveals a pronounced resonance centered at $\SI{-5}{mV}$. Notably, this resonance coincides with a dip in the integrated LDOS that we obtained by integrating over all wavevectors of the dataset (purple curve), which exhibits maxima at $\SI{-30}{mV}$ and near zero bias, as previously discussed in the context of Fig.~\ref{fig:Moire}(f,g). This indicates that the resonance is not driven by the LDOS itself, but instead reflects a moiré-induced interaction with Fermi surface nesting in the FBSS, occurring at band fillings where $q_\textrm{M}$ and $q_\textrm{N}$ coincide--a phenomenon that, to the best of our knowledge, has not been previously reported.

To theoretically examine this resonance, we employed the Density Matrix Renormalization Group (DMRG) method, well-suited for probing quasi-one-dimensional strongly correlated systems~\cite{White_1992,SCHOLLWOCK201196}. We constructed a minimal Hamiltonian model incorporating key features of the \ce{RuO2 (110)} surface: (i) a tight-binding term ($H_\text{Kin}$) fitted to the DFT surface band structure and adapted to the FBSS ARPES results~\cite{Jovic2018,Jovic2021}, (ii) a periodic onsite modulation representing the moiré potential ($H_\text{Moiré}$), and (iii) a two-particle interaction term capturing electron correlations ($H_\text{Cor}$). 
The total Hamiltonian reads:
\begin{equation}
   H = H_\text{Kin} + H_\text{Moiré} + H_\text{Cor}.
\end{equation}

We consider an isolated \ce{Ru-O_{br}} chain along the crystallographic $c$-axis (\chain). Using open boundary conditions, we solve the model for varying FBSS binding energies by tuning the chemical potential. As an analogue to the experimental LDOS, we compute the expectation value of the site-resolved electronic density $\langle \hat{n}_i \rangle = \langle \hat{n}_{i\uparrow} + \hat{n}_{i\downarrow} \rangle$.

Consistent with experiment, $\langle \hat{n}_i \rangle$ displays a periodic modulation along the investigated 1D chain (see supplementary Fig.~6). Its FFT amplitude, plotted as a function of energy and momentum in supplementary Fig.~7, reveals two main features: a dominant, non-dispersive peak at $q_\textrm{M} = 2\pi/\lambda_\textrm{M}=\SI{0.27}{\AA^{-1}}$, matching the experimentally determined moiré period, and a quadratically dispersing mode associated with Friedel oscillations from the FBSS (see supplementary Sec.~5)~\cite{White2002}. 

The amplitude at $q_\textrm{M} = \SI{0.27}{\AA^{-1}}$, calculated with both the moiré potential ($H_\text{Moiré}$) and correlation effects ($H_\text{Cor}$) included, is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Spectro}(b) (orange curve). It reproduces the characteristic resonance at $\SI{-5}{mV}$ observed experimentally (black curve). When the moiré potential is included but its interaction with the FBSS is suppressed by setting $H_\text{Cor} = 0$, the correct periodicity $q_\textrm{M}$ is recovered, but the central peak seen in experiment is absent (green curve). Finally, suppressing both the moiré potential and the correlation effects leads to the complete disappearance of the peak at $q_\textrm{M}$; the corresponding amplitude in Fig.~\ref{fig:Spectro}(b) remains essentially flat (turquoise curve). These findings indicate that only the combined influence of the moiré potential and Fermi surface nesting, as captured by DMRG, can stabilize the experimentally observed resonance in STS. We therefore do not expect charge ordering at $q_\textrm{M}$ on the bulk-truncated (110) surface of \ce{RuO2}.

We note in passing that irrespective of the interactions, neither of these models have produced any long range ordering tendency of the spin expectation value $S^z_i = \langle \hat{n}_{i \uparrow} - \hat{n}_{i \downarrow} \rangle / 2$, and the obtained ground states are spin singlets.


\section*{Emergent Surface Reconstructions}


In addition to the charge ordering phenomena discussed above, we experimentally observe two further types of periodic modulations that are not captured by our minimal DMRG model.

First, a subset of high-quality, well-ordered samples reveals a faint but reproducible peak (orange arrow in Figs.~\ref{fig:Reconstructions}(a,b,c)) in the FFT, which does not correspond to any known moiré scattering vector. This feature appears rotated relative to both the primary lattice and the moiré peaks, thereby introducing an additional symmetry breaking in the Fourier pattern. The non-dispersive character of this feature allows us to exclude Friedel oscillations, and its insensitivity to a magnetic STM tip rules out magnetic order as its origin [Fig.~\ref{fig:Reconstructions}(c)]. We thus tentatively associate this signal with a second, long-range charge ordering, distinct from that discussed above. However, a comprehensive theoretical explanation of this phenomenon lies beyond the scope of this work.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1\columnwidth]{Figures/Fig_4.jpg}
\caption{\ce{RuO2(110)} surface instabilities: (a,b) FFT of constant current d$I$/d$V$ scans measured with spin polarized Gd-tip at (a) $V_\text{bias} = \SI{-60}{mV}$ and (b) $V_\text{bias} = \SI{60}{mV}$ ($I_\text{T} = \SI{200}{pA}$, $V_\text{rms} = \SI{5}{mV}$). Both scans show a non-dispersive long range ordering peak whose rotation breaks the symmetries of the moiré pattern. (c) Corresponding FFT of unpolarized Ag-tip scan showing qualitatively similar results, indicating the peak's origin to be non-magnetic. (d) Constant current STM topography scan showing a $(2 \times 2)$ reconstruction of the \ce{RuO2(110)} surface ($I_\text{T} = \SI{1000}{pA}$, $V_\text{bias} = \SI{-15}{mV}$). (e,f) Constant current STM topography scans on the same area taken before and after switching between the two metastable reconstructions ($I_\text{T} = \SI{20}{pA}$, $V_\text{bias} = \SI{-15}{mV}$). (g,h) $(2 \times 2)$ surface reconstruction of \ce{RuO2(110)} as found by DFT slab calculations.}\label{fig:Reconstructions}
\end{figure}



Second, we detect a prominent $2\times2$ periodic modulation on a subset of samples, characterized by an alternating intensity pattern of surface \ce{Ru} sites, effectively doubling the \ce{RuO2}(110) unit cell. This reconstruction is clearly visible in the STM topography shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Reconstructions}(d). As demonstrated in Figs.~\ref{fig:Reconstructions}(e,f), two equivalent metastable configurations of this $2\times2$ pattern can be reversibly switched via short voltage pulses, indicating bistability of this surface reconstruction.

A previous study under ambient conditions linked a similar $2\times2$ reconstruction to the adsorption of \ce{CO} molecules at oxygen bridge sites~\cite{Rossler2007a}. However, such an interpretation is incompatible with our observations, as \ce{CO} adsorption would quench the FBSS~\cite{Jovic2021}, which remains clearly present in our data. Instead, our own DFT calculations for a clean \ce{RuO2}(110) surface slab reveal a structural relaxation that naturally doubles the unit cell, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Reconstructions}(g,h). This relaxed configuration is energetically favorable by approximately \SI{25}{meV} per $2\times2$ unit cell compared to the previously assumed ground state. We propose that this structural instability, potentially coupled with an electronic reconstruction, underpins the $2\times2$ modulation observed in STM and may play a crucial role in dictating the catalytic surface activity of \ce{RuO2}.


\section*{No Evidence for Magnetic Surface Instability}

Lastly, in light of recent theoretical predictions of surface magnetism on \ce{RuO2}(110)\cite{Torun2013,Jeong2024,Ho2025}, we complete our investigation with spin-polarized STM data obtained with a \ce{Gd}-coated tip. The tip's magnetic sensitivity was independently confirmed on a \ce{Gd} surface (see supplementary material). Within the experimental resolution, we found no significant differences between data acquired with the spin-polarized tip [Figs.~\ref{fig:Reconstructions}(a,b)] and those obtained with an unpolarized Ag tip [Fig.~\ref{fig:Reconstructions}(c)]. Specifically, we detected no signatures of spin-density waves, magnetic domains, or spin-polarized surface states.  
Given the sensitivity limit of our setup--estimated to be approximately $0.3~\mu_B$ at a spatial resolution of one unit cell \cite{Kurz2002,hartl2022}--we regard this null result as a benchmark for future investigations of possible magnetic ordering on \ce{RuO2}(110). We emphasize, however, that ultrathin \ce{RuO2} films offer a rich platform where tunable electronic and structural instabilities intertwine, giving rise to ordering phenomena and surface reconstructions that, within the limits of our experimental resolution, appear nonmagnetic.

\section*{Methods}

\subsection*{Sample preparation}
\label{Methods:Growth}

\ce{RuO2(110)} thin films were grown by controlled oxidation of a clean, atomically ordered \ce{Ru(0001)} single crystal \cite{Over2000,Over2012}. The \ce{Ru(0001)} surface was prepared in ultra high vacuum (UHV) by repeated cycles of argon sputtering and annealing. The \ce{RuO2(110)} oxide layer was subsequently formed by a $\SI{2}{} - \SI{5}{h}$ controlled oxidation in $\SI{1e-4}{}$ to $\SI{5e-4}{mbar}$ oxygen at $\SI{630}{K}$. The resulting \ce{RuO2(110)} oxide layer was renewed after every refill of the STM cryostat, as this process introduces surface contamination.

\subsection*{STM and STS measurements}
\label{Supplement:spin_STM}

STM data was acquired at sample temperatures below $\SI{5}{K}$ and base pressures below $\SI{5e-11}{mbar}$ (Omicron low-temperature LT STM). The spectroscopic \ce{Ag}-tip was obtained by dipping a chemically etched \ce{W}-tip several nanometers into an \ce{Ag} crystal, and subsequently characterized by imaging the surface state of \ce{Ag(111)}. STS maps and point spectroscopy d$I$/d$V$ curves were recorded using a standard lock-in technique (Stanford Research Systems: SR830 DSP) with a modulation frequency of \SI{787}{Hz}.

Spin-polarized STM data was acquired at sample temperatures below $\SI{5}{K}$ and base pressures below $\SI{5e-11}{mbar}$ (home-built low-temperature STM). The magnetic tip sensitivity was obtained by dipping the chemically etched \ce{W}-tip several nanometers into a deposited Gd film. The magnetic contrast of the STM-tip was ensured by imaging the Gd magnetic domain structure in d$I$/d$V$ maps (see supplementary Fig.~4(a-d) for a constant current STM topography and a constant current STS d$I$/d$V$ map of a Gd surface.). Spin-polarized STS maps and point spectroscopy d$I$/d$V$ curves were recorded using a standard lock-in technique (Stanford Research Systems: SR810 DSP) with a modulation frequency of \SI{5309}{Hz}. More details of the spin-polarized STM/STS setup and the tip characterization process can be found in Ref. \cite{hartl2022}.

FFT analysis for both methods was performed using Gwyddion 2.67. The displayed images correspond to the modulus output, processed with a Hann window function and mean-subtracted for clarity.


\subsection*{Density Functional Theory (DFT)}
\label{Methods:DFT}
DFT calculations have been performed employing the Vienna ab initio Simulation Package (VASP) \cite{Kresse1996,Kresse1999} with the projector augmented wave (PAW) method \cite{Bloechl1994}. The generalized gradient approximation (GGA) \cite{Perdew1992} within the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) approach \cite{Perdew1996} was used for the exchange and correlation functions. A plane-wave cutoff of \SI{500}{eV} was used for the truncation of the basis set. Concerning the relaxation of the electronic and ionic degrees of freedom, they were considered converged when the output difference between two steps was respectively equal or smaller than \SI{1e-6}{eV} and \SI{1e-6}{eV/\AA}. The electronic minimization is based on the Blocked-Davidson algorithm and the structure optimization is based on a conjugate gradient algorithm. The number of $k$-points for the enlarged 2$\times$2 slab is 8$\times$4$\times$1. The thickness of the slab super-cell is $\sim$18\AA, with $\sim$17\AA ~of vacuum.
Partial occupancies have been determined via Gaussian smearing with a width of \SI{0.1}{eV}. When considered, spin-orbit coupling (SOC) has been included self-consistently. 


\subsection*{Density Matrix Renormalization Group (DMRG)}
\label{Methods:DMRG}

DMRG is a powerful numerical technique originally developed for studying one-dimensional quantum many-body systems \cite{White_1992}. It has since been extended to higher dimensions and various other applications. DMRG is particularly effective for systems with strong correlations and has been widely used to investigate the ground state properties, low-lying excitations, and dynamic correlations of quantum systems~\cite{SCHOLLWOCK201196}.
\\
The core idea of DMRG is to iteratively optimize the wave function whilst only retaining the most significant part of the Hilbert space. Let us consider a quantum system consisting of $N$ sites, where each site is described by a $d$-dimensional local Hilbert space $\lbrace\sigma_i\rbrace$. Then, any state can be represented by a rank-$N$ tensor 
\begin{equation}
    \ket{\psi}=\sum_{\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_N} c_{\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_N}\ket{\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_N}.
\end{equation}
By applying a series of singular value decompositions (SVD), we can represent $\ket{\psi}$ as a matrix product state (MPS)
\begin{equation}
\ket{\psi}=\sum_{\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_N}A_{1,a_1}^{\sigma_1}A_{a_1,a_2}^{\sigma_2}\dots A_{a_{N-1},1}^{\sigma_N}\ket{\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_N},
\end{equation}
where the $A$' s are rank-$3$ tensors. By limiting the number of singular values in each SVD to some dimension $D$, we obtain a compressed representation of the quantum state.
\\
To find the ground state of a quantum system represented by a Hamiltonian $H$ in the space of MPS of dimension $D$, we minimize the energy
\begin{equation}
    E_0=\min_{\ket{\psi}}\frac{\bra{\psi}H\ket{\psi}}{\bra{\psi}\ket{\psi}}.
\end{equation}
In constrast to exact diagonalization, the strategy of DMRG is to minimize the energy by sweeping through all tensors and optimizing two tensors at a time by solving an effective eigenvalue problem. This is performed untill we reach convergence in energy~\cite{SCHOLLWOCK201196}.
\\
As the DMRG update only acts on two tensors at the same time, the algorithm is prone to getting stuck in local minima. To avoid this problem we use White's noise term \cite{White_2005}. All calculations were performed using the ITENSOR library \cite{ITensor,ITensor-r0.3} with an SVD cutoff of $10^{-10}$. We checked for convergence w.r.t. bond dimensions of $512$, $1024$ and $2048$. 


