\begin{abstract}
	We describe several randomized collections of $3\times 3$ rotation matrices and analyze their associated logarithmic energy. The best one (i.e. the one attaining the lowest expected logarithmic energy) is constructed by choosing $r$ spherical points, which come from the zeros of a randomly chosen degree $r$ polynomial, and considering at each of these points a set of $s$ evenly distributed rotation matrices. This construction yields a new upper bound on the minimal logarithmic energy of $n=rs$ rotation matrices.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle 

\section{Introduction and main result}
Constructively characterizing sets of points within a given space that possess specific desired properties is a fundamental topic in approximation theory. This involves designing and analyzing point distributions that satisfy various criteria, such as minimizing interpolation error, maximizing density in certain regions, or ensuring uniform coverage of a domain. The most basic and famous examples are probably polynomial approximation, where Chebyshev nodes are used to minimize the Runge phenomenon, leading to more accurate interpolation, or numerical integration, where Gaussian quadrature selects points and weights to achieve exact integration for polynomials of a given degree. A less known but remarkable result is the solution by Fejer to the so-called Fekete problem in the unit interval, that is, finding $x_1,\ldots,x_n\in[-1,1]$ such that the logarithmic energy
\begin{equation}\label{eq:intervalo}
\E(x_1,\ldots,x_n)=\sum_{i\neq j}\log|x_i-x_j|^{-1}
\end{equation}
is minimized. The solution turns out to be the extremes of the interval plus the $n-2$ zeros of a Gegenbauer polynomial; see \cite{Fejer,Granada}.

In higher-dimensional spaces the situation is more involved and much less is known about concrete, optimal point distributions. The topic has been intensively studied in the unit sphere, often taking as a criterium of optimality the minimization, in the spirit of \eqref{eq:intervalo}, of some energy such as the logarithmic energy (where absolute value in \eqref{eq:intervalo} is changed to Euclidean distance), the sum of the inverses of the mutual distances between the points or generalizations of that quantity where the distance is taken to an arbitrary power, see the foundational work \cite{Whyte1952}. A huge bibliography has been generated since then. It is impossible to make a comprehensive study but we mention some works such as \cite{RakhmanovSaffZhou1994,Brauchart2008,AlishahiZamani,BrauchartGrabner2015,BrauchartHardinSaff2012,HMS,BeltranMarzoOrtega,BeterminSandier2018,Lauritsen2021}, where the mentioned energies are considered and estimated, both from the theoretical and numerical points of view. Note that Smale's 7th problem from his celebrated list \cite{Smale1998}, which is wide open and was first posed by Shub and Smale in \cite{ShubSmale1993}, is precisely the generation of points in the usual $2$-sphere in such a way that the logarithmic energy is close to minimal. The actual minimizers are sometimes called elliptic Fekete points.

In the last years, the interest for point collections in other spaces different from the sphere has increased, often centered in the projective spaces, see \cite{Pedro2023,Andersonetal2023,Lizarte,BFLG2025}, the flat tori \cite{Marzo2018,Borda2024}, the special orthogonal group \cite{Mitchell,BeltranFerizovic2020} or the Grassmannians \cite{Grass2023,Pedro2025}. The problem can also be studied in a general setting where the space is any compact set in $\R^n$, and some celebrated results such as the so-called poppy seed bagel theorem are known, see \cite{BorodachovHardinSaff2019} for a complete monography.

In this paper we focus our attention on the special orthogonal group $\so\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}$, that is, the rotation group in $\R^3$ or the set of $3\times 3$ orthogonal matrices with determinant equal to $1$. We will search for collections of matrices $O_1,\ldots,O_n\in\so$ with as low as possible values of the logarithmic energy which is described in terms of the Frobenius norm of the diferences $O_i-O_j$ as follows:
\begin{align*}
\E(O_1,\ldots,O_n)=&\sum_{i\neq j}\log\|O_i-O_j\|_F^{-1}\\
=&-\frac12\sum_{i\neq j}\log\|O_i-O_j\|_F^2\\
=&-\frac12\sum_{i\neq j}\log(6-2\tr(O_i^TO_j)).
\end{align*}
The main problems that we look at are the following:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Which is the lowest possible value for $\E(O_1,\ldots,O_n)$? From the general theory \cite{BorodachovHardinSaff2019} and from \cite{Andersonetal2023}, we know that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:minimo}
\min_{O_1,\ldots,O_n\in\so}\E(O_1,\ldots,O_n)=\kappa n^2-\frac13n\log n+O(n),
\end{equation}
where
\[
\kappa=\frac{1}{\vol(\so)^2}\int_{O,O'\in\so}\log\|O-O'\|_F^{-1}\,dO\,dO',
\]
is the continuous energy, that is, the logarithmic energy of two matrices $O,O'\in\so$ chosen independently at random with the standard Haar distribution in $\so$. We will see below (see Proposition \ref{prop:continuousso3}) that
\[
\kappa=-\frac{1+\log2}{2}.
\]
\item Can we generate collections $O_1,\ldots,O_n$ whose energy is close (the closest, the better) to that minimal value? 
\end{enumerate}
Hence, our aim will be to find collections of orthogonal matrices with provable as low as possible values for the $O(n)$ term in \eqref{eq:minimo}. Our main result can be summarized as follows:
\begin{theorem}[Main result]\label{th:main}
	For all $n$ in a certain infinite subsequence of the natural numbers (starting $n=2,     3,     4,     5,    12,    14,    16,    18,    20,    22,    36,    39,    42,    45,\ldots$), there exists a randomized constructive way to produce $n$ rotation matrices $O_1,\ldots,O_n$ such that the expected value of the logarithmic energy of $O_1,\ldots,O_n$ is
	\[
	\kappa n^2-\frac13n\log + C_{\textup{zeros}}n+o(n),
	\]
	where $C_{\textup{zeros}}=-0.4191502\dotso$ is a constant.
\end{theorem}
See Proposition \ref{prop:zeros} for the actual construction and \eqref{eq:czeros} for a precise description of the constant.

The main tool we use is \cref{th:logenergy}, which establishes that, given any choice of $r$ spherical points, the logarithmic energy of the associated collection of $n=rs$ rotation matrices (obtained by choosing $s$ equidistant rotation matrices that fix each point) can be computed by studying another energy that depends solely on the spherical points. This allows us to study this other energy for different collections of spherical points.  The configuration yielding the best result is described in \cref{th:main}.













\section{The continuous energy}
In this section we compute the continuous energy $\kappa$, i.e. the expected value of the energy for random, uniformly chosen  $O,O'\in\so$.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:continuousso3}
Let $O,O'\in\so$ be chosen uniformly and independently with respect to the Haar measure in $\so$. Then, we have
\begin{align*}
\kappa=\Ex_{O,O'\in\so}\left(\log\|O-O'\|_F^{-1}\right)=-\frac{1+\log2}2.
\end{align*}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Using a symmetry argument we have
\begin{align*}
\Ex_{O,O'\in\so}\left(\log\|O-O'\|_F^{-1}\right)=&\Ex_{O\in\so}\left(\log\|O-\Id_3\|_F^{-1}\right)
\end{align*}
This last can be computed using \cite[Equation (1) and Lemma 3.1]{BeltranFerizovic2020}, which yield
\begin{align*}
\Ex_{O,O'\in\so}\left(\log\|O-O'\|_F^{-1}\right)=&-\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi\log\left(\sqrt{8}\sin\frac{\theta}{2}\right)\sin^2\frac{\theta}2\,d\theta\\
=&-\frac2\pi\int_0^{\pi/2}\log\left(8\sin^2\alpha\right)\sin^2\alpha\,d\alpha\\
=&-\frac{\log8}{2}-\frac4\pi\int_0^{\pi/2}\log\left(\sin\alpha\right)\sin^2\alpha\,d\alpha\\
=&-\frac{\log8}{2}-\frac12(1-\log4)\\
=&-\frac{1+\log2}2,
\end{align*}
where we have used \cite[4.384.9]{integrales}.
\end{proof}





\section{Expected energy of the harmonic ensemble in $\so$}
To the best of our knowledge, so far there is only one known sequence of point sets in $\so$ whose energy has been computed theoretically so far: the so-called harmonic ensemble in $\so$ studied in \cite{BeltranFerizovic2020}. In that work, the logarithmic energy is not computed but rather the authors study the Green energy and the $s$-energy for $s\in(0,3)$, which are given respectively by
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{G}}(O_1,\ldots,O_n)=&\sum_{i\neq j}\mathcal G(O_i,O_j),\\
\mathcal{E}_{s}(O_1,\ldots,O_n)=&\sum_{i\neq j}\|O_i-O_j\|_F^{-s},
\end{align*}
where $\mathcal G$ is the Green function of the Laplace--Beltrami operator in $\so$. For each $n$ of the form
\[
n=\binom{2L+3}{3}=\frac43L^3+O(L^2),\quad L=1,2,\ldots,
\]
the harmonic ensemble is a collection of $n$ random matrices $O_1,\ldots,O_n$ with a certain distribution in $\so\times\cdots\times\so$ ($n$ times). The expected logarithmic energy of that collection of points can be studied with the same techniques from \cite{BeltranFerizovic2020} as we do below, or can alternatively be computed from \cite{Andersonetal2023} using the fact that $\so$ is essentially isometric to the real projective space $\mathbb{RP}^3$.
\begin{theorem}
	The expected logarithmic energy of the points coming from the harmonic ensemble in $\SO(3)$ is
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\kappa n^2-\frac{1}{3}n\log n+Cn+o(n),
	\end{equation*}
	%
	with
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		C=\frac{7}{3}-\gamma+\frac{\log 2}{6}-\frac{\log 3}{3}\approx 1.5054,
	\end{equation*}
	%
	where $\gamma$ is the Euler--Mascheroni constant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
	Let $\mathbb{E}=\expectation{x\in(\SO(3))^n}{\E(x)}$ be the expected logarithmic energy. Then, following the first lines of the proof of \cite[Proposition 3.2]{BeltranFerizovic2020},
	%
	\begin{align*}    
		\mathbb{E}=&\kappa n^2 +\frac{2}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi}\log\biggl(\sqrt{8}\sin\frac{t}{2}\biggr)\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}\biggl(\cos\frac{t}{2}\biggr)^2\sin^2\frac{t}{2}\dd t\\
		=&\kappa n^2 +\frac{4}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\log\Bigl(\sqrt{8}\sin\theta\Bigr)\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta.
	\end{align*}
	%
	Let
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		I=\frac{4}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\log\Bigl(\sqrt{8}\sin\theta\Bigr)\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta.
	\end{equation*}
	%
	We have $I=I_1+I_2$ where
	%
	\begin{align*}
		I_1=&\frac{6\log 2}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta,\\
		I_2=&\frac{4}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\log(\sin\theta)\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta.
	\end{align*}
	%
	Using \cref{lemma:aux-integral-1,lemma:aux-integral-2}, we have
	%
	\begin{align*}
		I_1
		=&\frac{6\log 2}{\pi}\biggl(\frac{\pi}{3}L^3+O(L^{5/2})\biggr)=2L^3\log2+o(L^3).
	\end{align*}
	%
	Using \cref{lemma:aux-integral-3,lemma:aux-integral-4}, we have
	%
	\begin{align*}
		I_2=&\frac{4}{\pi}\biggl(-\frac{\pi}{3}L^3\log L+\biggl(\frac{(7-3\gamma-6\log{2})\pi}{9}\biggr)L^3+o(L^{3})\biggr)\\
		=&-\frac{4}{3}L^3\log L+\biggl(\frac{7-3\gamma-6\log 2}{3}\biggr)\frac{4}{3}L^3+o(L^{3}).
	\end{align*}
	%
	Then, the expected energy is
	%
	\begin{align*}    
		\mathbb{E}=\kappa n^2 -\frac{4}{3}L^3\log L+\biggl(\frac{7-3\gamma-6\log 2}{3}+\frac{3}{2}\log 2\biggr)\frac{4}{3}L^3+o(L^{3}).
	\end{align*}
	%
	The result follows using that $N=(4/3)L^3+O(L^2)$.
\end{proof}

In the proof we have used the following auxiliary results.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:aux-integral-1}
	The following equality holds:
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta=\frac{\pi}{3}L^3+O(L^{5/2}).
	\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
	To prove this lemma, we use Hilb's formula \eqref{eq:Hilb-Gegenbauer-2} for the Gegenbauer polynomials $\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}$. Using this formula, we have
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta=A_1+A_2+A_3,
	\end{equation*}
	%
	where
	%
	\begin{align*}
		A_1&=\frac{\pi}{8}(2L)^{3}(1+O(L^{-1}))\theta^{-1}\BesselJ{3/2}\bigl((2L+2)\theta\bigr)^2\\
		A_2&=\textup{Error}^2\\
		A_3&=L^{3/2}(1+O(L^{-1}))\theta^{-1/2}\BesselJ{3/2}\bigl((2L+2)\theta\bigr)\textup{Error}\\
	\end{align*}
	%
	and
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\textup{Error}=\begin{cases}
			O(L^{-3/2}) & L^{-1}\leq \theta\leq L^{-1/2}\\
			(\theta^{3})O(n^{3/2}) & 0<\theta<L^{-1}.
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation*}
	%
	Hence, calling $I$ the integral of the lemma, we have
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		I=I_1+I_2+I_3,
	\end{equation*}
	%
	where
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		I_j=\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}A_j\dd\theta.
	\end{equation*}
	%
	First, note that $\textup{Error}$ is always $O(L^{-3/2})$. Hence, $I_2=O(L^{-7/2})$. To estimate $I_3$, note that $\abs{\BesselJ{3/2}(x)}\leq 1$ for all $x\in\R$, so
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		I_3=O\biggl(\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}\theta^{-1/2}\dd\theta\biggr)=O\biggl(\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}\theta^{-1/2}\dd\theta\biggr)=O(L^{-1/4}).
	\end{equation*}
	%
	Finally, we estimate $I_1$. Note that
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		I_1=(1+O(L^{-1}))\biggl(\int_{0}^{\infty}A_1\dd\theta-\int_{L^{-1/2}}^{\infty}A_1\dd\theta\biggr)
	\end{equation*}
	%
	Using that $\BesselJ{3/2}(x)=O(x^{-1/2})$ as $x\tendsto\infty$, we have
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_{L^{-1/2}}^{\infty}A_1\dd\theta=O\biggl(L^3(2L+2)^{-1}\int_{L^{-1/2}}^{\infty}\theta^{-2}\dd\theta\biggr)=O(L^{5/2})
	\end{equation*}
	%
	Finally, we have
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_{0}^{\infty}A_1\dd\theta=\frac{\pi}{8}(2L)^3\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{\BesselJ{3/2}((2L+2)\theta)^2}{\theta}\dd\theta=\frac{\pi}{8}(2L)^3\frac{1}{3}=\frac{\pi}{3}L^3.
	\end{equation*}
	%
	The lemma follows. For the last integral involving Bessel's function we have used \eqref{eq:integral-Bessel}.
\end{proof}


\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:aux-integral-2}
	The following equality holds:
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_{L^{-1/2}}^{\pi/2}\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta=O(L^{5/2}).
	\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
	From \eqref{eq:bounds-Gegenbauer} we have the estimate $\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}=
	\theta^{-2}O(L)$. Hence, using that $\sin\theta=O(\theta)$,
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\abs[\bigg]{\int_{L^{-1/2}}^{\pi/2}\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta}=O\biggl(L^2\int_{L^{-1/2}}^{\pi/2}\theta^{-2}\biggr)=O(L^{5/2}).
	\end{equation*}
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:aux-integral-3}
	The following equality holds:
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_{L^{-1/2}}^{\pi/2}\log(\sin\theta)\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta=O(L^{5/2}\log L).
	\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}
	We use the estimates $\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}=
	\theta^{-2}O(L)$ and $\sin\theta=O(\theta)$, together with $|\log\sin\theta|\leq2|\log \theta|)$ for small $\theta$. Hence, the integral of the lemma can be estimated as
	%
	\begin{align*}
		\MoveEqLeft O\biggl(L^2\int_{L^{-1/2}}^{\pi/2}\theta^{-2}\log\theta\dd\theta\biggr)=O\biggl(L^2\biggl(-\frac{1}{\theta}-\frac{\log \theta}{\theta}\biggr)\eval_{L^{-1/2}}^{\pi/2}\biggr)=O(L^{5/2}\log L).
	\end{align*}
\end{proof}


\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:aux-integral-4}
	The following equality holds:
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}\log(\sin\theta)\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta=-\frac{\pi}{3}L^3\log L+\biggl(\frac{(7-3\gamma-6\log{2})\pi}{9}\biggr)L^3+o(L^3).
	\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
	Note that
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\log(\sin\theta)=\log(\theta(1+O(\theta^2)))=\log\theta+\log(1+O(\theta^2))=\log\theta+O(\theta^2).
	\end{equation*}
	%
	Hence, the integral of the lemma equals
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}\log\theta\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta+O\biggl(\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\theta^4\dd\theta\biggr).
	\end{equation*}
	%
	Using the bounds \eqref{eq:bounds-Gegenbauer} for the Gegenbauer polynomials, we have $\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)=\theta^{-2}O(L)$, so the second integral is
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		O\biggl(L^2\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}1\dd\theta\biggr)=O(L^{3/2}).
	\end{equation*}
	%
	Let us estimate then
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}\log\theta\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta
	\end{equation*}
	%
	Making the substitution $\theta=\hat\theta/(2L)$, this integral equals
	%
	\begin{align*}
		\MoveEqLeft\frac{1}{2L}\int_{0}^{2L^{1/2}}\log(\hat\theta/(2L))\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos(\hat\theta/(2L)))^2\sin^2(\hat\theta/(2L))\dd\hat\theta\\
		&=\frac{1}{2L}\int_{0}^{2L^{1/2}}\log\hat\theta\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos(\hat\theta/(2L)))^2\sin^2(\hat\theta/(2L))\dd\hat\theta\\
		&\quad-\frac{\log (2L)}{2L}\int_{0}^{2L^{1/2}}\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos(\hat\theta/(2L)))^2\sin^2(\hat\theta/(2L))\dd\hat\theta\\
		&=\frac{1}{2L}\int_{0}^{2L^{1/2}}\log\hat\theta\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos(\hat\theta/(2L)))^2\sin^2(\hat\theta/(2L))\dd\hat\theta\\
		&\quad-\log (2L)\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta.
	\end{align*}
	%
	From \cref{lemma:aux-integral-1} we have
	%
	\begin{align*}
		-\log (2L)\int_{0}^{L^{-1/2}}\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos\theta)^2\sin^2\theta\dd\theta&=-\log(2L)\biggl(\frac{\pi}{3}L^3+O(L^{5/2})\biggr)\\
		&=-\frac{\pi}{3}L^3\log (2L)+O(L^{5/2}\log L)\\
		&=-\frac{\pi}{3}L^3\log L-\frac{\pi\log2}{3}L^3+o(L^3).
	\end{align*}
	%
	Finally, we claim that
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\frac{1}{2L}\int_{0}^{2L^{1/2}}\log\hat\theta\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos(\hat\theta/(2L)))^2\sin^2(\hat\theta/(2L))\dd\hat\theta=\frac{\pi}{8}\biggl(\frac{7-3\gamma-3\log{2}}{9}\biggr)(2L)^3+o(L^3).
	\end{equation*}
	%
	To prove this claim, it suffices to check that
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\lim_{L\tendsto\infty}\frac{1}{(2L)^4}\int_{0}^{2L^{1/2}}\log\hat\theta\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos(\hat\theta/(2L)))^2\sin^2(\hat\theta/(2L))\dd\hat\theta=\frac{\pi}8\cdot\frac{7-3\gamma-3\log{2}}{9}
	\end{equation*}
	%
	Note that we can write the previous expression as
	%
	\begin{align*}
		\MoveEqLeft\lim_{L\tendsto\infty}\frac{1}{(2L)^6}\int_{0}^{\infty}\chi_{[0,2L^{1/2}]}\log\hat\theta\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos(\hat\theta/(2L)))^2\hat\theta^2(1+o(1))\dd\hat\theta\\
		&=\lim_{L\tendsto\infty}\frac{1}{(2L)^6}\int_{0}^{\infty}\chi_{[0,2L^{1/2}]}\log\hat\theta\Gegenbauer{2L}{2}(\cos(\hat\theta/(2L)))^2\hat\theta^2(1+o(1))\dd\hat\theta
	\end{align*}
	%
	Using the dominated convergence theorem and the Mehler--Heine formula \eqref{eq:Mehler-Heine-Gegenbauer-2}, this limit equals
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		\frac{\pi}{8}\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{\BesselJ{3/2}(\hat\theta)^2\log\hat\theta}{\hat\theta}\dd\hat\theta=\frac{\pi}{8}\cdot\frac{7-3\gamma-3\log{2}}{9},
	\end{equation*}
	%
	where the last equality follows from \cref{eq:integral-Bessel-log}.
\end{proof}
















\section{A construction in $\so$ from a set of points in $\s$}
In this section we describe a constructive collection of points in $\so$ from any collection of points in $\s$. A similar idea has been studied in \cite{Mitchell} from a mainly numerical point of view. Let 
\[
R(\phi):=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\cos\big(\phi\big)&-\sin\big(\phi\big) & 0\\
	\sin\big(\phi\big)&\cos\big(\phi\big) & 0\\
	0 &0 & 1
\end{array}\right]
\]
be the rotation matrix with angle $\phi$, with base point $e_3=(0,0,1)^T$.

Let $p\in\s$ be a spherical point. Let $s\in\{1,2,\ldots\}$ be a positive integer. We say that $s$ matrices $O_1,\ldots,O_s\in\so$ are \emph{equally spaced with base point $p$} if there exists an orthogonal matrix $H_p\in \so$ with $H_pe_3=p$ and an angle $\phi\in[0,2\pi)$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:OR}
O_j=H_p R(\phi_j+\phi).
\end{equation}
Here $\phi_j=2\pi j/s$, where $j=1,\ldots,s$, are the $s$ roots of unity. For example, the matrices $R(\phi_j)$, $j=1,\ldots,s$, are equally spaced with base point $e_3$ and indeed they are, for any fixed $s$, the unique matrices with that property up to the choice of a random phase.

One can give explicit formulas for $H_p$ in different manners. Here is a particular choice:
\begin{itemize}
\item If $p=e_3$, let $H_p^{(0)}=\diag(1,1,1)=\Id_3$, the identity matrix.
\item If $p=-e_3$, let $H_p^{(0)}=\diag(1,-1,-1)$.
\item Otherwise, let $p=(x,y,z)^T$, with $x^2+y^2\neq0$, and let
\[
H_p^{(0)}:=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
	\frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} &\frac{zx}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  & x\\
	\frac{-x}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} &\frac{zy}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} & y\\
0 &	-\sqrt{x^2+y^2} & z
\end{array}\right]=:[p^2,p^1,p]\in\so.
\]
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}\label{def:pointsinso3}
Given $r$ points $p_1,\ldots,p_r\in\s$ and a positive integer $s$, the \emph{collection of special orthogonal matrices associated to $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ and $s$} is a random set of $rs$ elements in $\so$ obtained as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item Choose $\phi^{(1)},\ldots,\phi^{(r)}$ uniformly at random in $[0,2\pi)$.
\item Consider the set
\[
\bigcup_{i=1}^r\bigcup_{j=1}^s\left\{O_{i,j}\right\},\quad\text{where}\quad O_{i,j}=H_{p_i}^{(0)}R(\phi_j+\phi^{(i)}).
\]
That is to say, for each $p_i$, $1\leq i\leq r$, we choose $s$ equally spaced matrices with base point $p_i$, rotated by a random phase $\phi^{(i)}$.
\end{enumerate}
Since the set described above is actually a random set of points, that is, a random process in $\so$, in order to study its properties we will compute the expected value of the logarithmic energy of the point set when the phases are chosen at random uniformly in $[0,2\pi)$.
\end{definition}

\section{The expected logarithmic energy of the points in the construction}
In this section we prove the following result, which relates the logarithmic energy of the collection of $n$ special orthogonal matrices associated with a collection of spherical points, to a certain expresion involving only the dot products of these spherical points (alternatively, we might write it in terms of the mutual distances between the spherical points). This will allow us to compute the logarithmic energy of these sets of orthogonal matrices using only computations on the sphere, a topic that has been much more studied.

\begin{theorem}\label{th:logenergy}
The expected logarithmic energy of the collection of special orthogonal matrices associated with $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ and $s$ is equal to
\begin{multline*}
\Ex\left(-\sum_{(i,j)\neq(i',j')}\log\|O_{i,j}-O_{i',j'}\|_F\right)=\\
-\frac{n^2}{2}\log2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s-s^2\sum_{i\neq i'}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}{2}}\right).
\end{multline*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $S=S_1+S_2$ be the expected value in the lemma, where
\begin{align*}
S_1=&\Ex\left(-\sum_{i=1}^r\sum_{j\neq j'}\log\|O_{i,j}-O_{i,j'}\|_F\right),\\
S_2=&\Ex\left(-\sum_{i\neq i'}\sum_{j,j'=1}^s\log\|O_{i,j}-O_{i',j'}\|_F\right).
\end{align*}
From Lemma \ref{lem:equallyspaced} below we have
\[
S_1=-r\left(\frac{s(s-1)}{2}\log2+s\log s\right).
\]
Moreover, from Lemma \ref{lem:twopoints} below, we have
\[
S_2=-\sum_{i\neq i'}s^2\log\left(\sqrt2+\sqrt{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}\right).
\]
Adding the two terms and simplifying finishes the proof.

\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:equallyspaced}
Let $p\in\s$ and let $O_1,\ldots,O_s\in\so$ be $s$ equally spaced matrices with base point $p$. Then,
\[
\sum_{i\neq j}\log\|O_i-O_j\|_F=\frac{s(s-1)}{2}\log2+s\log s.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $S$ be the sum in the lemma. Note that
\begin{align*}
S=&\log\prod_{i\neq j}\|H_p R(\phi_i+\phi)-H_p R(\phi_j+\phi)\|_F\\
=&\log\prod_{i\neq j}\|\Id_3-R(\phi_j+\phi)R(\phi_i+\phi)^{-1}\|_F\\
=&\frac12\log\prod_{i\neq j}\|\Id_3-R(\phi_j-\phi_i)\|_F^2\\
=&\frac12\log\prod_{i\neq j}\left(4-4\cos(\phi_j-\phi_i)\right)\\
=&s(s-1)\log2+\frac12\log\prod_{i=1}^s\prod_{\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{j=1}{j\neq i}}^s\left(1-\cos\left(2\pi\frac{i-j}{s}\right)\right).
\end{align*}
The inner product can be rewriten (taking $k=i-j$) as:
\begin{align*}
\prod_{\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{j=1}{j\neq i}}^s\left(1-\cos\left(2\pi\frac{i-j}{s}\right)\right)=&
\prod_{k=1}^{i-1}\left(1-\cos\left(2\pi\frac{k}{s}\right)\right)
\prod_{k=i-s}^{-1}\left(1-\cos\left(2\pi\frac{k}{s}\right)\right).
\end{align*}
This last part, taking $\hat k=k+s$ and using the periodicity of the cosine, equals:
\begin{align*}
\prod_{k=i-s}^{-1}\left(1-\cos\left(2\pi\frac{k}{s}\right)\right)=&
\prod_{\hat k=i}^{s-1}\left(1-\cos\left(2\pi\frac{\hat k-s}{s}\right)\right)\\
=&
\prod_{\hat k=i}^{s-1}\left(1-\cos\left(2\pi\frac{\hat k}{s}\right)\right).
\end{align*}
All together, we have proved:
\begin{align*}
\prod_{\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{j=1}{j\neq i}}^s\left(1-\cos\left(2\pi\frac{i-j}{s}\right)\right)=
\prod_{k=1}^{s-1}\left(1-\cos\left(2\pi\frac{k}{s}\right)\right).
\end{align*}
In particular, its value is independent of $i$. We hence have:
\begin{align*}
S=&s(s-1)\log2+\frac{s}2\log\prod_{j=1}^{s-1}\left(1-\cos\left(\frac{2\pi j}{s}\right)\right)\\
=&s(s-1)\log2+\frac{s(s-1)}{2}\log2+s\log\prod_{j=1}^{s-1}\sin\left(\frac{\pi j}{s}\right),
\end{align*}
where we have used  $1-\cos(2x)=2\sin(x)^2$. From \cite[1.392.1]{integrales} we have
\[
\prod_{j=0}^{s-1}\sin\Big(x+\frac{j\pi}{s}\Big)=\frac{\sin(sx)}{2^{s-1}},
\]
and hence as $x\ra 0$
\[
\prod_{j=1}^{s-1}\sin\Big(x+\frac{j\pi}{s}\Big)=\frac{1}{2^{s-1}}\frac{\sin(sx)}{\sin(x)}\Longrightarrow \prod_{j=1}^{s-1}\sin\Big(\frac{j\pi}{s}\Big)=\frac{s}{2^{s-1}}.
\]
We thus have:
\begin{align*}
S=&\frac32s(s-1)\log2+s\log\frac{s}{2^{s-1}},
\end{align*}
and the lemma follows.
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:an_integral}
Let $H\in\so$. Then, for $\alpha,\beta\in\R$ with $|\beta|\leq\alpha/3$, we have:
\[
\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\left(\alpha+\beta\tr\left(HR(\phi)\right)\right)\,d\phi= 2\log\frac{\sqrt{\alpha-\beta}+\sqrt{\alpha+\beta+2\beta e_3^THe_3}}{2}.
\]
In particular, taking $\alpha=6,\,\beta=-2$, we have:
\[
\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\left(6-2\tr\left(HR(\phi)\right)\right)\,d\phi=2\log\left(\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{1-e_3^THe_3}\right).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $I$ be the integral in the lemma. Writing $h_{ij}$ for the entries of $H$, we have
\begin{align*}
I=&\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\left(\alpha+\beta\left((h_{11}+h_{22})\cos\phi+(h_{12}-h_{21})\sin\phi+h_{33}\right)\right)\,d\phi\\
=&\log A+\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\frac{\alpha+\beta\left((h_{11}+h_{22})\cos\phi+(h_{12}-h_{21})\sin\phi+h_{33}\right)}{A}\,d\phi,
\end{align*}
for any choice of $A>0$. Choosing $A=\alpha+\beta h_{33}$ and defining $a=\beta(h_{12}-h_{21})/A$, $b=\beta(h_{11}+h_{22})/A$, we get
\begin{align*}
I=&\log (\alpha+\beta h_{33})+\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\left(1+a\sin\phi+b\cos\phi\right)\,d\phi.
\end{align*}
Note that
\begin{align*}
a^2+b^2=&\frac{h_{11}^2+h_{22}^2+h_{21}^2+h_{12}^2+2(h_{11}h_{22}-h_{12}h_{21})}{(\alpha+\beta h_{33})^2}\beta^2.
\end{align*}
From the fact that the columns of $H$ have norm $1$ we get $h_{11}^2+h_{22}^2+h_{21}^2+h_{12}^2=2-h_{31}^2-h_{32}^2$, which in turn, using that the last row of $H$ also has norm $1$, equals $1+h_{33}^2$. Moreover, since the determinant of $H$ is equal to $1$, from the construction of the inverse we have that the $(3,3)$ entry of $H^{-1}$ is precisely $h_{11}h_{22}-h_{12}h_{21}$, and from the fact that $H^{-1}=H^T$ we deduce that this last expresion equals $h_{33}$. All in one, we have:
\begin{align*}
a^2+b^2=&\frac{1+h_{33}^2+2h_{33}}{(\alpha+\beta h_{33})^2}\beta^2\\
=&\frac{(1+h_{33})^2}{(\alpha+\beta h_{33})^2}\beta^2\leq1,
\end{align*}
%\begin{align*}
%a^2+b^2=&\frac{1+h_{33}^2+2h_{33}}{(3-h_{33})^2}=\frac{(1+h_{33})^2}{(3-h_{33})^2}\leq 1,
%\end{align*}
the last inequality from the fact that $|h_ {33}|\leq 1$ and $|\beta|\leq\alpha/3$. Hence, we can use \cite[(4.225,3)]{integrales} that yields
\begin{align*}
I=&\log (\alpha+\beta h_{33})+\log\frac{1+\sqrt{1-\frac{1+h_{33}^2+2h_{33}}{(\alpha+\beta h_{33})^2}\beta^2}}{2}\\
=&-\log2+\log\left(\alpha+\beta h_{33}+\sqrt{(\alpha+\beta h_{33})^2-(1+h_{33}^2+2h_{33})\beta^2}\right)\\
=&-\log2+\log\left(\alpha+\beta h_{33}+\sqrt{\alpha- \beta}\sqrt{\alpha+\beta+2\beta h_{33}}\right)\\
=&-\log2+\log\left(\frac12\left(\sqrt{\alpha-\beta}+\sqrt{\alpha+\beta+2\beta h_{33}}\right)^2\right)\\
=& -\log4+2\log\left(\sqrt{\alpha-\beta}+\sqrt{\alpha+\beta+2\beta h_{33}}\right).
\end{align*}
This finishes the proof, since $h_{33}=e_3^THe_3$.
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:twopoints}
Let $p,p'\in\s$ and let $O_j=H_pR(\phi_j+\phi)\in\so$, $O'_j=H_{p'}R(\phi_j+\phi')\in\so$ be two collections of $s$ equally spaced o.n. matrices with respective points $p,p'$. Assume moreover that $\phi,\phi'\in[0,2\pi)$ are chosen at random uniformly. The expected value of the crossed logarithmic energy is:
\[
\Ex\left(\sum_{i=1}^s\sum_{j=1}^s\log\left\|O_i-O'_j\right\|_F\right)=s^2\log\left(\sqrt2+\sqrt{1-\langle p,p'\rangle}\right)
\]
 \end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $S$ be the sum in the lemma, and note that
\begin{align*}
S=&\frac{1/2}{(2\pi)^2}\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\sum_{i=1}^s\sum_{j=1}^s\log\left\|H_pR(\phi_i+\phi)-H_{p'}R(\phi_j+\phi')\right\|_F^2\,d\phi\,d\phi'\\
=&\sum_{i,j=1}^s\frac{1/2}{(2\pi)^2}\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\left(6-2\tr\left(R(-\phi_j-\phi')H_{p'}^TH_pR(\phi_i+\phi)\right)\right)\,d\phi\,d\phi'\\
=&\sum_{i,j=1}^s\frac{1/2}{(2\pi)^2}\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\left(6-2\tr\left(H_{p'}^TH_pR(\phi_i-\phi_j+\phi-\phi')\right)\right)\,d\phi\,d\phi',\\
\end{align*}
the last by the circularity of the trace. From the periodicity of the definition of $R(\phi)$, for any fixed $\phi_i,\phi_j$ and $\phi'$, we can change the integration interval of $\phi$ from $[0,2\pi]$ to $[\phi_i+\phi_j+\phi',\phi_i+\phi_j+\phi'+2\pi]$, thus getting:
\begin{align*}
S=&\sum_{i,j=1}^s\frac{1/2}{(2\pi)^2}\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\left(6-2\tr\left(H_{p'}^TH_pR(\phi)\right)\right)\,d\phi\,d\phi',\\
=&\frac{s^2}{4\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\left(6-2\tr\left(H_{p'}^TH_pR(\phi)\right)\right)\,d\phi.\\
\end{align*}
From Lemma \ref{lem:an_integral} we conclude:
\begin{align*}
S=&s^2\log\left(\sqrt2+\sqrt{1-e_3^TH_{p'}^TH_pe_3}\right).\\
\end{align*}
Now, note that $e_3^TH_{p'}^TH_pe_3=p'^Tp= \langle p,p'\rangle$, which finiches the proof.
\end{proof}















\section{Collections of points in $\s$ and the associated energy in $\so$}\label{sec:colecciones}
With Theorem \ref{th:logenergy} in hand, we can now choose collections of points in $\s$ and compute the logarithmic energy of the associated collections of special orthogonal matrices. The expresion
\begin{equation}\label{eq:energiaesfera}
\sum_{i\neq i'}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}{2}}\right)
\end{equation}
in Theorem \ref{th:logenergy} can also be seen as some kind of energy of points in $\s$, although it does not explode to $\infty$ in any case. Let us start by computing the continuous energy (i.e. the value of the energy for random, uniformly chosen $p,p'\in\s$).
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:continuousesfera}
Let $p,p'\in\s$ be chosen uniformly and independently. Then, we have
\begin{align*}
\Ex_{p,p'\in\s}\left(\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p,p'\rangle}2}\right)\right)=\frac12.
\end{align*}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Using a simmetry argument to fix $p=e_3$ we have:
\begin{align*}
\Ex_{p,p'\in\s}\left(\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p,p'\rangle}2}\right)\right)=&
\Ex_{p\in\s}\left(\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p,e_3\rangle}{2}}\right)\right),
\end{align*}
Since the expected value only depends on $p_3$ we can change it to a univariate integral (see, for example, \cite[Lemma 1]{BeltranMarzoOrtega}) that can be easily computed using the change of variables $t=1-2u^2$:
\begin{align*}
\Ex_{p,p'\in\s}\left(\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p,p'\rangle}2}\right)\right)=&\frac12\int_{-1}^1\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-t}{2}}\right)\,dt\\
=&\frac12\int_0^14u\log(1+u)\,du\\
=&\frac12\left[2u-u^2-2(1-u^2)\log(1+u)\right]_0^1\\
=&\frac12,
\end{align*}
as claimed. 
\end{proof}
A natural strategy to search for points on $\s$ that maximize \eqref{eq:energiaesfera} (and hence, produce minimal values of the logarithmic energy on $\so$ from Theorem \ref{th:logenergy}) is to use collections of points that have quasioptimal values of general energies such as the logarithmic energy or the Riesz energies on the sphere. Such collections of points have been intensively studied, but theoretical bounds for the corresponding energies are known in just a few cases:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Random, independent, and identically distributed points in $\s$.
\item Zeros of random polynomials \cite{ArmentanoBeltranShub2011,delatorre2022expectedenergyzeroselliptic}: a polynomial of degree $r$ is generated at random, in such a way that the coefficient of $z^j$ is drawn from the complex Gaussian distribution with mean $0$ and variance $\binom{r}{j}$. Then, its $r$ complex zeros are computed and sent to the sphere by the stereographic projection.
\item Equal area partitions \cite{Leopardi}: the sphere is divided into $r$ equal area pieces with diameters of the order $O(1/\sqrt{r})$. Then a point is picked at random in each piece.
\item The spherical ensemble \cite{AlishahiZamani}: two random $r\times r$ matrices $A,B$ whose entries are drawn from the complex Gaussian distribution with mean $0$ and variance $1$ are generated. Then, the $r$ complex eigenvalues of $A^{-1}B$ are computed and they are sent to $\s$ by the stereographic projection.
\item The harmonic ensemble \cite{BeltranMarzoOrtega}: a determinantal point process given by the spherical harmonics of bounded degree. Expected values of energies, discrepancy and other quantities can be computed although in practice the actual generation of points is more involved.
\item The diamond ensemble \cite{BeltranEtayo2020}: a number of parallels is chosen in the sphere. In each of them, a number of equally spaced points is chosen with a random phase. One must carefully choose the height of the parallels and the number of points in each of them.
\item Zeros of random instances of the polynomial eigenvalue problem \cite{armentano2024logarithmicenergysolutionspolynomial}: a random instance of a polynomial eigenvalue problem with a certain distribution of coefficients is generated. The zeros are again sent to the sphere by the stereographic projection. This process generalizes the zeros of random polynomials and the random eigenvalues from the spherical ensemble.
\end{enumerate}
Note that all these collections of points are randomized in the sense that each construction depends on the choice of a number of random parameters. This has been to the date necessary if we demand that the (expected values of) energies can be computed analytically. Among all of them, the one that produces the best values for the expected logarithmic energy is the diamond ensemble, but in that case only the logarithmic energy can be computed. The least (provable) Riesz energy is attained by zeros of random polynomials. In this work we deal with the cases (I) to (V), thus including this last case of zeros of random polynomials. However, the computations for the diamond ensemble and the polynomial eigenvalue problem are much more involved and are left as an open problem.
\subsection{Uniformity of minimizers}
Before entering into each concrete choice of spherical points, we will prove that the kernel
%
\begin{equation*}
	K(x,y)=-\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\innerprod{x}{y}}2}\right),\qquad x,y\in\S^2,
\end{equation*}
%
is conditionally strictly positive definite. Then, from \cite[Theorem 6.2.1]{BorodachovHardinSaff2019}, the uniform measure on $\S^2$ is the unique minimizer for the $K$-energy, justifying the convenience of the different sequences of spherical points described in the introduction to this section.


We denote the \emph{normalized Gegenbauer polynomials} by $\GegenbauerNormalized{n}{d}$. They are orthogonal on $[-1,1]$ with respect to the weight
%
\begin{equation*}
	w_d(t)\defined \frac{\Gamma\bigl((d+1)/2\bigr)}{\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(d/2)}(1-t^2)^{d/2-1},
\end{equation*}
%
and they satisfy $\GegenbauerNormalized{n}{d}(1)=1$. They are related to the classical Gegenbauer polynomials $\Gegenbauer{n}{\lambda}$ by
%
\begin{equation*}
	\GegenbauerNormalized{n}{d}(t)=\frac{1}{\Gegenbauer{n}{(d-1)/2}(1)}\Gegenbauer{n}{(d-1)/2}(t)=\frac{\Gamma(d-1)\Gamma(n+1)}{\Gamma(d-1+n)}\Gegenbauer{n}{(d-1)/2}(t)
\end{equation*}
%
Given a function $f\in\LL{2}_{w_d}[-1,1]$, let $\hat{f}(n)$ denote the $n$th Fourier coefficient of $f$ with respect to the Gegenbauer polynomials $\GegenbauerNormalized{n}{d}$:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Gegenbauer-coefficients}
	\hat{f}(n)\defined \frac{\innerprod{f}{\GegenbauerNormalized{n}{d}}_{w_d}}{\norm{\GegenbauerNormalized{n}{d}}_{w_d}^2},\qquad n=0,1,2,\dotso.
\end{equation}
%
The series 
%
\begin{equation*}
	\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\hat{f}(n)\GegenbauerNormalized{n}{d}(t)
\end{equation*}
%
converges in $\LL{2}_{w_d}[-1,1]$.


\begin{lemma}[{\cite[Lemma 5.2.8]{BorodachovHardinSaff2019}}]
	Let $f\in C[-1,1]$, and let $\hat{f}(n)$ be given by \eqref{eq:Gegenbauer-coefficients}. If $\hat{f}(n)\geq 0$ for $n\geq 0$, then $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}f_n<\infty$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 5.2.9]{BorodachovHardinSaff2019}}]\label{thm:cpd-coefficients}
	Suppose $f\in C[-1,1]$. The kernel $K(x,y)\defined f(\innerprod{x}{y})$ is positive semidefinite on $\S^d$ if and only if the Gegenbauer coefficients $\hat{f}(n)$ defined by are nonnegative for all $n\geq 0$.
\end{theorem}


\begin{definition}
	A function $f\from I\to\R$ is \emph{absolutely monotone} on an interval $I$ if, for any $n\geq 0$ and $x\in I$, its $n$th derivative $f^{(n)}$ exists\footnote{If $x$ is an endpoint of $I$, then $f^{(n)}(x)$ means the appropriate one-sided derivative.} and is nonnegative. The function $f$ is \emph{strictly absolutely monotone} if, in addition, $f^{(n)}(x)>0$ for all $x$ in the interior of $I$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 5.2.14]{BorodachovHardinSaff2019}}]\label{thm:monotone-coefficients}
	If $f\from [-1,1]\to\R$ is absolutely monotone on $[-1,1]$ and $\hat{f}(n)$ is given by \eqref{eq:Gegenbauer-coefficients} for $n\geq 0$, then $\hat{f}(n)\geq 0$ for all $n\geq 0$ and the Gegenbauer expansion
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		f(t)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\hat{f}(n)\GegenbauerNormalized{n}{d}(t)
	\end{equation*}
	%
	converges uniformly for $t\in[-1,1]$, If $f$ is strictly absolutely monotone on $[-1,1]$, then $\hat{f}(n)>0$ for all $n\geq 0$.
\end{theorem}


\begin{theorem}
	Let $x,y\in\S^2$. The kernel
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		K(x,y)=-\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\innerprod{x}{y}}2}\right)
	\end{equation*}
	%
	is conditionally strictly positive definite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
	From \cref{thm:cpd-coefficients,thm:monotone-coefficients}, it suffices to check that $f(t)=-\log(1+\sqrt{1-t}/\sqrt{2})$ is strictly absolutely monotone on $[-1,1]$. To compute the $n$th derivative of $f$ we use the Faà di Bruno's formula, which generalizes the chain rule to higher derivatives. Consider the functions $g\from[0,\sqrt{2}]\to\R$ and $h\from[-1,1]\to\R$ given by
	%
	\begin{align*}
		g(x)&=-\log(1+x/\sqrt{2}),\\
		h(x)&=\sqrt{1-x}
	\end{align*}
	%
	Then, we have $f=g\composed h$. Using the Faà di Bruno's formula, we have
	%
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:nth-derivative-kernel}
		f^{(n)}(t)=\frac{\dd^n}{\dd t^n}g(h(t))
		=\sum_{k=1}^{n}g^{(k)}(h(t))
		B_{n,k}(h'(t),\dotsc,h^{(n-k+1)}(t)),
	\end{equation}
	%
	where 
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		B_{n,k}(h'(t),\dotsc,h^{(n-k+1)}(t))=\hspace{1cm}\sum_{\mathclap{\substack{j_1+\dotsb+j_{n-k+1}=k\\ j_1+2j_1+\dotsb+(n-k+1)j_{n-k+1}=n}}}
		\hspace{1cm}\frac{n!}{j_1!j_2!\dotsb j_{n-k+1}!}\prod_{\ell=1}^{n-k+1}\biggl(\frac{h^{(\ell)}(x)}{\ell!}\biggr)^{j_{\ell}},
	\end{equation*}
	%
	is a Bell polynomial. Note that
	%
	\begin{align*}
		g^{(k)}(x)&=(-1)^{k}(k-1)!(\sqrt{2}+x)^{-k},\\
		h^{(k)}(x)&=-\frac{(2k-3)!!}{2^k}(1-x)^{-(2k-1)/2}.
	\end{align*}
	%
	To check that all the summands in \eqref{eq:nth-derivative-kernel} are positive, note that, for each $k$, with $1\leq k\leq n$, we have
	%
	\begin{align*}
		\prod_{\ell=1}^{n-k+1}\biggl(\frac{h^{(\ell)}(x)}{\ell!}\biggr)^{j_{\ell}}&=\prod_{\ell=1}^{n-k+1}(-1)^{j_{\ell}}\Bigl(\frac{(2\ell-3)!!}{2^{\ell}\ell!}(1-x)^{-(2\ell-1)/2}\Bigr)^{j_{\ell}}\\
		&=(-1)^{j_{1}+\dotsb+j_{n-k+1}}\prod_{\ell=1}^{n-k+1}\Bigl(\frac{(2\ell-3)!!}{2^{\ell}\ell!}(1-x)^{-(2\ell-1)/2}\Bigr)^{j_{\ell}}\\
		&=(-1)^{k}\prod_{\ell=1}^{n-k+1}\Bigl(\frac{(2\ell-3)!!}{2^{\ell}\ell!}(1-x)^{-(2\ell-1)/2}\Bigr)^{j_{\ell}}.
	\end{align*}
	%
	Hence, we have
	%
	\begin{align*}
		f^{(n)}(t)&=n!\sum_{k=1}^{n}(k-1)!(\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{1-t})^{-k}\\
		&\times\sum_{\substack{j_1+\dotsb+j_{n-k+1}=k\\ j_1+2j_1+\dotsb+(n-k+1)j_{n-k+1}=n}} \prod_{\ell=1}^{n-k+1}\frac{1}{j_{\ell}!}\Bigl(\frac{(2\ell-3)!!}{2^{\ell}\ell!}(1-t)^{-(2\ell-1)/2}\Bigr)^{j_{\ell}}.
	\end{align*}
	%
	Since all the summands are positive, we conclude that $f^{(n)}(t)\geq 0$ for all $t\in[-1,1]$ and so $f$ is strictly absolutely monotone on $[-1,1]$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Point set (I): random uniform points in $\s$}
We start by assuming that $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ are chosen independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.) with respect to the uniform distribution in $\s$.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:iid}
Assume that $p_1,\ldots,p_r\in\s$ are i.i.d. in $\s$. The expected logarithmic energy of the corresponding collection of special orthogonal matrices associated to $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ and a positive integer $s$ is:
\[
E_{\mathrm{uniform}}=\kappa n^2-n\log s+\frac{n}2s+ \frac{n}2\log2,
\]
\end{proposition}
It is inmediate to see that, for fixed $n=rs$, the minimum of this quantity is attained when $s=2$. This gives us our first complete construction of $n=2r$ poinst in $\so$: {\em the collection of spherical orthogonal matrices associated to iid points $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ and $s=2$.} By comparison with \eqref{eq:minimo} we see that the logarithmic energy
\[
E_{\mathrm{uniform}}(\text{any $r$; $s=2$})=
\kappa n^2+O(n).
\]
coincides with the theoretical minimum up to first order.
\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:iid}]
% From Theorem \ref{th:logenergy}, the expected logarithmic energy of the corresponding collection of special orthogonal matrices associated to $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ is:
%\[
%-r\left(\frac{s(s-1)}{2}\log2+s\log s\right)-s^2\sum_{i\neq i'}\Ex\left(\log\left(\sqrt2+\sqrt{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}\right)\right),
%\]
%where the inner expected value is taken for i.i.d spherical points $p_i,p_{i'}$. From a simmetry argument, we inmediately see that
%\[
%E_{\mathrm{uniform}}=-r\left(\frac{s(s-1)}{2}\log2+s\log s\right)-s^2r(r-1)\Ex\left(\log\left(\sqrt2+\sqrt{1-p_3}\right)\right),
%\]
%where $p=(p_1,p_2,p_3)\in\s$ is uniformly distributed in $\s$. 
From Theorem \ref{th:logenergy} and Proposition \ref{prop:continuousesfera} we deduce that:
\begin{align*}
E_{\mathrm{uniform}}=&
-\frac{n^2}{2}\log2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s-\frac12s^2r(r-1)\\
=&
-\frac{n^2}{2}\log2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s-\frac12n(n-s).
\end{align*}
The proposition follows.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Point set (II): zeros of random polynomials}
We now assume that $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ are chosen as the stereographic projection of zeros of random polynomials as in \cite{ArmentanoBeltranShub2011}.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:zeros}
Assume that $p_1,\ldots,p_r\in\s$ are chosen as the stereographic projection of zeros of random polynomials as in \cite{ArmentanoBeltranShub2011}. The expected logarithmic energy of the corresponding collection of special orthogonal matrices associated to $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ and a positive integer $s$ is:
\[
E_{\mathrm{zeros}}=\kappa n^2-n\log s+\frac{n}2\log2-\sqrt{n}s^{3/2}J+o\left(\sqrt{n}s^{3/2}\right),
\]
where $J=-0.57878934\ldots$ is computed in Lemma \ref{lem:constanteJ}.

\end{proposition}
It is immediate to see that, for fixed $n=rs$, the minimum of this quantity is attained when $s=\left(\frac{4n}{9|J|}\right)^{1/3}$, or equivalently $s=\sqrt{\frac{4r}{9|J|}}$. Since this last number is not an integer, we take the integer part to produce our second complete construction of $n=r\left\lfloor \sqrt{\frac{4r}{9|J|}}\right\rfloor$ points in $\so$: {\em the collection of spherical orthogonal matrices associated to the stereographic projection of zeros of random polynomials as in \cite{ArmentanoBeltranShub2011} and $s=\left\lfloor \sqrt{\frac{4r}{9|J|}}\right\rfloor$.} It has logarithmic energy
\[
E_{\mathrm{zeros}}\left(\text{any $r$, }s=\left\lfloor \sqrt{\frac{4r}{9|J|}}\right\rfloor\right)= \kappa n^2-\frac{1}{3}n\log n+C_{\mathrm{zeros}}n +  o(n),
\]
where
\begin{equation}\label{eq:czeros}
C_{\mathrm{zeros}}=-\frac13\log\frac{4}{9|J|}-\frac{2\sqrt{|J|}}{3}=-0.4191502\ldots
\end{equation}
By comparison with \eqref{eq:minimo} we see that the logarithmic energy coincides with the theoretical minimum up to $O(n)$. The first few values of $n$ that can be realized for this construction are $n=2,     3,     4,     5,    12,    14,    16,    18,    20,    22,    36,    39,    42,    45,\ldots$


For the proof we follow \cite[Section 2]{delatorre2022expectedenergyzeroselliptic}. Let $\gamma_r(u)=\gamma_{r,1}(u)+\gamma_{r,2}(u)$ where
\begin{align*}
	\gamma_{r,1}(u)=&\frac{\left(1-\frac{ru^2}{(1+u^2)^{r}-1}\right)^2(1+u^2)^{r-2}}{(1+u^2)^r-1},\\
	\gamma_{r,2}(u)=&\frac{\left(1-\frac{ru^2(1+u^2)^{r-1}}{(1+u^2)^r-1}\right)^2}{(1+u^2)^r-1}.
\end{align*}
\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:zeros}]
Let
\[
I_r=\Ex\left(\sum_{i\neq i'}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}{2}}\right)\right),
\]
where $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ are the image under the inverse stereographic projection of the zeros of degree $r$ elliptic random polynomials. Then, from \cite[Section 2]{delatorre2022expectedenergyzeroselliptic}, we have
\begin{align*}
	\frac{I_r}{r^2}=2\int_0^\infty u\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\gamma_r(u)\,du.
\end{align*}
From Lemma \ref{lem:gamma}, the expression inside the integral is bounded above by $constant/(1+u^2)$, for some constant, that is an integrable function. One can thus apply Legendre's Dominated Convergence Theorem to interchange limit and integral and get
\begin{align*}
	\lim_{r\to\infty}\frac{I}{r^2}=&2\int_0^\infty u\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\lim_{r\to\infty}\gamma_r(u)\,du,
\end{align*}
as long as this last limit is defined. Now, note that
\[
\lim_{r\to\infty}\gamma_{r,1}(u)=\frac{1}{(1+u^2)^2},\quad \lim_{r\to\infty}\gamma_{r,2}(u)=0,
\]
and hence (using the change of variables $t=u/\sqrt{1+u^2}$) we deduce that
\begin{align}
	\lim_{r\to\infty}\frac{I}{r^2}=&2\int_0^\infty \frac{u}{(1+u^2)^2}\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\,du\label{eq:auxn}\\
	=&2\int_0^1t\log(1+t)\,dt\nonumber\\
	=&\frac12\nonumber.
\end{align}
We thus have $I_r=r^2/2+o(r^2)$ and in order to compute the next order term we consider:
\[
J_r=\frac{1}{\sqrt{r}}\left(I_r-\frac{r^2}{2}\right).
\]
Lemma \ref{lem:constanteJ} claims that there exists a constant $J=\lim_{r\to\infty}J_r$, which implies:
\begin{align*}
I_r=&\frac{r^2}{2}+\sqrt{r}{J_r}\\
=&\frac{r^2}{2}+\sqrt{r}J+o(\sqrt{r}).
\end{align*}
Finally, from Theorem \ref{th:logenergy} we then conclude that
\begin{align*}
E_{\mathrm{zeros}}=&-\frac{n^2}{2}\log2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s-s^2I_r\\
=&-\frac{n^2}{2}\log2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s-s^2\left(\frac{r^2}{2}+\sqrt{r}J+o(\sqrt{r})\right)\\
=&\kappa n^2\log2-n\log s+\frac{n}2\log2-s^2\sqrt{r}J+o\left(s^2\sqrt{r}\right)\\
=&\kappa n^2\log2-n\log s+\frac{n}2\log2-\sqrt{n}s^{3/2}J+o\left(\sqrt{n}s^{3/2}\right).
\end{align*}
The proposition follows.

\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:gamma}
	Let $r\geq2$. Then,
	\[
	\gamma_{r,1}(u)\leq 
	\begin{cases}
		11 ru^2&0<u<r^{-1/2}\\
		\frac{2}{(1+u^2)^2}&r^{-1/2}\leq u<\infty
	\end{cases}
	\qquad
	\gamma_{r,2}(u)\leq 
	\begin{cases}
		2 ru^2&0<u<r^{-1/2}\\
		\frac{8r^2u^4}{(1+u^2)^{r+2}}&r^{-1/2}\leq u<\infty
	\end{cases}
	\]
	Moreover,
		\[
	\gamma_r(u)\leq 
	\begin{cases}
		13 ru^2&0<u<r^{-1/2}\\
		\frac{34}{(1+u^2)^2}&r^{-1/2}\leq u<\infty
	\end{cases}
	\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	Taylor's theorem on $g(t)=(1+t)^r$ with Lagrange's remainder gives for some $\zeta\in(0,t)$:
	\[
	(1+t)^r=1+rt+\frac{r(r-1)(1+\zeta)^{r-2}}{2}.
	\]
	We thus have in the range $0<u<r^{-1/2}$:
	\begin{align*}
		(1+u^2)^r\leq&1+ru^2+\frac{r(r-1)(1+1/r)^{r-2}}{2}u^4\leq 1+ru^2+2r^2u^4,\\
		(1+u^2)^r\geq &1+ru^2+\frac{r(r-1)}2u^4\geq 1+ru^2+\frac{r^2}{4}u^4.
	\end{align*}
	(For the second inequality we have used the binomial expansion). We thus deduce:
	\begin{align*}
		\gamma_{r,1}(u)\leq &\frac{\left(1-\frac{ru^2}{ru^2+2r^2u^4}\right)^2e}{ru^2}\leq \frac{4eru^2}{(1+2ru^2)^2}\leq 11ru^2,\\
		\gamma_{r,2}(u)\leq &\frac{\left(1-\frac{ru^2(1+ru^2-u^2)}{ru^2+2r^2u^4}\right)^2}{ru^2} =
		\frac{u^2\,{\left(r+1\right)}^2}{r\,{\left(2\,r\,u^2+1\right)}^2}\leq 2ru^2.
	\end{align*}
	Now, in the range $r^{-1/2}<u<\infty$, using that $x\to x/(x-1)$ is a decreasing function and that $(1+u^2)^r\geq (1+1/r)^r\geq 2$ we have
	\[
	\frac{(1+u^2)^r}{(1+u^2)^r-1}\leq 2,
	\]
	from where we deduce
	\begin{align*}
		\gamma_{r,1}(u)\leq&\frac{1}{(1+u^2)^2}\frac{(1+u^2)^r}{(1+u^2)^r-1}\\\leq&\frac{2}{(1+u^2)^2},
	\end{align*}
	and
	\begin{align*}
		\gamma_{r,2}(u)\leq&\frac{r^2u^4}{(1+u^2)^2((1+u^2)^r-1)}\left(\frac{(1+u^2)^r}{(1+u^2)^r-1}\right)^2\\
		\leq&\frac{4r^2u^4}{(1+u^2)^2((1+u^2)^r-1)}\\
		\leq &\frac{8r^2u^4}{(1+u^2)^2(1+u^2)^r}.
	\end{align*}
	The lemma follows. For the last bound on $\gamma$, note that $\gamma=\gamma_{r,1}+\gamma_{r,2}$ and that
	\begin{align*}
	\frac{8r^2u^4}{(1+u^2)^{r+2}}\leq &\frac{8r^2u^4}{(1+u^2)^2(1+ru^2+r(r-1)u^4/2)}\\
	\leq &\frac{8r^2u^4}{(1+u^2)^2r^2u^4/4}\\
	=&\frac{32}{(1+u^2)^2}.
	\end{align*}
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:constanteJ}
	The following limit exists:
	\[
	\lim_{r\to\infty}r^{3/2}\left(2\int_0^\infty u\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\gamma_r(u)\,du-\frac{1}{2}\right),
	\]
	and its value is
	\[
	J=\int_0^\infty\sqrt{t}
	\frac{2\,e^{2\,t}-4\,e^t-4\,t\,e^{2\,t}+t^2\,e^t+t^2\,e^{2\,t}+4\,t\,e^t+2}{{\left(e^t-1\right)}^3}\,dt= -0.57878934\ldots
	\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $J$ be the quantity in the lemma. We first note that from the integral computed in \eqref{eq:auxn}, $J=J_1+J_2$ where
\begin{align*}
J_1=&\lim_{r\to\infty}\left(2\int_0^\infty r^{3/2}u\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\left(\gamma_{r,1}(u)-\frac{1}{(1+u^2)^2}\right)\,du\right),\\
J_2=&\lim_{r\to\infty}\left(2\int_0^\infty r^{3/2}u\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\gamma_{r,2}(u)\,du\right).
\end{align*}
We start by $J_2$. Let
\begin{align*}
	A_r=&\int_0^{1} r^{3/2}u\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\gamma_{r,2}(u)\,du\\
	B_r=&\int_1^\infty r^{3/2}u\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\gamma_{r,2}(u)\,du.
\end{align*}
The change of variables $s=u\sqrt r$ transforms
\begin{align*}
	A_r=&\int_0^{\sqrt{r}} r^{1/2}s\log\left(1+\frac{s/\sqrt{r}}{\sqrt{1+s^2/r}}\right)\gamma_{r,2}(s/\sqrt{r})\,ds.
\end{align*}
From Lemma \ref{lem:gamma}, the expression inside this last integral is bounded above by a constant independent of $r$ in the interval $s\in[0,1]$, and in the interval $[1,\infty)$ it is bounded above by
\[
\frac{8s^6}{(1+s^2)^{r+2}}\leq \frac{8s^6}{(1+s^2)^{4}},
\]
which is integrable in $[1,\infty)$. Hence, Lebesgue's Dominated Convergence Theorem applies yielding
\begin{align*}
\lim_{r\to\infty}A_r=&\int_0^\infty\lim_{r\to\infty}\left(r^{1/2}s\log\left(1+\frac{s/\sqrt{r}}{\sqrt{1+s^2/r}}\right)\gamma_{r,2}(s/\sqrt{r})\right)\,ds\\
=&\int_0^\infty s^2\frac{\left(e^{s^2}-1-s^2e^{s^2}\right)^2}{\left(e^{s^2}-1\right)^3}\,ds\\
=&\frac12\int_0^\infty\sqrt{t}\frac{\left(e^{t}-1-te^{t}\right)^2}{\left(e^{t}-1\right)^3}\,dt.
\end{align*}
 It is much easier to check that $B_r\to0$. Indeed, from Lemma \ref{lem:gamma}, the expresion inside the integral of $B_r$ is bounded above by
 \[
 \frac{8r^{7/2}u}{(1+u^2)^r}= \frac{8r^{7/2}}{(1+u^2)^{r-2}}\frac{u}{(1+u^2)^2}\leq\frac{C}{u^3}
 \]
 for some constant $C>0$ and $u\in[1,\infty)$. Since $C/u^3$ is integrable in $[1,\infty)$ we can again apply the Dominated Convergence Theorem that yields
\begin{align*}
 \lim_{r\to\infty}B_r=&\int_1^\infty u\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\lim_{r\to\infty}(r^{3/2}\gamma_{r,2}(u))\,du\\
 =&0.
\end{align*}
All in one, we have proved that
\[
J_2=\int_0^\infty\sqrt{t}\frac{\left(e^{t}-1-te^{t}\right)^2}{\left(e^{t}-1\right)^3}\,dt.
\]
Now we center our attention in $J_1$. The integral inside the definition of $J_1$ equals
\[
\int_0^\infty \frac{r^{3/2}u}{(1+u^2)^2}\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\left(\frac{\left(1-\frac{ru^2}{(1+u^2)^{r}-1}\right)^2(1+u^2)^{r}}{(1+u^2)^r-1}-1\right)\,du.
\]
Some simple algebraic manipulations give the alternative expression:
\[
\int_0^\infty \frac{r^{3/2}u}{(1+u^2)^2}\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\frac{1+(1+u^2)^{r}\left(\frac{r^2u^4}{((1+u^2)^{r}-1)^2} -\frac{2ru^2}{(1+u^2)^{r}-1}\right)}{(1+u^2)^r-1}\,du.
\]
As before, we divide it into two pieces
\begin{align*}
C_r=&\int_0^1 \frac{r^{3/2}u}{(1+u^2)^2}\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\frac{1+(1+u^2)^{r}\left(\frac{r^2u^4}{((1+u^2)^{r}-1)^2} -\frac{2ru^2}{(1+u^2)^{r}-1}\right)}{(1+u^2)^r-1}\,du,\\
D_r=&\int_1^\infty \frac{r^{3/2}u}{(1+u^2)^2}\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\frac{1+(1+u^2)^{r}\left(\frac{r^2u^4}{((1+u^2)^{r}-1)^2} -\frac{2ru^2}{(1+u^2)^{r}-1}\right)}{(1+u^2)^r-1}\,du.
\end{align*}
It is easy to see that $D_r\to0$ since in that interval the expression inside the integral is bounded above by
\[
Constant\cdot \frac{r^{3/2}u}{(1+u^2)^2}\cdot \frac{2ru^2}{(1+u^2)^{r}}\leq Constant\cdot \frac{r^{5/2}u^3}{(1+u^2)^{2+r}}\leq\frac{Constant}{u^2},
\]
where the constant can be chosen independent of $r$ and hence we can apply the Dominated Convergence Theorem to interchange limit with integral in $\lim_{r\to\infty}D_r$ which yields
\begin{align*}
\lim_{r\to\infty}D_r=&\int_1^\infty \frac{r^{3/2}u}{(1+u^2)^2}\log\left(1+\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+u^2}}\right)\cdot0\,du\\
=&0.
\end{align*}
It only remains to deal with $C_r$. As before, we perform the change of variables $s=u\sqrt r$ that yields
\[
C_r=\int_0^{\sqrt{r}} \frac{r^{1/2}s}{(1+s^2/r)^2}\log\left(1+\frac{s/\sqrt{r}}{\sqrt{1+s^2/r}}\right)\frac{1+(1+s^2/r)^{r}\left(\frac{s^4}{((1+s^2/r)^{r}-1)^2} -\frac{2s^2}{(1+s^2/r)^{r}-1}\right)}{(1+s^2/r)^r-1}\,ds.
\]
Elementary manipulations show that the expression inside this last integral is bounded above in the interval $[0,1]$ by
\[
s^2\frac{1+e\left(\frac{s^4}{((1+s^2/2)^{2}-1)^2} -\frac{2s^2}{e^{s^2}-1}\right)}{(1+s^2/2)^2-1}\leq Constant,
\]
while in the interval $[1,\infty]$, using that $(1+s^2/r)^r-1\geq (1+s^2/r)^r/4$  we can bound it above by
\[
Constant\cdot \frac{s^2}{(1+s^2/r)^{r}}\left(\frac{s^4}{(1+s^2/r)^{r}} -2s^2\right)\leq \frac{Constant\cdot s^6}{(1+s^2/4)^4}.
\]
All together, we have bounded the expression inside $C_r$ by a function which is integrable in $[0,\infty)$ which allows us to apply the Dominated Convergence Theorem. This yields:
\begin{align*}
\lim_{r\to\infty}C_r=&\int_0^\infty s^2\frac{1+e^{s^2}\left(\frac{s^4}{(e^{s^2}-1)^2}-\frac{2s^2}{e^{s^2}-1}\right)}{e^{s^2}-1}\,ds\\
=&\frac12\int_0^\infty \sqrt{t}\frac{1+e^{t}\left(\frac{t^2}{(e^{t}-1)^2}-\frac{2t}{e^{t}-1}\right)}{e^{t}-1}\,dt.
\end{align*}
Finally, we have proved that
\begin{align*}
J=&J_1+J_2\\
=&\lim_{r\to\infty}(2A_r+2B_r+2C_r+2D_r)\\
=&\int_0^\infty\sqrt{t}\frac{\left(e^{t}-1-te^{t}\right)^2}{\left(e^{t}-1\right)^3}\,dt + \int_0^\infty \sqrt{t}\frac{1+e^{t}\left(\frac{t^2}{(e^{t}-1)^2}-\frac{2t}{e^{t}-1}\right)}{e^{t}-1}\,dt\\
=&\int_0^\infty\sqrt{t}
\frac{2\,e^{2\,t}-4\,e^t-4\,t\,e^{2\,t}+t^2\,e^t+t^2\,e^{2\,t}+4\,t\,e^t+2}{{\left(e^t-1\right)}^3}\,dt.
\end{align*}
\end{proof}


\subsection{Point set (III): points coming from equal area partitions}
We now assume that $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ are chosen at random, each of them in one element of an equal area partition as in \cite{Leopardi}. That is to say, the sphere $\s$ is divided into $r$ open, pairwise disjoint subsets $B_1,\ldots,B_r$, all of them having relative area equal to $1/r$, and such that the union of all the borders of the $B_i$ is zero measure in $\s$, and that each $B_i$ is contained in some ball $\{q\in\s:\langle p_i,q\rangle\geq 1-C_0/r\}$, with $C_0$ some fixed constant. Then, $p_i$ is chosen randomly inside $B_i$ for all $i=1,\ldots,r$.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:eap}
Assume that $p_1,\ldots,p_r\in\s$ are chosen at random, each of them in one element of an equal area partition as in \cite{Leopardi}. The expected logarithmic energy of the corresponding collection of special orthogonal matrices associated to $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ and a positive integer $s$ satisfies:
\[
E_{\mathrm{EAP}}\leq\kappa n^2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s+Constant\cdot \sqrt{n}s^{3/2}+O(s^2).
\]
\end{proposition}
It is inmediate to see that, for fixed $n=rs$, the minimum of this quantity is attained when $s=\mathrm{Constant}\cdot n^{1/3}$, or equivalently $s=\mathrm{Constant}\cdot \sqrt{r}$. Choosing for example this last constant equal to $1$ and taking integer part, this gives us our third complete construction of $n$ points in $\so$, for a certain infinite sequence of values of $n$: {\em the collection of spherical orthogonal matrices associated to points $p_1,\ldots,p_{r}$ chosen at random, each of them in one element of an equal area partition as in \cite{Leopardi}, and $s=\lfloor\sqrt{r}\rfloor$}, which has expected energy
\[
\mathbb E_{\mathrm{EAP}}(\text{any $r$; $s=\lfloor\sqrt{r}\rfloor$})=\kappa n^2-\frac13n\log n+O(n).
\]
 By comparison with \eqref{eq:minimo} we see that the logarithmic energy coincides with the theoretical minimum up to second term. An explicit constant giving an upper bound for the term $O(n)$ can be estimated with some effort. The first few values of $n$ that can be realized for this construction are $n=2,3,8,10,12,14,16,27\ldots$
\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:eap}]
Let $i\in\{1,\ldots,r\}$ be fixed, and note that for any integrable function $f:\s\to\R$ we have (denoting by $E_{p\in B}$ the expected value for $p\in B$):
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i'\neq i}E_{p_{i'}\in B_{i'}}f(p_{i'})=&\frac{r}{\vol(\s)}\int_{p\in\cup_{i'\neq i}B_{i'}}f(p)\,dp\\
=&\frac{r}{\vol(\s)}\int_{p\in\s}f(p)\,dp-\frac{1}{\vol(B_i)}\int_{p\in B_{i}}f(p)\,dp.
\end{align*}
In particular, 
\begin{multline*}
\sum_{i'\neq i}E_{p_{i'}\in B_{i'}}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}2}\right)=\\\frac{r}{\vol(\s)}\int_{p\in\s}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p\rangle}2}\right)\,dp
-\frac{1}{\vol(B_i)}\int_{p\in B_i}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p\rangle}2}\right)\,dp.
\end{multline*}
The first of these two terms equals $r/2$ from Proposition \ref{prop:continuousesfera} and, since $B_i$ is contained in a ball $\{q\in\s:\langle p_i,q\rangle\geq 1-C_0/r\}$, the last term satisfies 
\[
\frac{1}{\vol(B_i)}\int_{p\in B_i}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p\rangle}{2}}\right)\leq
 \log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{C_0}{2r}}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{C_0}}{\sqrt{2r}}+O(1/r).
\]
We hence have
\[
\sum_{i'\neq i}E_{p_{i'}\in B_{i'}}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p\rangle}{2}}\right)\geq
\frac{r}2-\frac{\sqrt{C_0}}{\sqrt{2r}}+O(1/r).
\]
Since this is valid for all $i$, we conclude that the double sum satisfies:
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i'\neq i}E_{p_{i'}\in B_{i'}}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}2}\right)\geq&\frac{r^2}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{C_0r}}{\sqrt{2}}+O(1).
\end{align*}
Putting all together, from Theorem \ref{th:logenergy}, the expected logarithmic energy of the corresponding collection of special orthogonal matrices associated to $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ satisfies:
\begin{align*}
E_{\mathrm{EAP}}\leq&-\frac{n^2}{2}\log2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s-s^2\left(\frac{r^2}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{C_0r}}{\sqrt{2}}+O(1)\right)\\
=&-\frac{1+\log2}{2}n^2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s+\frac{\sqrt{C_0}}{\sqrt{2}}\sqrt{n}s^{3/2}+O(s^2),
\end{align*}
and the proposition follows.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Point set (IV): the spherical ensemble}
We now assume that $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ are chosen from the spherical ensemble as in \cite{AlishahiZamani}. That is to say, we generate at random two $r\times r$ matrices $A,B$ with Gaussian entries following the complex $\mathcal N_\mathbb{C}(0,1)$ distribution, then we compute the $r$ eigenvalues of $A^{-1}B$ and we send them to $\s$ using the stereographic projection.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:sphericalensemble}
Assume that $p_1,\ldots,p_r\in\s$ are chosen from the spherical ensemble as in \cite{AlishahiZamani}. The expected logarithmic energy of the corresponding collection of special orthogonal matrices associated to $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ and a positive integer $s$ is:
\begin{align*}
E_{\mathrm{sph}}=&\kappa n^2-n\log  s+\frac{\log2}{2}n+\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2}\frac{\Gamma(n/s)}{\Gamma(n/s+1/2)}s n - \frac{s^2}{2}\\
=&\kappa n^2-n\log  s+\frac{\log2}{2}n+\frac{\sqrt  \pi s^{3/2}}{2}\sqrt n +  o(s^2)+o(s^{3/2}\sqrt n),
\end{align*}
\end{proposition}

It is easy to see that, for fixed $n=rs$, the minimum of the higher order terms in this quantity is attained when $s=\left(\frac{16n}{9\pi}\right)^{1/3}$ or equivalently $s=\sqrt{\frac{16r}{9\pi}}$. Since this last number is not an integer, we take the integer part to produce our third complete construction of $n$ points in $\so$, for a certain infinite sequence of values of $n$: {\em the collection of spherical orthogonal matrices associated to points $p_1,\ldots,p_r$, chosen from the spherical ensemble as in \cite{AlishahiZamani}, and $s=\left\lfloor\sqrt{\frac{16r}{9\pi}}\right\rfloor$}. It has logarithmic energy
\[
E_{\mathrm{sph}}\left(\text{any $r$, }s=\left\lfloor\sqrt{\frac{16r}{9\pi}}\right\rfloor\right)= \kappa n^2-\frac{1}{3}n\log n+C_{\mathrm{sph}}n +  o(n),
\]
where
\[
C_{\mathrm{sph}}=-\frac{5}{6}\log2+\frac{2}{3}\log3+\frac{1}{3}\log\pi+\frac{2}{3}=1.203028\ldots.
\]
 By comparison with \eqref{eq:minimo} we see that the logarithmic energy coincides with the theoretical minimum up to $O(n)$. The first few values of $n$ that can be realized for this construction are $n=2,3,4,5,6,7,16,18,20\ldots$

\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:sphericalensemble}]
Following \cite[Eqn. (4.2)]{AlishahiZamani}, for any measurable function \(\phi:\s\times\s\to[0,+\infty)\), 
\[
E\left(\sum_{i\neq i'}\phi(p_i,p_{i'})\right)=\frac{r^2}{(4\pi)^2}\int_{p,q\in \s\times\s}\phi(p,q)\left(1-\left(1-\frac{\|p-q\|^2}{4}\right)^{r-1}\right)dp\,dq.
\]
If $\phi(p,q)=f(\langle p,q\rangle)$ then by a symmetry argument we can fix $q=e_3$ and substituting $\|p-e_3\|^2=2-2p_3$ we get:
\[
E\left(\sum_{i\neq i'}\phi(p_i,p_{i'})\right)=\frac{r^2}{4\pi}\int_{p\in \s}f(p_3)\left(1-\left(\frac{1+p_3}{2}\right)^{r-1}\right)dp,
\]
We thus have:
\begin{multline*}
\sum_{i\neq i'}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}{2}}\right)=\\\frac{r^2}{4\pi}\int_{p\in \s}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-p_3}{2}}\right)\left(1-\left(\frac{1+p_3}{2}\right)^{r-1}\right)dp.
\end{multline*}
As in the proof of Proposition  \ref{prop:continuousesfera}, we can write the last expresion to an integral depending only on $t\in[-1,1]$. If we additionally change variables to $t=1-2u$ and we integrate by parts we get:
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i\neq i'}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}{2}}\right)=&\frac{r^2}{2}\int_{-1}^1\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-t}{2}}\right)\left(1-\left(\frac{1+t}{2}\right)^{r-1}\right)dt\\
=&r^2\int_{0}^1\log\left(1+\sqrt{u}\right)\left(1-\left(1-u\right)^{r-1}\right)du\\
&=r^2\left(\log2-\frac12\int_0^1\frac{u+\frac{1}{r}(1-u)^r}{u+\sqrt{u}}du\right)\\
&=r^2\left(\log2-\int_0^1\frac{x^2+\frac{1}{r}(1-x^2)^r}{1+x}dx\right)\\
&=r^2\left(\frac12-\frac{1}{r}\int_0^1 (1-x)(1-x^2)^{r-1}dx\right)\\
&=\frac{r^2}{2}-r\int_0^1 (1-x^2)^{r-1}dx+r\int_0^1 x(1-x^2)^{r-1}dx\\
&=\frac{r^2}{2}-\frac{r}{2}B\left(\frac12,r\right)+\frac{r}{2}B\left(1,r\right)\\
&=\frac{r^2}{2}-\frac{\sqrt \pi}{2}\frac{r\Gamma(r)}{\Gamma(r+1/2)}+\frac{1}{2},
\end{align*}
where \(B(x,y)\) is the beta function of \(x,y\in\C\) and we have used \cite[8.380.1]{integrales}.

Putting all this together with Theorem \ref{th:logenergy}, the expected logarithmic energy for the spherical ensemble in \(\so\) is equal to
\begin{align*}
-\frac{n^2}{2}\log2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s-s^2\left(\frac{r^2}{2}-\frac{\sqrt \pi}{2}\frac{r\Gamma(r)}{\Gamma(r+1/2)}+\frac{1}{2}\right).
\end{align*}
The proposition follows from this last equation rearranging terms. The asymptotics is a direct consecuence of Stirling's approximation for Euler's Gamma function.
\end{proof}

