\subsection{Point set (V): the Harmonic ensemble}
The Harmonic Ensemble from \cite{BeltranMarzoOrtega} defines, for any positive integer $r=(L+1)^2$ which is a perfect square, a random collection of points $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ with the property that 
\begin{multline*}
\Ex\left(\sum_{i\neq i'}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}{2}}\right)\right)=\\\frac{1}{16\pi^2}\int_{p,p'\in\s}H_L(p,p')\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p,p'\rangle}2}\right)\,dp\,dp',
\end{multline*}
where $H_L(p,p')$ is defined from the projection kernel $K_L(p,p')$ onto the subspace spanned by spherical harmonics of degree at most $L$, which is a subspace of $H^2(\s)$ of dimension $r=(L+1)^2$, by the following formula:
\[
H_L(p,p')=K_L(p,p)K_L(p',p')-|K_L(p,p')|^2.
\]
This is essentially the defining property of determinantal point processes, see \cite[Proposition 1]{BeltranMarzoOrtega} for a detailed introduction and examples of computations of different energies of spherical points using this approach.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:harmonic}
Assume that $p_1,\ldots,p_r\in\s$ are drawn from the harmonic ensemble with $r=(L+1)^2$ in $\s$. The expected logarithmic energy of the corresponding collection of special orthogonal matrices associated to $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ and a positive integer $s$ is:
\[
E_{\mathrm{\s-harmonic}}=\kappa n^2-n\log s+\frac{n}{2}\log2+g(n,s),
\]
where $g(n,s)$ is some function such that
	%
	\begin{equation*}
		Cn^{1/2}s^{3/2}\log(n/s)\leq g(n,s)\leq C'n^{1/2}s^{3/2}\log(n/s),
	\end{equation*}
for some positive constants $C<C'$.
\end{proposition}

It is inmediate to see that, for fixed $n=rs$, the minimum of this quantity is attained when $s=o(n)$. This allows us to change $\log(n/s)$ by $\log n$ in the bounds for $g(n,s)$ and then optimizing in $s$ we note that the optimal value is attained when $s=cn^{1/3}/(\log n)^{2/3}$ for some constant $c$, or equivalently $s=\sqrt{r}/\log r$? This gives us our first complete construction of $n=r\lfloor \sqrt{r}/\log r\rfloor$ poinst in $\so$: {\em the collection of spherical orthogonal matrices associated to $r=(L+1)^2$ points $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ drawn from the harmonic ensemble and $s=\lfloor \sqrt{r}/\log r\rfloor$.} It satisfies $s=O(n^{1/3}/(\log n) ^{2/3})$ and has logarithmic energy
\[
E_{\mathrm{\s-harmonic}}\left(\text{ any } r,s=\lfloor \sqrt{r}/log r\rfloor\right)=\kappa n^2-\frac13n\log s+O(n\log\log n).
\]
 By comparison with \eqref{eq:minimo} we see that the logarithmic energy coincides with the theoretical minimum up to second order, but it does not get to produce an $O(n)$ term. The first few values of $n$ that can be realized for this construction can be computed just taking $r=2,3,\ldots$, the corresponding $s$ and computing $n=rs$. This yields: $n=4,3,4,5,6,7,8\ldots,72,73,74,150,152,154,156\ldots$ 

\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:harmonic}]
It is known that $K_L$ can be written in terms of the Jacobi polynomial $P_L^{(1,0)}$ as follows, see \cite[Section 1.3]{BeltranMarzoOrtega}:
\[
K_L(p,p')=(L+1)P_L^{(1,0)}(\langle p,p'\rangle)
\]
We hence have:
\begin{align*}
H_L(p,p')=&(L+1)^2(P_L^{(1,0)}(1)^2-P_L^{(1,0)}(\langle p,p'\rangle)^2)\\
=&r^2\left(1-\frac{1}{(L+1)^2}P_L^{(1,0)}(\langle p,p'\rangle)^2\right).\\
\end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{multline}
\Ex\left(\sum_{i\neq i'}\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle p_i,p_{i'}\rangle}{2}}\right)\right)=\label{eq:auxiliar}\\\frac{r^2}{2}\int_{-1}^1\left(1-\frac{1}{(L+1)^2}P_L^{(1,0)}(t)^2\right)\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-t}2}\right)\,dt.
\end{multline}
Using the computations in the proof of Proposition  \ref{prop:continuousesfera} we can rewrite this as
\[
\frac{r^2}{2}-\frac{r}{2}\int_{-1}^1P_L^{(1,0)}(t)^2\log\left(1+\sqrt{\frac{1-t}2}\right)\,dt.
\]
The change of variables $t=\cos\theta$ transforms the previous integral into:
\[
\int_{0}^{\pi}\Jacobi{L}{1}{0}(\cos\theta)^2\log\bigl(1+\sin(\theta/2)\bigr)\sin\theta\dd \theta.
\]
This integral is estimated in \cref{integralsjacobi}, proving that, for some positive constants $C,C'$ we have (recall: $r=(L+1)^2$):
\[
\frac{r^2}{2}-C\sqrt{r}\log r\leq \eqref{eq:auxiliar}\leq\frac{r^2}{2}-C'\sqrt{r}\log r.
\]
From Theorem \ref{th:logenergy} and using $n=rs$ we deduce that
\begin{align*}
E_{\mathrm{\s-harmonic}}\leq&-\frac{n^2}{2}\log2+\frac{n}2\log2-n\log s-s^2\left(\frac{r^2}{2}-C\sqrt{r}\log r\right)\\
=&\kappa n^2-n\log s+\frac{n}{2}\log 2+C\sqrt{n}s^{3/2}\log\frac{n}{s},
\end{align*}
with the reverse inequality (and changing $C$ to $C'$) for the lower bound. The proposition follows.
\end{proof}
