\section*{Introduction}
Minimally invasive surgeries have reshaped healthcare by reducing patient trauma, shortening recovery time, and improving clinical outcomes. Among these techniques, transluminal interventions --which access internal anatomy through natural orifices and small incisions-- are significantly less invasive than alternative surgeries such as open surgery, enabling intricate procedures at difficult-to-reach anatomical targets\cite{wagh2007,Omisore2022,reviewSoroMIS}. These interventions typically follow a two-phase structure: first, the instruments navigate along anatomical pathways toward the target and, second, once the instrument is positioned, surgical tasks are performed at the target site. Because tissue interaction differs markedly between these phases, the design of transluminal instruments must accommodate contrasting functional requirements. In particular, friction must be low during insertion for smooth navigation and minimal tissue damage, yet needs to remain high when the instrument is at the target location for stability. 

