\begin{abstract}

The mechanism by which odorants are recognized by olfactory receptors remains primarily unresolved. While charge transport is believed to play a significant role, its precise nature is still unclear. Here, we present a novel perspective by exploring the interplay between the intrinsic energy scales of odorant molecules and the gap states that facilitate intermolecular charge transport. We find that odorants act as weak tunneling conductors mainly because of the limited magnitude of electronic coupling. This behavior is further connected to electron-phonon interactions and reorganization energy, suggesting that physically meaningful values for these parameters emerge only in the deep off-resonant tunneling regime. These findings complement the swipe card model of olfaction, in which an odorant needs both the right shape to bind to a receptor and the correct vibrational frequency to trigger signal transduction. Moreover, they reveal that the underlying mechanisms are much more complex than previously assumed.

%{The way how the odorants are recognized by the olfactory receptors remains largely elusive. The charge transport may play an important role, yet its exact mechanism is somewhat unknown. Here novel canonical perspective is presented by examining interplay between intrinsic energy scales of odorants in terms of the gap states that mediate intermolecular charge transport. The odorants are found to be weak tunnelling conductors, not primarily due to their large energy gaps, but rather owing to the magnitude of the associated electronic coupling. This observation is linked here with the behavior of the electron-phonon interaction and reorganization energy, indicating that physically relevant values of these parameters can be obtained only for the deep off-resonant tunnelling regime. As such, the findings appears to complement the swipe card model of olfaction, but shows that initial argumentation behind it is more complex than previously anticipated.}

%From the global prespective, the conducted analysis provides also instructive insights into quantum transport processes in biological systems.

\end{abstract}

