\subsection{The ruler and compass construction}
In ancient Greek, the geometric objects were constructed using only a single unmarked ruler and the compass.  A wide range of constructions is possible with only a single unmarked ruler and the compass. We can divide a line segment into arbitrary many equal parts.  All angles of multiple of three can be constructed.  Pentagon and $17$-gon can be built with little more effort by just using a ruler and compass.  But the Greeks wanted to go further and wanted to perform: Duplication of the cube; trisection of the angle; quadrature of the circle. But they could not find these constructions.  It is not surprising since later (using abstract algebra) it is proved that these constructions are not possible. 

Let us state this more formally.  Let $P_0$ be the set of points in the Euclidean plane, and the following two operations using rules and compass are allowed:
\begin{itemize}
\item Drawing straight line through any two points of $P_0$.
\item Drawing a circle with the center is from $P_0$, and whose radius is equal to the distance between some pair of points in $P_0$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition} A point of the plane is called \emph{one-step constructible} from $P_0$ if it is the point of intersection of any two distinct lines or circle, drawn using the above operations from $P_0$.  A point or number is \emph{constructible from $P_0$} if there is a finite sequence;
               $$    r_1,\ldots, r_n=r$$ 
of points of $\mathbb{R}^2$ such that for each $i=1,...,n$ the points $r_i$ is constructible. 
\end{definition}



