The following is the necessary condition for a point to be constructible. We say a polynomial $f$ is irreducible if $f$ can not be written as a product of two non-constant polynomials.  That is if $f=gh$, where $g$ and $h$ are polynomials, then either $g$ is constant or $h$ is constant.  

\begin{theorem} If a point $r=(x,y)$ in $\mathbb{R}^2$ is constructible from a subset $P_0$ of $\mathbb{R}^2$, then $x$ and $y$ are a root of an irreducible polynomial of the degrees powers of $2$ with coefficients from the co-ordinates of points of $P_0$, or there addition and multiplication. 
\end{theorem}

\begin{example} The regular pentagon is constructible.  So $(\cos 2\pi/5, \sin 2\pi/5)$ is constructible.  If you notice $\cos 2\pi/5$ is a root of an irreducible polynomial  $x^2+x+1$, which is of degree $2$.  But the angle $\pi/3$ cannot be trisected using ruler-and-compass.  The above statement follows from the fact that $\cos \pi/9$ is a root of an irreducible polynomial $x^3-3x-1$ of degree $3$, which is not a power of $2$.   In the same way, we can prove that the three constructions which are listed above are not possible.
\end{example} 
