\section{Conclusion}
This article investigates the fundamental question of whether QPs can be solved with \textit{data-independent} $O(n^3)$ on par with direct methods, such as Cholesky, LU, and QR factorizations for solving linear systems. To this end, we for the first time propose an \textit{implementable} Box-QP algorithm with $O(n^3)$ time complexity. We demonstrate that Box-QPs have broad applicability, encompassing general strictly convex QPs, Lasso problems, and SVMs. Regarding the proposed Box-QP algorithm, we derive the explicit equation of the upper bound of its required number of iterations and the number of rank-1 updates, thereby being able to offer an execution time certificate for real-time QP-based applications. Moreover, the proposed Box-QP algorithm is \textit{simple to implement} and \textit{matrix-free} in the iterative procedures, making it suitable for embedded deployment.

%\textbf{Limitations and future work:} The theoretical bound for the time complexity currently remains too large, leading to low computational efficiency in practice. Deriving a significantly tighter certified $O(n^3)$ time complexity is the next crucial step toward outperforming state-of-the-art, yet heuristic, IPM algorithms, such as the Mehrotra predictor-corrector IPM \cite{mehrotra1992implementation}, which serves as the foundation for most IPM-based software.
