% These distributive properties are used to reduce the optimized parameters of the neural network and enable the one-to-one mapping between the parameters and importance weights of the inferred explanations. 

\section{Results}

We perform a series of empirical experiments to compare our method to relevant baselines for robotic tasks. We consider three baselines for inference of wSTL explanations, each adapted to model our normalized wSTL structures (given by \Cref{eqn:nn_templ}):
% Clarify what we want to compare and evaluate in terms of the baselines and ablation studies
\begin{outline}
    \1 \emph{NN-TLI} \cite{li_learning_2023}: this method implements a greedy pruning strategy that independently removes network weights that do not affect classification accuracy after optimization. 
    We removed their weight-rounding process (to convert to STL) to consider the structure in \Cref{eqn:nn_templ} and used the aggregation functions given in \Cref{eqn:agg}, since our empirical results showed more training stability with those functions.
    % \1 A \emph{greedy} pruning strategy proposed by \cite{li_learning_2023}, which independently removes individual network weights that do not affect classification accuracy post-optimization. 
    % \1 A \emph{top-5} pruning strategy used by \cite{yan_stone_2021,yan_neuro-symbolic_2022}, which retains the 5 largest network weights after training.
    \1 \emph{STONE (top-3)} \cite{yan_stone_2021}: this method retains the 3 largest network weights after training.
    \1 \emph{STONE (top-5)} \cite{yan_stone_2021,yan_neuro-symbolic_2022}: this method retains the 5 largest network weights after training.
\end{outline}
All baselines were implemented using the same neural network architecture as \tlnet to ensure a consistent basis for comparison.
For each policy we explain, we collected 500 positive trajectories using the trained policy and 500 negative trajectories using a random-walk policy. 
Each dataset was split 80\% and 20\% for training and evaluation, respectively.
