\subsubsection{Benefits and Limitations of \tlnet}
\Cref{tab:baseline_qual} presents the most frequent explanation obtained by each method for each scenario. 
Across all scenarios, \tlnet consistently inferred both task and constraint clauses, while also producing concise and relatively easy-to-understand explanations.
% apability to Exclude Redundant Predicates
% The inferred explanations further highlight \tlnet's ability to effectively filter redundant predicates across all scenarios. 
% \todo{update} 
% For example, in the Robot Navigation scenario, the inferred explanation from \tlnet was ``\textit{With importance 0.5, eventually the robot reaches the goal; with importance 0.5, globally, it avoids the chaser.}'' 
% % Unnecessary predicates, such as $\psi_\mathrm{hc}$ and $\psi_\mathrm{hv}$, were excluded, confirming \tlnet’s ability to eliminate irrelevant predicates and generate concise explanations.
% All the unnecessary predicates, such as $\psi_\mathrm{hc}$ and $\psi_\mathrm{hv}$, were excluded, confirming \textsc{TLNet}’s ability to eliminate irrelevant predicates and generate concise explanations.
In comparison, NN-TLI typically yielded verbose explanations that are difficult to understand, while STONE often captured only one temporal clause, resulting in incomplete explanations.
% This structural incompleteness stems from the fact that top-$k$ pruning lacks assurances on preserving clause-level composition, despite yielding concise formulas with high classification accuracy.

% Why our explanations are reasonable
% Our method produced well-structured explanations across environments. 
The explanations produced by \tlnet are also qualitatively reasonable (and often correct).
For example, for all CtF scenarios, \tlnet correctly inferred the task clause as $\psi_\mathrm{ba,rf}$ (\textit{``blue agent captures the red flag''})---no other method correctly inferred this task for every scenario.
The inferred CtF constraints are also syntactically reasonable.
For example, in the Capture scenario, the blue agent (global) constraint includes \textit{``red agent not on the blue flag''} because 
the capture red policy only targets the blue flag, which required the blue agent to learn to prevent the red agent from reaching the blue flag.
Other scenarios, such as Fight and Patrol, did not include this predicate in the inferred constraint because those red policies do not target the blue flag.
In comparison, the CtF constraints inferred by NN-TLI are generally too verbose to understand, while STONE did not infer any constraints.

For Fetch Push, \tlnet also correctly inferred the task clause as $\psi_\mathrm{bt}$ (\textit{``block reaches the target''}), which no other method correctly inferred.
While \tlnet inferred part of the expected constraint ($\psi_\mathrm{gb}, \psi_\mathrm{bt}$), it missed an expected constraint $\neg\psi_\mathrm{go}$ (``grip does not touch the obstacle''), likely due to insufficient occurrences of the grip touching the obstacle in the sampled data.
% This limitation arises partly from the binary classification formulation and passive negative trajectory sampling strategy (e.g., random-walk). 
Future research could mitigate this issue by introducing more intelligent sampling strategies for generating the negative dataset (e.g., adversarial or targeted trajectory sampling).
NN-TLI also inferred part of the expected constraint, but also missed the $\neg\psi_\mathrm{go}$ constraint.
Meanwhile, both variations of STONE inferred an incorrect constraint of $\psi_\mathrm{bt}
$ (\textit{``block reaches the target''}), since this is actually the task and not possible to always satisfy.

For Robot Navigation, all methods correctly inferred the task clause but \tlnet was the only one to infer the correct constraint clause as $\neg\psi_\mathrm{ec}$ (\textit{``the robot does not hit the chaser''}).

% Baseline comparison by quantitative evaluation
As shown in \Cref{tab:baseline_quant}, \tlnet also outperformed all baselines in conciseness, consistency, and strictness, with marginal differences in classification accuracy. 
Across all scenarios, \tlnet achieved up to 1.9$\times$ higher conciseness, 2.6$\times$ higher consistency, and 2.7$\times$ higher strictness. 
% The lower classification accuracies seen by all methods in the CtF Mixture scenario can be attributed to the suboptimality of the learned policy, which reduced the separability between positive and negative trajectories.
These results support the qualitative findings above, validating the explainability of our method’s outputs and the use of these explainability metrics.


% Usefulness of Weights in Inferred Explanations
\subsubsection{Benefit of Including Weights}
We now demonstrate the benefit of including weights within inferred explanations (e.g., using wSTL rather than STL \cite{li_learning_2023} or LTL \cite{gaglione_learning_2021,bombara_offline_2021,aasi_classification_2022}) by examining how inferred weights differ across CtF scenarios.
% Across CtF scenarios, \tlnet consistently inferred both task ($\psi_\mathrm{ba,rf}$) and constraint clauses with meaningful weight distributions, demonstrating the importance and usefulness of weights for nuanced interpretation of policies. 
Consider the Capture and Mixture explanations in \Cref{tab:baseline_qual}.
% hows the critical subtleties in agent strategies across different environmental dynamics.
Both capture and mixture red policies target the blue flag, but the capture policy is more aggressive in doing so.
The blue agent, in response, learns slightly different policies: a more defensive policy in Capture, prioritizing the protection of its flag, and a more aggressive policy in Mixture, focusing more on capturing the red flag.
This policy difference is reflected in the weights in the constraint clause,
% relative to the weights on $\psi_\mathrm{ba,rf}$ in the task and constraint clauses.
% where $\neg\psi_\mathrm{ra,bf}$ predicate receives a higher weight (0.70) in the Capture explanation to emphasize the priority of the constraint over the task, and the weight drops to 0.56 for Mixture because task completion is relatively prioritized.
where the $\neg\psi_\mathrm{ra,bf}$ predicate receives a higher weight in the Capture explanation (0.70) than in the Mixture explanation (0.56), due to the Capture policy's prioritization of flag protection.
Conversely, the Mixture policy shows a higher weight for the $\psi_\mathrm{ba,rf}$ predicate (0.44) than the Capture policy (0.30), due to the Mixture policy's prioritization of flag capture.
Note that $\psi_\mathrm{ba,rf}$ appears in both the task and constraint clauses in these explanations. 
This duplication results from our design choice to fix equal weights (0.5) on the task and constraint clauses, which forces \tlnet to express prioritization between the task and constraint by including relevant task predicates in the constraint clause.
% These weights are relative importance to the task
% These relative weightings offer insight into agent behaviors that are not captured in binary STL/LTL formulations.
% ---0.70 for Capture and 0.56 for Mixture---to emphasize the priority of the constraint over the task and vice versa.
% In particular, the predicate $\psi_\mathrm{ba,rf}$ appeared in both the task and constraint clauses in both scenarios. 
% This duplication results from our design choice to fix equal weights on the task and constraint clauses while allowing \tlnet to express priority through internal predicate weights.
Although one could alternatively include the task and constraints weights within the optimized parameters, we found that doing so introduced training instability.
% Thus, our current formulation balances explainability and stability. 

Now, consider the Capture and Capture 0 scenarios, which differ in the reward to kill the red agent (0.5 vs. 0).
While one might naively expect this difference to produce different policy explanations, it actually should not change the optimal blue agent policies because, to win the game, the blue agent should first defend its flag (by killing the red agent), since the red agent uses the capture policy.
We see that \tlnet successfully captured this nuance by inferring constraints with nearly identical weights,
% : $\mathcal{G}_I^{\bar{w}}(0.30\psi_\mathrm{ba,rf} \vee 0.70\neg\psi_\mathrm{ra,bf})$ and $\mathcal{G}_I^{\bar{w}}(0.31\psi_\mathrm{ba,rf} \vee 0.69\neg\psi_\mathrm{ra,bf})$,
reflecting its robustness to minor reward differences that still lead to similar policies.
NN-TLI and STONE did not infer similar constraints across these scenarios.
More broadly, this result also demonstrates the importance of inferring explanations from actual observed behaviors of RL agents rather than simply looking at their specified reward function.

