% Contribution of Simplification Components
\subsubsection{Importance of \tlnet Components}
Finally, the ablation study results shown in \Cref{tab:abl_ctf} show that filtering, regularization, and pruning are all important to the performance of \tlnet. 
Removing predicate filtering notably reduced conciseness (by 27.2\%) and consistency (by 46.1\%). 
Removing regularization decreased conciseness (by 2.94\%) and significanbly reduced consistency (by 28.3\%) with marginal accuracy improvement.
% Regularization improved conciseness (by 3.0\%) and significantly boosted consistency (by 39.4\%) with only marginal accuracy trade-offs. 
% The pruning step proved the most impactful, substantially improving conciseness---nearly tripling it---demonstrating that all simplification steps collectively enhance explainability without sacrificing classification performance.
Finally, removing the pruning step nearly tripled conciseness and halved strictness.

% Overall, \tlnet generated meaningful, concise, and robust explanations, especially in environments with diverse, high-signal dynamics, and demonstrated clear paths for addressing limitations in more challenging scenarios.



\section{Conclusion}
We introduce a neuro-symbolic framework, based on the \tlnet architecture, for generating wSTL explanations of robot policies.
% , addressing limitations of trajectory classification-based methods. 
Our approach generates easy-to-understand explanations through predicate filtering, loss regularization, and weight pruning. 
To assess explanation quality beyond classification metrics, we also propose explainability metrics---conciseness, consistency, and strictness. 
Experiments in three simulated robotic environments show that our method produces explanations that better capture these metrics while retaining high classification accuracy.
% , with an ablation study confirming our simplification process's effectiveness. 
By bridging neural network learning and formal logic-based explanations, our work advances explainability in complex robotic applications, with future directions including more expressive wSTL structures, human-in-the-loop refinement, and explainability-by-design policy optimization.
