The active viscoelastic theory in Eq. \eqref{simplified_viscoelastic} coupled with Eq. (\ref{continuity_equation}) allow us to predict the corresponding tissue flows: it gives the tissue velocity field $\bm{v} \simeq \partial_t \bm{u}$ as a function of gradients of the displacement $\bm{u}$, that we link to the density field using the geometric relation $\rho-\rho_0 = \rho_0 \bm{\nabla}\cdot \bm{u}$, in which $\rho_0$ is the density of the undeformed tissue. 
In the channel geometry of our experiments confined in the $x$ direction where $\partial_y \equiv 0$ (Fig.~\ref{fig.3}A), this leads to $\partial_x \rho = \rho_0 \partial_x^2 u_x$. Neglecting viscous terms in \eqref{simplified_viscoelastic}, we end up with
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\begin{pmatrix}
v_x \\
v_y
\end{pmatrix}
=
\frac{1}{\rho_0 \zeta}
\begin{pmatrix}
B+\mu\\
A-K^o
\end{pmatrix}
\partial_x \rho
\end{aligned}
\label{flow}
\end{equation}
where we recall that the active elastic moduli $A$ and $K^{\rm o}$ are only present in chiral tissues.
