%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Stretched tetrahedra}

For the stretched tetrahedra (types $111r$ and $rrrr$) the situation is more complex because their neighborhood are (partly) defined by the radius of the support sphere (Figure~\ref{fig:local_maxima}).
We will get around this by taking a larger neighborhood and showing that if the density of a tetrahedron $T$ in this neighborhood exceeds that of $T_t^*$, then the radius of its supporting sphere is necessarily greater than $r$, that is, this tetrahedron is not in the set of FM-tetrahedra.
Namely, we consider the neighborhood $B_\varepsilon:=[0,\varepsilon]^4\times[-\varepsilon,\varepsilon]^2$ for the type $rrrr$, assuming that the two last edges are the stretched ones, and the neighborhood $B_\varepsilon:=[0,\varepsilon]^5\times[-\varepsilon,\varepsilon]$ for the type $111r$, assuming that the last edge is the stretched one.
In order to compare the growth rates of both the density $\delta$ and the radius $R$ of the support sphere, we introduce the function
\[
f:=R-\alpha\times\delta,
\]
where $\alpha$ must be positive and such that $f(T_t^*)=0$.
If $\varepsilon$ is such that the vector $\nabla f(T_t^*+B_\varepsilon)$ is positive (this can be checked by interval arithmetic), then the mean value theorem ensures $f(T)\geq f(T_t^*)=0$ for any $T$ in $T_t^*+B_\varepsilon$, that is:
\[
R(T)-R(T_t^*)\geq\alpha(\delta(T)-\delta(T_t^*)).
\]
Since $\alpha$ is positive and $R(T_t^*)=r$ for both types $rrrr$ and $111r$, this will prove that $T$ has a sphere support of radius larger than $r$ as soon as its density is larger than $\delta(T_t^*)$, that is, no FM-tetrahedron in $T_t^*+B_\varepsilon$ is denser than $T_t^*$.

To make this work, the first idea is to take for $\alpha$ the constant $R(T_t^*)/\delta(T_t^*)$, which is positive and yields $f(T_t^*)=0$.
A computation shows that this indeed works for the type $rrrr$ and yields $\varepsilon=1/2963$.
But this fails for the type $111r$.

But $\alpha$ can be made to depend on $T$, the idea being to capture the interdependence between the radius of the support sphere and density.
We searched for $\alpha$ in the form
\[
\alpha(T):=\frac{R(T_t^*)}{\delta(T_t^*)}
\left(\frac{ab^*}{ab}\right)^{k_1}
\left(\frac{ac^*}{ac}\right)^{k_2}
\left(\frac{ad^*}{ad}\right)^{k_3}
\left(\frac{bc^*}{bc}\right)^{k_4}
\left(\frac{bd^*}{bd}\right)^{k_5}
\left(\frac{cd^*}{cd}\right)^{k_6},
\]
where $xy$ (resp. $xy^*$) denotes the length of the edge $XY$ in $T$ (resp. $T_t^*$) and the vector $k=(k_1,\ldots,k_6)$ has to be suitably chosen.
Such functions $\alpha$ are positive over $T_t^*+B_\varepsilon$ and are such that $f(T_t^*)=0$.
Of course, this is just one possibility among many.

To find a vector $k$ which yield a small enough $\varepsilon$, we proceeded heuristically.
Namely, assuming $\varepsilon$ and $k$ are given (we start with some arbitrary values), we calculate the vector $\nabla f(T_t^*+B_\varepsilon)$ and notice that increasing a $k_i$ such that the $i$-th partial derivative is negative tends to increase this partial derivative (this is just an experimental observation).
With a little practice, we generally manage to find a suitable $k$.
We can then try further with a smaller $\varepsilon$.
After a few tries we eventually obtained $\varepsilon=1/173$ with $k=(35,35,58,40,35,35)$ for the stretched $T_{rrrr}$ and $\varepsilon=1/445$ with $k=(20,80,80,40,40,160)$ for the 11-stretched $T_{111r}$.
These values are reported in the two last columns in Table~\ref{tab:epsilon}.
This pedestrian method as well as the form of the function $\alpha$ can certainly be improved, but it was enough here to find satisfactory $\varepsilon$ values (i.e. which allowed to automatically prove all cases outside the corresponding neighborhoods, as detailed in Section~\ref{sec:dim_reduc}).

