Figure \ref{fig:Hov.PR-GPM_C1_SST} illustrates a case study that compares the spatiotemporal evolution of precipitation observed by GPM with data from the control run and the SST (+0.5 K) experiment. The control run compares favorably with GPM observations, effectively capturing the extended offshore precipitation event exceeding 600 km in the positive X channel during the second diurnal cycle. In the negative X channel, stronger precipitation reaches approximately 200 km offshore during the first diurnal cycle, while weaker precipitation extends to about 350 km in the second diurnal cycle. Note that the model tends to initiate land convection earlier and produces more organized precipitation. 


In the negative X channel, the effect of orographic precipitation, induced by banded hills ($\sim$1 km in elevation) located approximately 50 km onshore, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:2coors}, is more pronounced in the model. Nevertheless, the approximately 100 km jump between the two offshore propagation convection modes remains a distinct and stable characteristic in the WRF simulation, aligning with the GPM climatology depicted in Figure \ref{fig:clim.Hov.coast}.

Although a uniform increase of 0.5 K in SST is much smaller than the typical temperature variations experienced on land within a diurnal cycle, we observe that it leads to a slight enhancement of offshore precipitation over the ocean in the negative X channel (Figure \ref{fig:Hov.PR-GPM_C1_SST}a,c) during the second diurnal cycle. Focusing on this period, we find that precipitation triggered by the onshore sea breeze in the afternoon also intensifies in the positive X channel. 


