\subsection{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:suboptmal}}
\begin{IEEEproof}
 For a fixed $N$, $A$ and $\sigma_{P}^{2}$, the first two terms of \eqref{eq:err_Adv} are independent of $K_{1}$. However, the third and the fourth terms are dependent on $K_{1}$ owing to the error correcting capability of the DCT decoder. In particular, as we increase $K_{1}$, while the effective precision noise decreases due to better approximation on the Taylor series expansion of $f(u(\cdot))$, the dimension of the parity check matrix decreases, thereby introducing more localization errors. Thus, we believe there exists an optimal value of $K_{1}$ that minimizes \eqref{eq:err_Adv} for a given $N$, $A$ and $\sigma_{P}^{2}$. On the other hand, suppose all the $N$ worker nodes return their computations and the master uses a DCT decoder of dimension $K_{1}$. Due to the precision noise in the computations, the error localization may be imperfect and therefore, the residual errors continue to exist on some of the computations. Suppose that the master uses only $N_{1} \leq N$ number of points for interpolation. Interestingly, as $N_{1}$ increases, the first term of \eqref{eq:err_Adv} decreases due to Theorem \ref{Th:theorem 1}, whereas the possibility of including the residual noise in the interpolation increases. Thus, we believe there exists an optimal value of $N_{1}$ that minimizes \eqref{eq:err_Adv} for a given $N$, $K_{1}$, $A$ and $\sigma_{P}^{2}$.
\end{IEEEproof}
