Csisz\'ar partitions the source sequences according to their type (i.e., $m = N_k$), assigning a distinct codeword distribution to each one. 
A more general partitioning was later proposed in~\cite{6803047}, where they derived the maximum a posteriori (MAP) random-coding error exponent for arbitrary partitioning of the source sequence set.
It was further shown that a specific two-class partitioning suffices to recover Csisz\'ar's random-coding exponent. 
