\subsection{Research Questions and Hypotheses}\label{subsec:rq}

While a possible benefit of visualizations such as \ac{dcn} for learning is predicted by previous research, it is uncertain to what extend this benefit manifests also in problem solving. Although there is evidence that visualizations also provide benefits in this realm, they are likely to depend on visualization, context, and task solver and the exact moderating factors have not yet been explored sufficiently~\cite{Schewior2024}. Possible factors include the previous experience of the student, which could result in less reliance on a visualization, spatial reasoning ability as described by the \ac{itpc}~\cite{schnotz2005}, and more particularly mental rotation ability~\cite{Taylor2023}, and \ac{rc}~\cite{rau2017conditions}, whereas possible differences may be due to support of the decision making step~\cite{LINDNER201791}.

There is a need for studies considering also other moderators like learner and representational characteristics and eye-tracking data to further investigate the underlying processes and conditions for benefits of presenting a visualization. As is apparent, the \ac{dcn} visualization adds a complementary strategy (as proposed within the \ac{deft} framework) to understanding the process of the application of a Hadamard gate in two- and three-qubit systems. The question of whether there is a benefit to this strategy and under which conditions remains to be answered. We formulate the following research questions.

\begin{description}
    \item[\textup{\textbf{RQ1}}] Do participants benefit from the visualization when solving questions on the Hadamard gate in two- and three-qubit systems in terms of...\noindent
    \begin{description}
     \item[\textup{\textbf{a}}] ...performance?
     \item[\textup{\textbf{b}}] ...cognitive load (\ac{icl} and \ac{ecl})?
    \end{description}
\end{description}

To evaluate our test instrument for \ac{rc}, we ask the following research questions.

\begin{description}
    \item[\textup{\textbf{RQ2}}] Is our test instrument for \ac{rc} able to predict...
        \begin{description}
     \item[\textup{\textbf{a}}] ...performance increases?
     \item[\textup{\textbf{b}}] ...reductions in cognitive load?
    \end{description}
\end{description}

We ask the following research questions to explore whether the integration of visualization with mathematics has an impact on problem solving. 

\begin{description}
    \item[\textup{\textbf{RQ3}}] What influence do integration processes of the visualization with the mathematics have on...
    \begin{description}
     \item[\textup{\textbf{a}}] ...performance?
     \item[\textup{\textbf{b}}] ...cognitive load?
    \end{description}
\end{description}

By answering these research questions, we will be able to make statements about how to support problem solving in the context of the Hadamard gate in multi-qubit systems. Subsequently, we discuss the generalizability of the results to other associated contexts in the realm of understanding multi-qubit system processes and conclude with recommendations for educators in the field of \ac{qis} as well as perspectives for future research.

\section{Methods}\label{sec:methods}

As we found the study design to be suitable, we adopted methodological paths similar to the pilot study we conducted~\cite{bley2025visualizingquantumstatespilot} with, however, multiple adaptations. We stay with the A-B crossover design and cognitive load measurements, but restrict the context of the questions to the Hadamard gate, employ eye tracking techniques, introduce a new way of measuring representational competence and also measure mental rotation ability.

\subsection{Participants}\label{subsec:participants}

The total number of participants in the study was 42, including 31 people who were relatively new to the field of quantum physics, with a maximum of two years' experience. Due to the possible influence of expertise-reversal effects, we excluded all participants with more than three years of experience in quantum physics from the analyses. Of the 31 remaining participants, 22 were male and 9 female. The participant's ages ranged between 19-33 years. The main field of study was physics for 10 participants, computer science or engineering for 10, other fields of natural sciences for 5, and \ac{qis} for 4. Furthermore, 4 of the participants were bachelor students, 14 students were in their master's degree, 9 doing their Ph.D., and 1 was a post doc.

\subsection{Study Design}\label{subsec:design}

For the present study, a within-subject design was chosen. This is, first and foremost, enabled by the possibility of designing multiple similar questions to test task solving in the same context, therefore being able to show some of these questions with and some without visualization, to make comparative assessments. 

\subsubsection{Study structure}

We first showed an introductory video explaining \ac{dcn} and the Hadamard gate to the participants. We then measured the translational understanding between \ac{dn} and \ac{dcn} with a set of eight questions and then the visual understanding of the Hadamard gate with a set of six questions. During the following main phase of the study, eye movements were tracked. During part A of the main phase, half of the participants were shown a block of six questions without visualization and half with visualization. We then measured the perceived cognitive load during this part of the study, before giving each participant another block of six questions without or with visualization, depending on which they did not receive during part A. This random order ensures that the learning effects during part A of the study do not interfere with the results of part B. Lastly, spatial reasoning ability was measured without eye tracking. The complete structure is shown in Figure \ref{fig:structure}.

