\section{Generalizability and Robustness Checks}
\label{sec:robust}

We now discuss the generalizability of our findings, provide some extensions, and present a series of tests and alternative specifications to establish the robustness of our findings. 

%In \S\ref{ssec:alt_demand1}, we provide two alternative demand model specifications. In \S\ref{sssec:self_selection}, we use matching and IPW methods to control for selection on observables. In \S\ref{sssec:alternative.control}, we consider only using later adopter cohorts as the control group and estimate the ATT. 

\subsection{Generalizability}
\label{ssec:generalizability}

We now discuss the generalizability of our findings and provide some evidence in favor of portability to other settings. 

First, consumers may adopt online shopping for different reasons, and a natural question is whether our results are specific to consumers who adopt online shopping for certain reasons but not others. To examine this question, we leverage the fact that our observation period starts before the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic and continues through it. The cohorts that adopted from July 2019 to Feb 2020 are likely to have done so organically, while those who adopted from March 2020 are likely to have done so for exogenous reasons, i.e., the COVID-19 pandemic. Indeed, there is a big surge in online adoptions starting March 2020 (see Figure \ref{fig:2_adopter_samplesize}). We use this exogenous shock in the reason for adoption across cohorts to examine this issue. Overall, we find that the reason why a consumer switches to online shopping does not seem to have a significant impact on post-adoption price sensitivity: the ATT of cohorts that switched during the COVID-19 pandemic is similar to that of cohorts that switched organically before the onset of COVID-19 (see Figure \ref{fig:5_Main_Elas_Prediction}). This suggests that the online adoption effect is not sensitive to the reasons that drive a consumer's decision to adopt online shopping.

Second, we examine the extent to which our results are likely to be driven by the fact that online prices are slightly lower than offline prices in our setting. To that end, we examine the magnitude of the price differences between online and offline channels in our study (see details in in Web Appendix \ref{appssec:generalizability}). We find that online prices are only 2--6\% lower than offline in our data. Furthermore, the variation in prices explained by channel is significantly smaller than the variation in prices explained by SKU-level differences -- across all categories, only 4\% of the variation in prices is explained by channels, whereas when we include SKU fixed effects, this increases to 65\%. Overall, these data patterns suggest that the differences between online and offline prices are relatively small and are unlikely to be the primary driver of our results. Other potential mechanisms, such as consumers becoming more accustomed to comparing prices across brands or their exposure to a wider variety of prices, are likely to be at play as well (as discussed in $\S$\ref{sec:intro}). Thus, we expect that our results are likely to generalize to other empirical settings where online prices are the same or higher than offline prices.\footnote{In practice, there is considerable heterogeneity in whether online prices are lower, the same, or higher than offline prices across retailers, countries, and product categories \citep{cavallo2017online}.} 

Nevertheless, we note that we are unable to tease out the exact source of these effects because we lack granular data on consumers' search and shopping behavior in the online channel. We do not observe online click-stream data, so we cannot comment on price search behavior online or the variety of prices seen online. We also do not observe any search behavior in offline stores. Further, while there are some differences in offline prices across physical stores at any given point in time, we do not have a sufficiently large proportion of stores that are consistently higher or lower priced than others throughout our entire time period. Therefore, we cannot conduct a clean test of whether the effect we observe is driven more by price differences across the offline and online channels, the variety of prices that the consumer sees, or changes in search behavior that occur after adopting online shopping. We hope that future research can further dis-entangle which of these mechanisms are at play and to what extent. Further studies of such spillover effects in other countries, product categories, and retailers can also provide a more comprehensive understanding of the extent to which these effects manifest in different settings. 

%While we demonstrate empirically that online shopping leads to consumers becoming \textit{more} price sensitive in their offline shopping, we are not able to test explicitly the mechanism by which this happens. There may be several mechanisms involved (e.g. consumers compare prices more easily online, and this behavior spills over to offline shopping, exposure to lower prices online or exposure to a wider distribution of prices online). We do not explicitly test these mechanisms due to limitations of our data. We do not observe online click-stream data so we cannot comment on price search behavior online or the variety of prices seen online, and we also do not observe any search behavior in offline stores. Further, while there are some differences in offline prices across physical stores at any given point in time, we do not have a sufficiently large proportion of stores that are consistently higher or lower priced than others throughout our entire time period. Therefore we cannot conduct a clean test of whether the effect we observe is driven more by price differences across the offline and online channels, the variety of prices that the consumer sees or changes in search behavior that occur after adopting online shopping. We leave this for future research.

\subsection{Robustness checks}
\label{ssec:alt_demand1}

We first examine alternative demand specifications in $\S$\ref{sssec:alt_demand} and then consider matching methods and alternative control groups in $\S$\ref{sssec:self_selection} and \ref{sssec:alternative.control}, respectively.  

\subsubsection{Alternative Demand Specifications}
\label{sssec:alt_demand}
We now consider two different demand model specifications:
\squishlist
\item \textbf{Binary online adoption variable:} In our main demand model, we use the continuous variable \\$\textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{it}$ to capture how consumers' offline price sensitivity changes after adopting online shopping. We now consider an alternative specification where we treat online adoption as a binary treatment. To that end, we use the binary indicator, $\textrm{Post\_OnlineAdoption}_{it}$, which takes value one if customer $i$ has adopted online shopping by time $t$, and zero otherwise. We substitute $\textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{it}$ with $\textrm{Post\_OnlineAdoption}_{it}$ in Equation \eqref{eq:util_choice_M1}, and also modify the corresponding control function term accordingly. We estimate revised demand model and compute the average elasticity at the customer-month level for each product category following the same steps as before. The details of the model and  estimation results are provided in Web Appendix $\S$\ref{appssec:alternative.demand.model}. The conclusions from this alternative demand model and the corresponding ATT estimates are consistent with our main results.

\item \textbf{Accounting for a potential change in state dependence or brand loyalty due to online adoption:} In our main model, we include customer-brand fixed effects to capture time-invariant preferences and use $\mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{jt}\}$ to capture the state dependence. We now consider a specification with an additional control variable, $\mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{jt}\} \times \textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{it}$, which captures the interaction between state dependence (e.g., repeated purchase of a brand) and the share of online spending. This can account for any effects that online adoption may have on brand loyalty.

\squishend
The utility specifications and the estimation results from these two exercises are shown in Web Appendix $\S$\ref{appssec:alternative.demand.model}. We find that the results are consistent with our main specification. We also do not find evidence of significant changes to offline brand loyalty after consumers adopt online shopping. 

\subsubsection{Matching and IPW Approaches to Control for Selection on Observables}
\label{sssec:self_selection}

Consumers self-select into adopting a retailer's online channel (or not), and as shown in Web Appendix $\S$\ref{appssec:demographic}, there are some systematic differences between adopters and non-adopters in terms of observable characteristics like demographics. In general, such differences are not an issue as long as the parallel pre-trends assumption holds (see Chapter 5 of \citet{angrist2008mostly} and \citet{roth2023s} for additional discussions). To that end, we consider the pre-adoption trends in price elasticities between adopters and non-adopters and verify that the parallel pre-trends assumption holds in Web Appendix $\S$\ref{appssec.parallel_pretrend}. 

Nevertheless, differences in characteristics between groups may still potentially lead to differences in price elasticities over time in some way that is not showing up in the first few months of data or is not being accounted for in our model. For instance, there may be some factor that affects younger customers who are more likely to adopt online shopping, and only shows up in the later part of our data period. To provide additional credibility to our findings and to ensure that they are robust to any time-varying factors correlated with observable characteristics, we use propensity score-based methods like Inverse Propensity Weighting (IPW) and Propensity Score Matching (PSM) to account for differences in observable characteristics between the groups, which improves the comparability between the groups. These methods improve covariate balance, discard poorly matched customers, and make our results more robust to model misspecification with respect to any time-varying factors that are correlated with observable characteristics.

We perform the propensity score estimation and matching for each adoption cohort separately. Specifically, we define the pre-adoption period separately for each cohort and measure pre-adoption variables (e.g., monthly average spend) using all purchase data up to the month of adoption \citep{gu2021dark}. For example, when identifying matched non-adopters for customers who adopt in July 2019, we measure the pre-adoption variables for both adopters and non-adopters using the data up to June 30, 2019, and estimate the propensity score model for the adoption cohort in July 2019. We repeat this process for each cohort and estimate twelve different propensity score models corresponding to each monthly adoption cohort. We compute propensity scores with a logistic regression model and the variables included are demographics (e.g., gender, age, and household income)  and pre-adoption purchase variables (i.e. average monthly spend, number of unique orders, unique brands, and unique categories). With the estimated propensity scores, we use our CS estimator to estimate the ATT using both IPW and PSM methods. The propensity score model specification and detailed results from the IPW and PSM methods are shown in Web Appendix \ref{appssec:selection.psm}. We find that our conclusions remain the same.

\subsubsection{Alternative Control Groups}
\label{sssec:alternative.control}

While we do our best to account for differences in observable characteristics between adopters and non-adopters in $\S$\ref{sssec:self_selection}, it may be that there are certain time-specific unobserved factors that only affect adopters or would-be adopters, or disproportionately affect them more than non-adopters, thus potentially making non-adopters an unsuitable control group. We conduct an additional robustness check, leveraging the fact that we have multiple cohorts of consumers who adopted online shopping at different times. Following \cite{narang2019mobile} and \cite{manchanda2015social}, we consider an alternative specification where later adopter cohorts serve as control groups for earlier adopter cohorts. Since all of these cohorts adopt online shopping at some point, the differences in any time-specific unobservables (that correlate with factors that drive the decision of whether a consumer chooses to adopt online shopping or not) should be minimized across these different adoption cohorts, at least relative to non-adopters. The results from this alternative CS estimator are shown in Web Appendix $\S$\ref{appssec:alternative.control.group}. Overall, we see that the conclusions remain similar to those from our main specification, i.e., that consumers become more offline price-sensitive after adopting online shopping. 

%This analysis provides some additional credibility to our results. However, it does not account for time-varying unobservables that may affect different adoption cohorts differently. 

\section{Implications for Retailer Pricing and Profitability}
\label{sec:counterfactual}

So far, we have established that when consumers begin to shop online, their price sensitivity in the offline channel increases. We now examine how the retailer should modify its offline pricing strategy in response to these customer-level changes. In particular, given the firm's current level of online adoption, how much \textit{offline} profit can be gained by properly accounting for the change in price elasticity relative to a scenario where the firm ignores this effect? To quantify the profit implications, we conduct counterfactual analyses that compare offline profits under pricing policies that incorporate the online adoption effect vs. those that do not. In $\S$\ref{ssec:naive.demand.model}, we first outline how a {\it naive} retailer who ignores the online adoption effect would (incorrectly) estimate the offline price elasticities. Next, in \S\ref{ssec:optimal_prices}, we characterize the profit-maximizing prices that would be set under the true demand model (accounting for the online adoption effect) and under the naive demand model (ignoring the online adoption effect) for two pricing strategies: uniform pricing and personalized pricing. In \S\ref{ssec.counterfactual.scenarios}, we describe how we compute the profits under four potential counterfactual pricing strategies and quantify the profit implications of accounting for the online adoption effect. We also mention some caveats of our counterfactual results. 

\subsection{Naive demand model without adoption effect}
\label{ssec:naive.demand.model}

First, we characterize how a naive retailer would estimate consumers' offline price sensitivities. In this case, we assume that the retailer uses a demand model that does not account for online adoption effects. 

Following notation of the demand model in Equation \eqref{eq:util_choice_M2} in \S\ref{sec:model}, we specify the mean utility for consumer $i$ choosing brand $j$ at purchase occasion $t$ in this case as follows: 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:naive.model}
    \tilde{\psi}_{ijt} =  \tilde{\alpha}_0 \textrm{Price}_{jt} +  \tilde{\alpha}_{1} \mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{ijt}\}   + \tilde{\beta}_{1}\textrm{CF\_Price}_{jt} + \tilde{\delta}_{ij} ~~ \forall j \in \{1, \ldots, J\}\\
\end{equation}
Here, $\tilde{\alpha}_0$ is the \textit{naive} estimate of the price sensitivity parameter. $\tilde{\alpha}_1$ and $\tilde{\beta}_{1}$ are coefficients for the state dependence variable and price control function, respectively, and $\tilde{\delta}_{ij}$ denotes customer-brand fixed effects. The corresponding choice probability for consumer $i$ choosing brand $j$ on purchase occasion $t$ is then given by $\tilde{Pr}_{ijt} = \frac{\exp(\tilde{\psi}_{ijt})}{1 + \sum_{j'=1}^{J}\exp(\tilde{\psi}_{ij't})}$. We follow the estimation procedure described in \S\ref{ssec:estimation} and report the parameter estimates in Table \ref{tab:naive.demand.model} in Web Appendix \ref{appsec:counterfactual}. 
Compared to the parameter estimates in Table \ref{append.main.demand.model}, we find that the estimated price coefficient $\tilde{\alpha}_{0}$ from the naive model reflects a weighted average of price sensitivities for both adopters and non-adopters. This naive estimate serves as a key input for computing the counterfactual profit-maximizing prices that the retailer would set if they ignored the effect of online adoption.


\subsection{Optimal Offline Price Characterization Under Counterfactual Scenarios}
\label{ssec:optimal_prices}
We now characterize the profit-maximizing offline prices for a given month, under two pricing strategies: (1) Uniform pricing, where all consumers who visit an offline store during a particular time period see the same price for a given brand, and (2) Personalized pricing, where each consumer may potentially receive a different price vector depending on their demographics, prior purchase behavior, and under our demand model, their online shopping status (whether they have adopted online shopping and how much of their cumulative spend is on the online channel). In practice, the personalized pricing strategy would need to be implemented using targeted coupons \citep{rossi1996value}. Personalized pricing is being increasingly adopted by firms in retail settings and has been the focus of recent research in marketing \citep{dube2023personalized,smith2023optimal,jain2024effective}. Therefore the impact of the online adoption effect on both uniform and personalized pricing is of interest for managers. 

Let $\mathcal{M}_{k}$ be a generic demand model, where the subscript $k$ denotes the model used. Specifically, $\mathcal{M}_{N}$ denotes the {\it N}aive model defined in Equation \eqref{eq:naive.model}, whereas $\mathcal{M}_{O}$ refers to {\it O}ur model in Equation \eqref{eq:util_choice_M2}. We now characterize the optimal offline pricing for a generic demand model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$ and then derive the profit-maximizing price vectors for both the uniform and personalized pricing strategies. 

Let $N$ be the market size, which is the number of offline shoppers who make at least one offline store visit during the month. Since we focus on monthly profit, we drop the month index ($t$) for notational simplicity. Let $\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{R}^{J\times 1}$ denote the price vector, with $p_j$ being the price of brand $j$. We use the notation $p_j$ for price here (instead of Price$_j$) since price is now a decision variable rather than an observable. Under the naive model $\mathcal{M}_{N}$, the mean utility for consumer $i$ choosing brand $j$ based on Equation \eqref{eq:naive.model} can be written as:
\begin{equation}
\psi_{ij}(\mathbf{p};\mathcal{M}_{N}) = \tilde{\alpha}_0 p_{j} +  \tilde{\alpha}_{1} \mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{ij}\}   + \tilde{\beta}_{1}\textrm{CF\_Price}_{j} + \tilde{\delta}_{ij}\nonumber  
\end{equation}
In contrast, the mean utility of our demand model $\mathcal{M}_{O}$ based on Equation \eqref{eq:util_choice_M2} can be written as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    \psi_{ij}(\mathbf{p};\mathcal{M}_{O}) &= \alpha_{0} p_{j} +
    \alpha_{1} \textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{i} + 
    \alpha_2   p_{j}\times \textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{i} \\
    & + \alpha_{3} \mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{ij}\}  +\lambda_1 \textrm{CF\_Price}_{j} + \lambda_2 \textrm{CF\_Price\_cumPercOnlineSpend}_{ij} + \delta_{ij} 
    \end{aligned} \nonumber
\end{equation}


\subsubsection{Uniform Pricing}
\label{sssec.optimal.offline.price}

Under uniform pricing, the retailer maximizes the expected profit per customer, defined as: 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:uniform.profit.function}
R(\mathbf{p}; \mathcal{M}_{k}) & = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} \sum_{j=1}^{J}(p_{j} - m_{j})\times Pr_{ij}(\mathbf{p}; \mathcal{M}_{k}) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} R_{i}(\mathbf{p}; \mathcal{M}_{k})
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $m_j$ is the cost of brand $j$.
$Pr_{ij}(\mathbf{p};\mathcal{M}_{k}) = \frac{\exp(\psi_{ij}(\mathbf{p}; {\mathcal{M}_{k}})}{1 + \sum_{j'=1}^{J}\exp(\psi_{ij'}(\mathbf{p};{\mathcal{M}_{k}}))}$ refers to the choice probability of consumer $i$ for brand $j$ given price vector $\mathbf{p}$ and demand model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$, and $R_{i}(\mathbf{p}; \mathcal{M}_{k})$ represents customer $i$'s profit contribution. By taking the derivative of $R(\mathbf{p};\mathcal{M}_{k})$ with respect to each brand's price $p_{j}$, we characterize the optimal uniform pricing condition under a demand model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$: 

\begin{defnitalic}
\textit{Profit-Maximizing Uniform Price under Model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$:} Let $\alpha_{i}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}}$ denote the price sensitivity estimate for each customer $i$ under model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$. Then the optimal uniform price vector $\mathbf{p}_{*} = (p_{*,1},\ldots, p_{*,J})'$ satisfies the following system of equations:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:j_th_FOC}
    p_{*,j} - m_j = c_{j}(\mathbf{p}_{*}; \mathcal{M}_{k}) + \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*};\mathcal{M}_{k})R_{i}(\mathbf{p}_{*};\mathcal{M}_{k}),
\end{equation}
where $c_{j}(\mathbf{p}_{*};\mathcal{M}_{k}) = -\sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{Pr_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*};\mathcal{M}_{k})}{\sum_{i'=1}^{N}\alpha_{i'}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}}  Pr_{i'j}(\mathbf{p}_{*};\mathcal{M}_{k})}$ and $w_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*};\mathcal{M}_{k}) = \frac{\alpha_i^{\mathcal{M}_{k}} Pr_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*};\mathcal{M}_{k})}{\sum_{i'=1}^{N}\alpha_{i'}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}} Pr_{i'j}(\mathbf{p}_{*}; \mathcal{M}_{k})}$. Further, when we use the naive model, $\alpha_{i}^{\mathcal{M}_{N}} = \tilde{\alpha}_{0}$ and when we use our model, the $\alpha_{i}^{\mathcal{M}_{O}} = \alpha_{0} + \alpha_{2} \times \textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{i}$. 
\end{defnitalic}
This formulation gives us $J$ equations corresponding to $J$ unknown prices. We can numerically solve for the optimal price vectors based on this system of $J$ equations for a given demand model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$. See Web Appendix $\S$\ref{appssec:nonpersonalized.offline.price} for detailed proofs and derivations. 

We denote the profit-maximizing uniform prices solution for a given demand model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$ as $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{U, \mathcal{M}_k}$, and $k\in\{N, O\}$. Specifically, $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{U, \mathcal{M}_N}$ denotes the profit-maximizing uniform prices under the naive model $\mathcal{M}_{N}$ and $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{U, \mathcal{M}_O}$ denotes the profit-maximizing uniform prices under our model $\mathcal{M}_{O}$. 


\subsubsection{Personalized Pricing}
\label{sssec.personalized.offline.price}

We now consider a personalized pricing strategy where the firm can tailor prices to each customer based on their price elasticity through targeted coupons and email campaigns. Personalized pricing is now commonly used by firms in retail settings and has been the focus of recent research in marketing \citep{rossi1996value,smith2023optimal,jain2024effective}. 

Here, the retailer's objective is to maximize the profit for each customer $i$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:personalized.profit.function}
    R_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i}; \mathcal{M}_{k}) =  \sum_{j=1}^{J}(p^{i}_{j} - m_{j})\times Pr_{ij}(\mathbf{p}^{i}; \mathcal{M}_{k}),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{p}^{i}\in\mathcal{R}^{J\times 1}$ is the personalized price vector offered to customer $i$. As before, let $Pr_{ij}(\mathbf{p}^{i}; \mathcal{M}_{k}) = \frac{\exp(\psi_{ij}(\mathbf{p}^{i};\mathcal{M}_{k})}{1 + \sum_{j'=1}^{J} \exp(\psi_{ij'}(\mathbf{p}^{i};{\mathcal{M}}_{k}))}$ denote the probability that customer $i$ chooses brand $j$ under model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$ given price $\mathbf{p}^{i}$. Following \cite{jain2024effective}, we show that there exists a unique globally optimal revenue and price vector characterized by a fixed-point equation and derive the optimal price vector as follows.

\begin{defnitalic} \textit{Profit-Maximizing Personalized Price under Model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$:} The optimal personalized price vector under model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$ for any customer $i$, $\mathbf{p}^{i, \mathcal{M}_{k}}_{*}:= \arg \max_{\mathbf{p}^{i}\in \mathcal{R}^{J\times 1}_{\geq 0}} R_{i} = R_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i}; \mathcal{M}_{k})$ satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:optimal.personalized.price.condition}
    p^{i}_{*,j} - m_j = - \frac{1}{\alpha_i^{\mathcal{M}_{k}}} + R_{i},
\end{equation}
where $p^{i}_{*,j}$ is the optimal price for brand $j$ for customer $i$, $\alpha_i^{\mathcal{M}_{k}}$ is the price sensitivity estimate of customer $i$. Further, $R_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i}_{*}; \mathcal{M}_{k})$ is the solution of the fixed point equation: 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:8_Optimal_Personalized_Price}
    R_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i};\mathcal{M}_{k}) = -\frac{1}{\alpha_{i}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}}}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\exp(-1 + \alpha_{i}^{\mathcal{M}_{k}} \cdot m_j + v_{ij}({\mathcal{M}_{k}}))\exp(\alpha_i^{^{\mathcal{M}_{k}}} \cdot R_i (\mathbf{p}^{i};\mathcal{M}_{k})),
\end{equation}
where $v_{ij}({\mathcal{M}_{k}})$ represents the sum of all remaining terms in the utility for customer $i$ and brand $j$ under model $\mathcal{M}_{k}$ that do not include price.
%Specifically, under our model $\mathcal{M}_{O}$, $v_{ij}(\mathcal{M}_{O}) = \alpha_1 \textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{i} + \alpha_{3}\mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{ij}\} +\lambda_1 \textrm{CF\_Price}_{j} + \lambda_2 \textrm{CF\_Price\_cumPercOnlineSpend}_{ij} + \delta_{ij}$. Under the naive model $\mathcal{M}_{N}$, $v_{ij}(\mathcal{M}_{N}) =  \tilde{\alpha}_{1}\mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{ij}\} + \tilde{\beta}_1 \textrm{CF\_Price}_{j} + \tilde{\delta}_{ij}$.
\end{defnitalic}

Notice that Equation \eqref{eq:8_Optimal_Personalized_Price} has a unique solution since the left-hand side is increasing and starts from zero, and the right-hand side is positive at $R_i(\mathbf{p}^{i};\mathcal{M}_{k}) = 0$ and decreasing. Following \citet{jain2024effective}, we use a binary search algorithm to obtain the optimal $R_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i}_{*}; \mathcal{M}_{k})$ for each individual consumer $i$ and then calculate her optimal price vector $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i,\mathcal{M}_{k}}$. The detailed proofs for personalized pricing definitions are shown in Web Appendix \S\ref{appssec:personalized.offline.price}. Finally, we denote the profit-maximizing personalized prices for each customer $i$ under the naive and our model as $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i,\mathcal{M}_{N}}$ and $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i,\mathcal{M}_{O}}$, respectively. 

\subsection{Profit Evaluation under Counterfactual Scenarios}
\label{ssec.counterfactual.scenarios}

We now consider four counterfactual scenarios that allow for different pricing strategies (uniform vs. personalized pricing) and whether the retailer accounts for the online adoption effect (naive vs. our model). Each scenario is characterized by its input parameters, including the individual price sensitivity parameter $\alpha_{i}$ and the mean utility function $\psi_{ij}(\mathbf{p};\mathcal{M}_{k})$ as defined in \S\ref{ssec:optimal_prices}. Specifically, we consider four counterfactual scenarios, where the firm sets profit-maximizing prices: 
\squishlist
\item Uniform Price using the Naive Model, denoted by $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{U, \mathcal{M}_{N}}$. 

%This is characterized by using inputs  $\alpha_{i} = \tilde{\alpha}_{0}$ from Equation \eqref{eq:naive.model}, and $\psi_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{U, \mathcal{M}_{N}};\mathcal{M}_{N})$. %from the naive model in Equation \eqref{eq:naive.model}. 
\item Uniform Price using Our Model, denoted by $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{U, \mathcal{M}_{O}}$. 

%This is characterized by using inputs $\alpha_{i} = \alpha_{0} + \alpha_{2} \times \textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{i}$ from Equation \eqref{eq:util_choice_M2}, and $\psi_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{U, \mathcal{M}_{O}};\mathcal{M}_{O})$. %from our model in Equation \eqref{eq:util_choice_M2}. 

\item Personalized Price under the Naive Model, denoted by $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i,\mathcal{M}_{N}}$ for customer $i$. 

%This is characterized by using inputs $\alpha_{i} = \tilde{\alpha}_{0}$ from Equation \eqref{eq:naive.model}, and $\psi_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i,\mathcal{M}_{N}};\mathcal{M}_{N})$. %from naive model in Equation \eqref{eq:naive.model}. 

\item Personalized Price under Our Model, denoted by $\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i,\mathcal{M}_{O}}$ for customer $i$. 
%This is characterized by using inputs  $\alpha_{i} = \alpha_{0} + \alpha_{2} \times \textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{i}$ from Equation \eqref{eq:util_choice_M2}, and $\psi_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i,\mathcal{M}_{O}};\mathcal{M}_{O})$. %from our model in Equation \eqref{eq:util_choice_M2}. 
\squishend


The counterfactual analysis consists of two steps: (1) Optimal price calculation and (2) Profit evaluation. In the optimal price calculation step, we numerically solve for the optimal uniform and personalized price vectors under both our model and the naive model, as described in \S\ref{sssec.optimal.offline.price} and \S\ref{sssec.personalized.offline.price}. Next, in the profit evaluation step, we take the optimal price from Step 1 and evaluate the counterfactual profit using Our model $\mathcal{M}_{O}$ (which we treat as the true model that accounts for the online adoption effect). This gives us the counterfactual profits for all four scenarios described above. 

Next, we define how much the firm would gain if it uses our model instead of the naive model and engages in uniform pricing, as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
\label{eq:lift_profit_nonPersonalized}
       \Delta \textrm{Gain from Uniform Pricing (\%)} = \frac{R(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{\mathcal{U, M}_{O}};\mathcal{M}_{O}) - R(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{U,\mathcal{M}_{N}};\mathcal{M}_{O})}{R(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{U, \mathcal{M}_{N}};\mathcal{M}_{O})}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Similarly, we can define the gains from using our model instead of the naive model for the personalized pricing case:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
\label{eq:lift_profit_Personalized}
       \Delta \textrm{Gain from Personalized Pricing (\%)} = \frac{\Sigma_{i=1}^N R_i(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i, \mathcal{M}_{O}};\mathcal{M}_{O}) - \Sigma_{i=1}^{N} R_i(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i,\mathcal{M}_{N}};\mathcal{M}_{O})}{\Sigma_{i=1}^N R_i(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i, \mathcal{M}_{N}};\mathcal{M}_{O})}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Finally, we simulate the four counterfactual scenarios using data from June 2021. We focus on the last month of the observational period to provide a clear interpretation of the firm's \textit{monthly} incremental profit and to avoid any time-specific variations across months that could complicate the interpretation. Table \ref{tab:8_Sample_Size_Comparison} in Web Appendix \ref{appssec:scenarios} summarizes the sample sizes for each category included in this counterfactual study (i.e., customers defined in \S\ref{sssec:construction} with a purchase occasion in June 2021).

%\footnote{In June 2021, the average fraction of online adopters over four categories is 27.7\%. This is calculated by dividing the total number of online adopters by the total number of shoppers (adopters and non-adopters), as shown in Web Appendix \ref{appsec:counterfactual} Table \ref{tab:8_Sample_Size_Comparison}.} 

We now discuss the results of our counterfactual analysis. Under uniform pricing, we find that using our model (accounts for the online adoption effect)  to optimize prices, increases monthly profit by 1.17\% compared to pricing using the naive model. Further, under personalized pricing, using our model to optimize prices increases monthly profit by 4.1\% compared to pricing using a naive model. Overall, these results suggest that the retailer can benefit from accounting for the effect of online adoption on consumers' offline price sensitivity when optimizing their offline pricing decisions. Further, the magnitude of these gains is non-trivial, economically meaningful and consistent with the earlier literature on personalization \citep{rossi1996value, yoganarasimhan_etal_2023, rafieian_yoganarasimhan_2023}. 

Overall, our findings highlight an important yet overlooked managerial implication for multichannel retailers: retailers need to incorporate online adoption effects into offline pricing strategies, and doing so can improve retailers' profitability in the offline channel, regardless of whether pricing is uniform or personalized. Further, the magnitude of these gains is economically meaningful. As retailers continue to expand their multichannel operations, adjusting for this effect in offline pricing becomes increasingly important. 

%and by 4.1\% under personalized pricing \sbis{@Hema: Do you know if there is a better point of comparison for us to cite here instead of the Rossi paper?} These gains are in line with the literature on personalization \footnote{As a point of comparison, \cite{rossi1996value} reported a 7.26\% gain from price personalization using historical purchases and demographics, and a 0.27\% gain when using demographics alone. We refer to Table 5 in \cite{rossi1996value} and use the Blanket strategy as the benchmark to calculate the percentage increase in profit. For example, using the full information set can lead to 0.1570 / 0.1459 - 1 = 7.26\% improvement in profit relative to the Blanket strategy. A blanket strategy refers to a targeted coupon strategy only based on the distribution of brand preferences and price sensitivity in the population.}. Accounting for the online adoption effect, thus results in an economically meaningful improvement in profits for retailers, especially, for those using personalized pricing.


%The value of accounting for the online adoption effect increases with the fraction of adopters, making it an increasingly important factor for multichannel retailers, especially for those rapidly expanding their e-commerce channels and incentivizing offline-only customers to shop across both channels. 
%\hz{Shall we do some impact analysis to justify the market defined is large, or the customer sample included is large, as a rebuttal for these caveat; TO-DO}

% Next, our incremental profit estimates are based on a restricted sample of adopters and non-adopters who exclusively purchase the top pack size in a category, as defined in \S\ref{sssec:construction}. 

Nevertheless, our counterfactual results should be interpreted carefully, with the appropriate caveats.  First, it is possible that consumers' likelihood of shopping offline changes under the counterfactual pricing strategy. However, consistent with the earlier literature studying brand choice within channels, \citep{danaher2003comparison,chu2008research}, we do not model the channel choice decision \citep{valentini2011decision}, and this can potentially bias our counterfactual results. Second, we abstract away from retailer-level competition, and we do not model consumers' choices between different retailers and how changes to offline prices may affect these choices\citep{bell1998determining}. Again, this is outside of the scope of our data. Finally, our counterfactual policy estimates are based on model-based estimates rather than based on off-policy IPW evaluation (such as \cite{smith2023optimal}). This is because there is insufficient overlap in price distributions between monopolistic profit-maximizing prices and observed prices in the data. Nevertheless, in line with model-based evaluation similar to \cite{rossi1996value}, these counterfactual analyses provide important insights into the potential gains from accounting for the effect of online adoption on offline pricing. We hope that future research can build on our findings and make further progress on these issues.

