Taking the derivative of $\tilde{R}(\mathbf{p})$ with respect to price $p_{j}$ yields the retailer's uniform pricing condition under the naive model, which is given by the following definition.


\begin{defnitalic}
\textit{Profit-Maximizing Uniform Price under the Naive Model:} Given consumers' offline price sensitivity being $\tilde{\alpha}_{0}$, the profit-maximizing uniform price vector under the naive model, $\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{*} = (\tilde{p}_{*,1},\ldots, \tilde{p}_{*,J})'$, with the $j_{th}$ entry being the price for brand $j$, $\tilde{p}_{*,j}$ satisfies the following condition: 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:naive.model.j_th_FOC}
    & \tilde{p}_{*,j} - m_j = -\frac{1}{\tilde{\alpha}_{0}} + \sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{\tilde{Pr}_{ij}(\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{*}) }{\sum_{i'=1}^{N}\tilde{Pr}_{i'j}(\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{*})} \times \tilde{R}_{i}(\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{*}) \\
