\begin{defnitalic}
\textit{Profit-Maximizing Uniform Price under Our Model:} Given consumer $i$'s offline price sensitivity $\alpha_i = \alpha_0 + \alpha_2 \times \textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{i}$, the optimal uniform price vector $\mathbf{p}_{*} = (p_{*,1},\ldots, p_{*,J})'$, with the $j_{th}$ entry  $p_{*,j}$ being the optimal price for brand $j$, satisfies the following condition: 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:j_th_FOC}
    & p_{*,j} - m_j = c_{j}(\mathbf{p}_{*}) + \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*})R_{i}(\mathbf{p}_{*}) \\
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $c_{j}(\mathbf{p}_{*}) = -\sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{Pr_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*})}{\sum_{i'=1}^{N}\alpha_{i'} Pr_{i'j}(\mathbf{p}_{*})}$ and $w_{ij}(\mathbf{p}_{*};\mathcal{M}_{k}) = \frac{\alpha_i Pr_{ij}(\mathbf{p};\mathcal{M}_{k})}{\sum_{i'=1}^{N}\alpha_{i'} Pr_{i'j}(\mathbf{p}_{*})}$.
\end{defnitalic}
This formulation gives us $J$ equations corresponding to the $J$ unknown prices to solve, with the $j_{th}$ row equation given by Equation \eqref{eq:j_th_FOC}. We can numerically solve for the optimal price vectors based on this system of $J$ equations. The detailed proofs and derivation are shown in Web Appendix $\S$\ref{appssec:nonpersonalized.offline.price}. 


\subsubsection{Personalized Pricing}
\label{sssec.personalized.offline.price}

We now consider a personalized pricing strategy where the firm can tailor prices to each customer based on their price elasticity through targeted coupons and email campaigns. 

\noindent \textbf{Setting profit-maximizing personalized prices under the naive model}

First, under the naive model, the retailer sets a personalized price for each customer $i$ by maximizing the expected offline profit over the brand choice probability from the naive model in Equation \ref{eq:naive.model}: 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:naive.personalized.profit.function}
    \tilde{R}_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i}) & =  \sum_{j=1}^{J}(p^{i}_{j} - m_{j})\times \frac{\exp(\tilde{\psi}_{ij})}{1 + \sum_{j'=1}^{J} \exp(\tilde{\psi}_{ij'})} \\
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}

The retailer's profit-maximizing personalized price vector under the naive model for customer $i$ is defined as follows: 
\hspace{2em}
\begin{defnitalic} 
\textit{Profit-Maximizing Personalized Price under Naive Model:} The personalized price vector under the naive model for any customer $i$, denoted as $\tilde{\mathbf{p}}^{i}_{*}$, satisfies
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    \tilde{p}^{i}_{*,j} - m_j = - \frac{1}{\tilde{\alpha}_0} + \tilde{R}_{i}(\tilde{\mathbf{p}}^{i}_{*})
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{R}_{i}(\tilde{\mathbf{p}}^{i}_{*})$ is the solution of the fixed point equation. 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:naive_model_personalized_price}
    \tilde{R}_{i} = -\frac{1}{\tilde{\alpha}_0}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\exp(-1 + \tilde{\alpha}_0 m_j + \tilde{v}_{ij})\exp(\tilde{\alpha}_0 \tilde{R}_i),
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{v}_{ij} = \tilde{\alpha}_1\mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{ij}\} + \tilde{\beta}_{1}\textrm{CF\_Price}_{jt} + \tilde{\delta}_{ij}$ refers to the sum of all remaining terms that do not include price but affect the utility in Equation \eqref{eq:naive.model}. %\footnote{Specifically, $\tilde{v}_{ij} = \tilde{\alpha}_1\mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{ij}\} + \tilde{\beta}_{1}\textrm{CF\_Price}_{jt} + \tilde{\delta}_{ij}$} 
\end{defnitalic}


\noindent \textbf{Setting profit-maximizing personalized prices under our model}

Next, when the retailer sets the personalized price for each customer using our model, the objective is to maximize each consumer $i$'s expected offline profit over the choice probabilities predicted by our model, defined as: 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:personalized.profit.function}
    R_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i}) & =  \sum_{j=1}^{J}(p^{i}_{j} - m_{j})\times \frac{\exp(\psi_{ij})}{1 + \sum_{j'=1}^{J} \exp(\psi_{ij'})} \\
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{p}^{i} \in R^{J\times 1}$ represents the personalized price vector for customer $i$. First, following \cite{jain2024effective}, we show that there exists a unique global optimum revenue and price vector characterized by a fixed-point equation. Next, we derive the optimal price vector as follows.
\hspace{2em}
\begin{defnitalic} \textit{Profit-Maximizing Personalized Price under Our Model:} The personalized price vector under our model for any customer $i$, $\mathbf{p}^{i}_{*}:= \arg \max_{\mathbf{p}^{i}\in \mathbb{R}^{J}_{\geq 0}} R_{i} = R_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i})$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    p^{i}_{*,j} - m_j = - \frac{1}{\alpha_i} + R_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i}_{*})
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $R_{i}(\mathbf{p}^{i}_{*})$ is the solution of the fixed point equation. 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:8_Optimal_Personalized_Price}
    R_{i} = -\frac{1}{\alpha_i}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\exp(-1 + \alpha_i m_j + v_{ij})\exp(\alpha_i R_i),
\end{equation}
where $v_{ij} = \alpha_{1} \textrm{cumPercOnlineSpend}_{i} + \alpha_{3}\mathcal{I}\{\textrm{BuyLastMonth}_{ij}\}+ \delta_{ij} +\lambda_1 \textrm{CF\_Price}_{j} \\+ \lambda_2 \textrm{CF\_Price\_cumPercOnlineSpend}_{ij}$. It refers to the sum of all remaining terms that do not include price but affect the utility in Equation \eqref{eq:util_choice_M2}. 
\end{defnitalic}

%\sbis{Again shouldn't we have two definitions here?}

Notice that Equation \eqref{eq:8_Optimal_Personalized_Price} has a unique solution since the left-hand side is increasing and starts from zero, and the right-hand side is positive at $R_i = 0$ and decreasing. Following \citet{jain2024effective}, we use a binary search algorithm to obtain the optimal $R_{i}$ for each individual consumer $i$ and then calculate her optimal price vector $\mathbf{p}^{i}_{*}$. The detailed proofs for personalized pricing definitions are shown in Web Appendix \S\ref{appssec:personalized.offline.price}.



\subsection{Evaluating profits under the counterfactual scenarios}
\label{ssec.counterfactual.scenarios}

So far, we have characterized conditions for optimal offline pricing. We now summarize four counterfactual scenarios that allow for different pricing strategies (uniform vs. personalized pricing) and whether the retailer accounts for the online adoption effect (naive vs. our model). 

\squishlist
\item Profit-Maximizing Uniform Price under the Naive Model, denoted as $\tilde{\boldsymbol{p}}_{*}$. 
\item Profit-Maximizing Uniform Price under Our Model, denoted as $\boldsymbol{p}_{*}$. 
\item Profit-Maximizing Personalized Price under the Naive Model: denoted as $\tilde
{\boldsymbol{p}}_{*}^{i}$ for customer $i$. 
\item Profit-Maximizing Personalized Price under Our Model: denoted as $\boldsymbol{p}_{*}^{i}$ for customer $i$. 
\squishend


The counterfactual analysis consists of two steps: \textbf{optimal price calculation} and \textbf{profit evaluation}. In the \textbf{optimal price calculation} step, we numerically solve for each of the optimal uniform and personalized price vectors under our and naive model as described in \S\ref{sssec.optimal.offline.price} and \S\ref{sssec.personalized.offline.price}.

In the \textbf{profit evaluation} step, we use these price vectors to calculate the total offline profits across all categories under each pricing scenario. Specifically, for uniform pricing, we compute two counterfactual profit measures, $R(\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{*})$ and $R(\mathbf{p}_{*})$, which represent the retailer's counterfactual offline profit when the offline price (i) does not account for the online adoption effect and (ii) does account for the online adoption effect. These profits are computed by substituting the respective price vectors into our utility model $(\psi_{ij})$ and calculating individual-level brand choice probabilities $Pr_{ij}(\mathbf{p};\mathcal{M}_{k})$ and expected profit according to Equation \eqref{eq:uniform.profit.function} for each category. We define the retailer's \textit{Incremental Profit (\%)} under uniform pricing as the difference in the counterfactual offline profit (across all three categories where we find an effect) between the profit-maximizing uniform price under our model and naive model, normalized by the profit evaluated at the profit-maximizing uniform price under the naive model:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
\label{eq:8_lift_profit_nonPersonalized}
        \textrm{Incremental Profit (\%)} \textrm{ for Uniform Pricing} = \frac{R(\mathbf{p}_{*}) - R(\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{*})}{R(\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{*})}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}

For personalized pricing, we aggregate individual profits and obtain total personalized profit with and without accounting for online adoption as $PR = \sum_{i=1}^{N}R_{i}(\mathbf{p}_{*}^{i})$ and $\tilde{PR} = \sum_{i=1}^{N}R_{i}(\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{*}^{i})$, respectively. The retailer's \textit{Incremental Profit (\%)} under personalized pricing is defined as the difference in counterfactual offline profit between profit-maximizing personalized prices under our and under naive model, normalized by the counterfactual profit evaluated at the personalized prices under naive model:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
\label{eq:8_lift_profit_Personalized}
        \textrm{Incremental Profit (\%)} \textrm{ from Personalized Pricing} = \frac{PR - \tilde{PR}}{\tilde{PR}}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}


We simulate the four counterfactual scenarios, using data from June 2021. We focus on the last month of the observational period to provide a clear interpretation of the firm's \textit{monthly} incremental profit and to avoid any time-specific variations across months that could complicate the interpretation. Table \ref{tab:8_Sample_Size_Comparison} in Web Appendix \ref{appssec:scenarios} summarizes the sample sizes for each category included in this counterfactual study, specifically customers defined in \S\ref{sssec:construction} with a purchase occasion in June 2021. 

%\footnote{In June 2021, the average fraction of online adopters over four categories is 27.7\%. This is calculated by dividing the total number of online adopters by the total number of shoppers (adopters and non-adopters), as shown in Web Appendix \ref{appsec:counterfactual} Table \ref{tab:8_Sample_Size_Comparison}.} 

We find that at the retailers' current level of online adoption, accounting for the online adoption effect increases monthly profit by 1.17\% under the \textit{uniform} pricing policy, and by 4.1\% under personalized pricing \sbis{@Hema: Do you know if there is a better point of comparison for us to cite here instead of the Rossi paper?} These gains are in line with the literature on personalization
\footnote{As a point of comparison, \cite{rossi1996value} reported a 7.26\% gain from price personalization using historical purchases and demographics, and a 0.27\% gain when using demographics alone. We refer to Table 5 in \cite{rossi1996value} and use the Blanket strategy as the benchmark to calculate the percentage increase in profit. For example, using the full information set can lead to 0.1570 / 0.1459 - 1 = 7.26\% improvement in profit relative to the Blanket strategy. A blanket strategy refers to a targeted coupon strategy only based on the distribution of brand preferences and price sensitivity in the population.}. Accounting for the online adoption effect, thus results in an economically meaningful improvement in profits for retailers, especially, for those using personalized pricing.

Overall, these findings highlight an important yet overlooked managerial implication for multichannel retailers: managers should properly incorporate online adoption effects into retailers' offline pricing strategies, which can improve retailers' profitability in the offline channel, regardless of whether pricing is uniform or personalized. 

%The value of accounting for the online adoption effect increases with the fraction of adopters, making it an increasingly important factor for multichannel retailers, especially for those rapidly expanding their e-commerce channels and incentivizing offline-only customers to shop across both channels. 
%\hz{Shall we do some impact analysis to justify the market defined is large, or the customer sample included is large, as a rebuttal for these caveat; TO-DO}

Finally, we discuss some caveats and limitations of our counterfactual analyses. First, we do not model how the counterfactual offline prices would change the consumers' channel choice decision in a given month and potentially change the number of offline purchase occasions. We take the offline purchase visits in a particular month as given. Modeling the consumers' channel choice decision and how changes in offline and online prices would affect those decisions is a much harder problem and beyond the scope of what we do in this paper. This is something we leave for future research. Next, our incremental profit estimates are based on a restricted sample of adopters and non-adopters who exclusively purchase the top pack size in a category, as defined in \S\ref{sssec:construction}. Further, we abstract away from retailer-level competition and we do not model consumers' choices between different retailers, and how changes to offline prices may affect these choices\citep{bell1998determining}. Finally, in our application, it is infeasible to conduct an off-policy evaluation of a price targeting policy like \cite{smith2023optimal}, because there is insufficient overlap in price distributions between monopolistic profit-maximizing prices and observed prices in the data, and it is infeasible to control for competitors' complex pricing dynamics. Nevertheless, in line with model-based evaluation similar to \cite{rossi1996value}, these counterfactual analyses shed light on a crucial yet overlooked managerial insight in offline pricing: accounting for the online adoption effect can lead to economically significant profit improvements under both uniform and personalized pricing. As retailers continue to expand their multichannel operations, adjusting for this effect in offline pricing becomes increasingly important. 


%However, the customer samples defined in our study (e.g., customers who exclusively buy top one pack size) account for an average of 45\% of total customers in each category, ensuring that the analysis captures a substantial share of the retailer’s customer base. \footnote{We define a customer in a given category as any customer who has made at least one in-store purchase in that category during our observation period.} 


