Fig.~\ref{fig:shd_total_energy} shows the accuracy versus the total energy consumption, including both compute and transmission energy. As in Fig.~\ref{fig:shd_sparsity}, each point in the figure corresponds to a model trained under a different regularization strength $\alpha$. The BRF model achieves the best energy-accuracy trade-off, reaching up to 93.1\% accuracy with total energy of as low as 5.12~$\mu \rm J$. RF achieves similar accuracy, but at the higher energy cost of 10.43~$\mu \rm J$. ALIF reaches up to 89.5\% accuracy but requires 25.99 to 32.23~$\mu \rm J$, while LIF reaches only 81.8\% accuracy at an energy cost as high as 38.58~$\mu \rm J$. Overall, despite the BRF neuron's increased somatic complexity, the associated energy cost is negligible, and the substantial spike sparsity levels lead to lower synaptic and transmission energy consumption, resulting in the most efficient energy-accuracy trade-off.

