Noise management is a critical aspect of HE schemes. Table \ref{tab:noise} summarizes that code-based HE inherits inherent error correction from the underlying codes (such as Goppa codes), which helps to keep noise growth bounded in additive settings. However, noise growth in these schemes is generally linear, as seen in some additive constructions \cite{armknecht2011constructing}. In lattice-based HE, the noise grows polynomially with each multiplicative operation, necessitating frequent noise management strategies modulus switching and bootstrapping. Mature bootstrapping techniques, as pioneered by Gentry and further optimized in schemes such as BGV \cite{brakerski2012fully}, mitigate the effects of noise accumulation, albeit at an additional computational cost.

