\begin{proof}
Set $p_{1}:=\mathrm{Id}\otimes \varepsilon $ and $p_{2}:=\varepsilon \otimes
\mathrm{Id}$. Observe that $p_{1}$ and $p_{2}$ are counitary algebra maps. Moreover, we
have
\begin{equation*}
(p_{1}\otimes p_{1})\Delta _{H_{1}\otimes H_{2}}(a\otimes
b)=a_{1}\varepsilon (b_{1})\otimes a_{2}\varepsilon (b_{2})=a_{1}\otimes
a_{2}\varepsilon (b)=(1\otimes 1)\Delta (a\varepsilon (b))(1\otimes 1)^{-1}
\end{equation*}%
and also
\begin{equation*}
(p_{1}\otimes p_{1})(\mathrm{Id}\otimes \tau \otimes \mathrm{Id})(\mathcal{R}%
_{1}\otimes \mathcal{R}_{2})=(\mathrm{Id}\otimes \mathrm{Id}\otimes
\varepsilon \otimes \varepsilon )(\mathcal{R}_{1}\otimes \mathcal{R}_{2})=%
