We now look for a terminal object in $\TwTrBialg$. We first recall the definition in the $2$-categorical context, see again \cite[\S 2.2]{CKWW2}. 

\begin{definition}
A \emph{terminal object} in a $2$-category $\Cc$ is a 0-cell $\mathbf{1}$ such that the functor 
\[\Cc(X,\mathbf{1})\to* \] into the terminal category is an equivalence of categories. 
Explicitly, this means that for any 0-cell $X$ there is a 1-cell $X\rightarrow \mathbf{1}$ and for any two 1-cells $f,g:X\rightarrow \mathbf{1}$ there is a unique $2$-cell $f\Rightarrow g$. Note that this $2$-cell is necessarily  an isomorphism. 
\begin{invisible}
There are unique $\alpha:f\Rightarrow g$ and $\beta:g\Rightarrow f$ and also their compositions are unique and have than to be the identities. 
%\rd{[Qui la traduzione del fatto che il funtore nella categoria terminale sia un'equivalenza torna con la definizione trovata su wiki. Ma non coincide con la traduzione scritta in \cite{CKWW2}.]}
\end{invisible}
\end{definition}

Note that in $\TwTrBialg$ the functor above is even bijective on objects, whence a category isomorphism, see \cite[Definition 1.5.1]{BorI94}.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:termobj}
The triangular bialgebra $(\Bbbk,1\otimes 1)$ is a terminal object in the $2$-category $\TwTrBialg$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $(H,\mathcal{R})$ be a triangular bialgebra. Since $\mathcal{F}=1\otimes1$ is the unique twist (and triangular structure) on $\Bbbk$ and $\varepsilon:H\to\Bbbk$ is the unique morphism in $\Bialg$ from $H$ to $\Bbbk$, we get that $(\varepsilon,1\ot1):(H,\Rr)\to(\Bbbk,1\ot1)$ is the unique morphism in $\TwTrBialg$ from $(H,\Rr)$ to $(\Bbbk,1\ot1)$. Given a $2$%
-cell $k:\left( \varepsilon ,1\otimes 1\right) \Rightarrow \left(
\varepsilon ,1\otimes 1\right) ,$ we have that $(k\otimes k)(1\ot 1)=(1\otimes 1)\Delta_{\Bbbk} (k)$, so that $k^2=k$. Since $k\neq 0$  (cf. \cref{rmk:gaugeps}), we get $k=1$, so that the corresponding $2$-cell is necessarily the identity $2$-cell. Therefore, $(\Bbbk,1\ot1)$ is a terminal object in the 2-category $\TwTrBialg$.
\end{proof}

In view of \cite[Proposition 2.4]{CKWW2}, by \cref{thm:Tw2cart} and \cref{lem:termobj}, we get the following result.

\begin{corollary}
The $2$-category $\TwTrBialg$ has finite products.
\end{corollary}

Moreover, in view of \cite[Theorem 2.15]{CKWW2}, we also obtain the following result. Here, by a symmetric monoidal $2$-category we mean $2$-category that is symmetric monoidal as a bicategory.
%E' lo stesso che fanno qui \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2211.04464}{GURSKI} a pag. 3.

\begin{corollary}
$\TwTrBialg$ is a symmetric monoidal 2-category with the binary product, defined on objects as in \eqref{def:binaryproduct} and on 1-cells as in \eqref{eq:tns1cell}, as its tensor product and the terminal object $(\Bbbk,1\ot1)$ as its unit object.
\end{corollary}

We end this subsection by proving a result that will be useful in the following.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:isoTwTr}
A $1$-cell $\left( f,\mathcal{F}\right) :\left( H,\mathcal{R}\right) \rightarrow \left(
H^{\prime },\mathcal{R}^{\prime }\right) $  in $\TwTrBialg$ is invertible if, and
only if, $f:\left( H,\mathcal{R}\right) \rightarrow \left( H_{\mathcal{F}%
}^{\prime },\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\prime }\right) $ is an isomorphism in $\TrBialg$.

Thus, $(H,\Rr)$ and $(H',\Rr')$ are isomorphic in $\TwTrBialg$ if, and only if, they are twist equivalent up to isomorphism.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $\left( f,\mathcal{F}\right) :\left( H,\mathcal{R}\right) \rightarrow
\left( H^{\prime },\mathcal{R}^{\prime }\right) $ be an isomorphism in $\TwTrBialg$. Then
there is $\left( g,\mathcal{G}\right) :\left( H^{\prime },\mathcal{R}%
^{\prime }\right) \rightarrow \left( H,\mathcal{R}\right) $ in $\TwTrBialg$ such that $%
\left( f,\mathcal{F}\right) \circ \left( g,\mathcal{G}\right) =\left(
\mathrm{Id}_{H^{\prime }},1_{H^{\prime }}\otimes 1_{H^{\prime }}\right) $
and $\left( g,\mathcal{G}\right) \circ \left( f,\mathcal{F}\right) =\left(
\mathrm{Id}_{H},1_{H}\otimes 1_{H}\right) .$ In particular, $f g=\mathrm{%
Id}_{H^{\prime }}$ and $g f=\mathrm{Id}_{H}$ so that $f:\left( H,%
\mathcal{R}\right) \rightarrow \left( H_{\mathcal{F}}^{\prime },\mathcal{R}_{%
\mathcal{F}}^{\prime }\right) $ is an isomorphism in $\TrBialg$.

Conversely, if $f:\left( H,\mathcal{R}\right) \rightarrow \left( H_{\mathcal{%
F}}^{\prime },\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\prime }\right) $ is an isomorphism in $\TrBialg$, we can set $g:=f^{-1}$ and $\mathcal{G}:=\left(
f^{-1}\otimes f^{-1}\right) \left( \mathcal{F}^{-1}\right) \in H\otimes H.$
We check that $\mathcal{G}$ is a twist on $H.$ Since $%
f:H\rightarrow H^{\prime }$ is an algebra map ($H^{\prime }$ and $H_{%
\mathcal{F}}^{\prime }$ have the same underlying algebra), we get
\begin{align*}
\left( f\otimes f\otimes f\right) \left( \left( \mathcal{G}\otimes
1\right) \left( \Delta \otimes \mathrm{Id}\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right)
\right)  
&=\left( \left( f\otimes f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right) \otimes
1\right) \left( \left( f\otimes f\right) \Delta \otimes f\right) \left(
\mathcal{G}\right)  \\
&=\left( \mathcal{F}^{-1}\otimes 1\right) \left( \Delta _{\mathcal{F}%
}^{\prime }f\otimes f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right)  \\
&=\left( \Delta ^{\prime }f\otimes f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right)
\left( \mathcal{F}^{-1}\otimes 1\right)  \\
&=\left( \Delta ^{\prime }\otimes \mathrm{Id}\right) \left( \mathcal{F}%
^{-1}\right) \left( \mathcal{F}^{-1}\otimes 1\right)  \\
&=\left( \mathrm{Id}\otimes \Delta ^{\prime }\right) \left( \mathcal{F}%
^{-1}\right) \left( 1\otimes \mathcal{F}^{-1}\right)  \\
&=\left( f\otimes \Delta ^{\prime }f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right)
\left( 1\otimes \mathcal{F}^{-1}\right)  \\
&=\left( 1\otimes \mathcal{F}^{-1}\right) \left( f\otimes \Delta _{\mathcal{%
F}}^{\prime }f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right)  \\
&=\left( 1\otimes \left( f\otimes f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right)
\right) \left( f\otimes \left( f\otimes f\right) \Delta \right) \left(
\mathcal{G}\right)  \\
&=\left( f\otimes f\otimes f\right) \left( \left( 1\otimes \mathcal{G}%
\right) \left( \mathrm{Id}\otimes \Delta \right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right)
\right)
\end{align*}%
so that $\left( \mathcal{G}\otimes 1\right) \left( \Delta \otimes \mathrm{Id}%
\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right) =\left( 1\otimes \mathcal{G}\right) \left(
\mathrm{Id}\otimes \Delta \right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right) .$ Moreover%
\begin{equation*}
\left( \varepsilon \otimes \mathrm{Id}\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right)
=\left( \varepsilon \otimes \mathrm{Id}\right) \left( f^{-1}\otimes
f^{-1}\right) \left( \mathcal{F}^{-1}\right) =\left( \varepsilon \otimes
f^{-1}\right) \left( \mathcal{F}^{-1}\right) =1
\end{equation*}%
and similarly $\left( \mathrm{Id}\otimes \varepsilon \right) \left( \mathcal{%
G}\right) =1.$ Now, we verify that $g:H^{\prime }\rightarrow H_{\mathcal{G}}$
is a coalgebra map, whence a bialgebra map. We compute%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left( f\otimes f\right) \Delta _{\mathcal{G}}\left( h\right)  &=&\left(
f\otimes f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\Delta \left( h\right) \mathcal{G}%
^{-1}\right)  \\
&=&\left( f\otimes f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right) \left( f\otimes
f\right) \Delta \left( h\right) \left( f\otimes f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}%
^{-1}\right)  \\
&=&\mathcal{F}^{-1}\left( f\otimes f\right) \Delta \left( h\right) \mathcal{F%
}=\Delta ^{\prime }f\left( h\right) 
\end{eqnarray*}%
so that $\left( f\otimes f\right) \Delta _{\mathcal{G}}=\Delta ^{\prime }f$
and hence $\Delta _{\mathcal{G}}g=\left( g\otimes g\right) \Delta ^{\prime }.
$ Moreover
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left( g\otimes g\right) \left( \mathcal{R}^{\prime }\right)  &=&\left(
g\otimes g\right) \left( \left( \mathcal{F}^{\mathrm{op}}\right) ^{-1}%
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{F}}^{\prime }\mathcal{F}\right)  \\
&=&\left( \left( g\otimes g\right) \left( \mathcal{F}^{-1}\right) \right) ^{%
\mathrm{op}}\left( g\otimes g\right) \left( \mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{F}%
}^{\prime }\right) \left( g\otimes g\right) \left( \mathcal{F}\right)  \\
&=&\mathcal{G}^{\mathrm{op}}\left( g\otimes g\right) \left( f\otimes
f\right) \left( \mathcal{R}\right) \mathcal{G}^{-1}=\mathcal{G}^{\mathrm{op}}%
\mathcal{RG}^{-1}=\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{G}}.
\end{eqnarray*}%
Thus, $g:\left( H^{\prime },\mathcal{R}^{\prime }\right) \rightarrow \left(
H_{\mathcal{G}},\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{G}}\right) $ is a morphism in $\TrBialg$, hence $\left( g,\mathcal{G}\right)
:\left( H^{\prime },\mathcal{R}^{\prime }\right) \rightarrow \left( H,%
\mathcal{R}\right)$ is a morphism in $\TwTrBialg$. By definition of composition, we have
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left( g,\mathcal{G}\right) \circ \left( f,\mathcal{F}\right)  &=&\left(
gf,\left( g\otimes g\right) \left( \mathcal{F}\right) \mathcal{G}\right)
=\left( \mathrm{Id}_{H},1_{H}\otimes 1_{H}\right) , \\
\left( f,\mathcal{F}\right) \circ \left( g,\mathcal{G}\right)  &=&\left(
fg,\left( f\otimes f\right) \left( \mathcal{G}\right) \mathcal{F}\right)
=\left( \mathrm{Id}_{H^{\prime }},1_{H^{\prime }}\otimes 1_{H^{\prime
}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
and hence $\left( f,\mathcal{F}\right) $ is an isomorphism in $\TwTrBialg$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Comparison with cocommutative bialgebras}
Here we are going to see how cocommutative bialgebras sit inside twisted triangular bialgebras.
Given a cocommutative bialgebra $H$, we know that $(H,1\otimes1)$ is an object in $\TwTrBialg$. If $H'$ is another cocommutative bialgebra, then a twisted morphisms of triangular bialgebras $(f,1\ot 1):(H,1\otimes1)\to (H',1\otimes1)$ is just a bialgebra map $f:H\to H'.$ A gauge transformation $a:(f,1\ot 1)\Rightarrow (f',1\ot 1)$ is just a grouplike element $a\in G(H')$ such that \eqref{def:gauge2} holds true.

Since, the assignments 
\[G:\Bialgcc\to \TwTrBialg,\; H\mapsto (H,1\otimes 1),\; f\mapsto (f,1\ot 1),\; a\mapsto a,\]
preserve the identity $1$-cells, the identity $2$-cells, the composition of $2$-cells, and horizontal compositions of $1$-cells and $2$-cells, then $G$ defines a $2$-functor, see also \cite[Explanation 4.1.9]{Johnson-Yau-book}.

Here $\Bialgcc$ is the $2$-category where: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item[i)] $0$-cells are cocommutative bialgebras,
    \item[ii)] $1$-cells are bialgebra maps $f:H\to H'$, 
    \item[iii)] $2$-cells $a:f\Rightarrow f'$ are grouplike elements $a\in H'$ such that \eqref{def:gauge2} holds true. 
\end{itemize}
 The vertical and horizontal compositions are defined as follows:
 \[\xymatrix@C=2cm{
H\ruppertwocell^{f}{b}
\rlowertwocell_{f''}{a}
\ar[r]_(.35){f'} & H'}
 =
\xymatrix{H\rtwocell^{f}_{f''}{ab}&H'},\qquad
\xymatrix{H\rtwocell^{f}_{f'}{b} & H' \rtwocell^{g}_{g'}{a}& H''}
=  \xymatrix@C=1cm{H \rrtwocell^{gf}_{g'f'}{\mathrlap{ag(b)}}&  & H''}.
\] 
\begin{invisible}
The vertical composition of $2$-cells is clearly associative and unital. \\
If $a:\alpha\Rightarrow \alpha'$, $b:\beta\Rightarrow \beta'$ and $c:\gamma\Rightarrow \gamma'$, then 
\[(a\hc b)\hc c=(a\alpha(b))\hc c=a\alpha(b)\alpha\beta(c)
=a\alpha(b\beta(c))
=a\alpha(b\hc c)=a\hc(b\hc c).\] 
Moreover $1\hc a=a\alpha(1)=a$ and $a\hc 1=1\id(a)=a$ so that the horizontal composition is associative and unital. Note that $\id_g\hc \id f=1\hc 1=1=\id_{gf}.$ Moreover, for $2$-cells as in the 
\[
\xymatrix@C=2cm{
H\ruppertwocell^{g}{b'}
\rlowertwocell_{g''}{a'}
\ar[r]_(.35){g'} & H'\ruppertwocell^{f}{b}
\rlowertwocell_{f''}{a}
\ar[r]_(.35){f'}&H''}
\]
we have the interchange law
$(a\vc b)\hc (a'\vc b')
=(ab)\hc (a'b')
=abf(a'b')
=abf(a')f(b')
=af'(a')bf(b')
=(af'(a'))\vc (bf(b'))
=(a\hc a')\vc (b\hc b')$.
Thus, in view of \cite[Proposition 2.3.4]{Johnson-Yau-book}, $\Bialgcc$ is a $2$-category.
\end{invisible}

In some $2$-categories the product of two objects can exist without being their product in the $2$-categorical sense, see \cite[exercise 7.10.4]{BorI94}. Still, for $\Bialgcc$ we have the following result.

\begin{lemma}
The product of two objects in the $1$-category $\Bialgcc$ is also the product in the $2$-categorical sense. The same goes for the terminal object.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
We mentioned that the binary product of objects $H_1$ and $H_2$ in the $1$-category $\Bialgcc$ is $(H_1\otimes H_2,\id\ot\varepsilon,\varepsilon\ot\id)$ and that, given morphisms $f_1:H\to H_1$ and $f_2:H\to H_2$ in $\Bialgcc$, the diagonal morphism is $\langle f_1,f_2\rangle\coloneq (f_1\ot f_2)\Delta_H:H\to H_1\ot H_2$. Now, given morphisms $f,f':H\to H_1\ot H_2$ and $2$-cells $g_1:(\id\ot\varepsilon)f\Rightarrow (\id\ot\varepsilon) f'$ and $g_2:(\varepsilon\ot\id)f\Rightarrow (\varepsilon\ot\id)f'$ in the $2$-category $\Bialgcc$, we have that $g_1:(\id\ot\varepsilon,1\ot1)(f,1\ot1)\Rightarrow (\id\ot\varepsilon,1\ot1) (f',1\ot1)$ and $g_2:(\varepsilon\ot\id,1\ot1)(f,1\ot1)\Rightarrow (\varepsilon\ot\id,1\ot1)(f',1\ot1)$ are also $2$-cells in $\TwTrBialg$. Thus, by \cref{thm:Tw2cart}, we get a unique $2$-cell $g:(f,1\ot1)\Rightarrow (f',1\ot1)$ in $\TwTrBialg$ such that $(\id\ot\varepsilon,1\ot1)g=g_1$ and $(\varepsilon\ot\id,1\ot1)g=g_2$, namely $g=g_1\ot g_2$. This means that we have a unique $2$-cell $g:f\Rightarrow f'$ in $\Bialgcc$ such that $(\id\ot\varepsilon)g=g_1$ and $(\varepsilon\ot\id)g=g_2$, namely $g=g_1\ot g_2$.

The terminal object in the $1$-category $\Bialgcc$ is given by the base field $\Bbbk$. Now, given two morphisms $f,f':H\to \Bbbk$ in $\Bialgcc$, we  have that $f=f'=\varepsilon_H$. Thus, a $2$-cell $k:f\Rightarrow f'$ is a grouplike element $k$ in $\Bbbk$ and hence $k=1$ is the identity $2$-cell.    
\end{proof}

We observe that $G$ preserves binary products and terminal object. Indeed, given cocommutative bialgebras $H_1$ and $H_2$, by Theorem \ref{thm:Tw2cart} the binary product object of $G(H_{1})$ and $G(H_{2})$ in $\TwTrBialg$ is given by
\[G(H_1)\otimes G(H_2)
=(H_1, 1\ot 1)\ot (H_2, 1\ot 1)=(H_1\ot H_2,1\ot1\ot1\ot 1)=G(H_1\ot H_2),\]
while the projections are given by $(\id\ot\varepsilon,1\ot 1)=G(\id\ot\varepsilon)$ and $(\varepsilon\ot\id,1\ot 1)=G(\varepsilon\ot\id).$ We can compute the diagonal morphism  $\langle G(\id\ot\varepsilon),G(\varepsilon\ot\id)\rangle
=((\id\ot\varepsilon)\ot (\varepsilon\ot\id))\Delta_{H_1\ot H_2},1\ot1\ot1\ot 1)
=\id.$ Thus, the $2$-functor $G$ preserves binary products. 
\begin{invisible}
 Note that, for $\Cc\coloneqq\TwTrBialg$, we have that
 \[\Cc((H,\Ss),G(H_1\ot H_2))=\Cc((H,\Ss),G(H_1)\ot G(H_2))\cong\Cc((H,\Ss),G(H_1))\times \Cc((H,\Ss),G(H_2))\]
 is a category equivalence.
\end{invisible}
Moreover, $G(\Bbbk)=(\Bbbk,1\ot 1)$, which is the terminal object in $\TwTrBialg$, see Lemma \ref{lem:termobj}. Therefore, the terminal morphism $G(\Bbbk)\to (\Bbbk,1\ot 1)$ is necessarily the identity, so $G$ preserves the terminal object.
\begin{invisible}
 Also here, for $\Cc\coloneqq\TwTrBialg$, we have that the functor
 \[\Cc((H,\Ss),G(\Bbbk))
 =\Cc((H,\Ss),(\Bbbk,1\otimes 1))\to *\] is a category isomorphism. 
\end{invisible}

If we compose $G$ and the $2$-functor $F:\TwTrBialg\to\TwBialg$ of \eqref{def:funcF}, we obtain the $2$-functor 
\[FG:\Bialgcc\to \TwBialg,\; H\mapsto H,\; f\mapsto (f,1\ot 1),\; a\mapsto a.\]

\subsection{The classifying category of \texorpdfstring{$\TwTrBialg$}{TwTr}}
% \rd{[Se vogliamo includere questa sezione, diventa interessante l'articolo \href{https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/196249CA26B5AA751850948C25988F3D/S0008414X00026869a.pdf/groupoid-enriched-categories-and-homotopy-theory.pdf}{Fantham}. Infatti $\TwTrBialg$ sembra essere una \emph{groupoid enriched category} mentre $\GaTwTrBialg$ sembra essere la \emph{homotopy class category} $\pi\TwTrBialg$.] } \as{[Vero, si potrebbe aggiungere un remark con il collegamento a \href{https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/196249CA26B5AA751850948C25988F3D/S0008414X00026869a.pdf/groupoid-enriched-categories-and-homotopy-theory.pdf}{Fantham}]}\medskip

Let $\Cc$ be a (locally small) $2$-category. Then, the isomorphism classes of $1$-cells form a set, i.e. $\Cc$ is \emph{locally essentially small}. Therefore, following \cite[Example 2.1.27]{Johnson-Yau-book}, we can consider the \emph{classifying category} $\mathsf{Cla}(\Cc)$ of $\Cc$. Its objects are the objects in $\Cc$ while for objects $X,Y$ in $\mathsf{Cla}(\Cc)$, the set $\mathsf{Cla}(\Cc)(X,Y)$ consists of isomorphism classes $[f]$ of $1$-cells $f$ in $\Cc(X,Y)$. The identity morphism of an object $X$ in $\mathsf{Cla}(\Cc)$ is $[\id_X]$. The composition of isomorphism classes is given by $[f]\circ [g]=[f\circ g]$.

It is easy to check that, if the $2$-category $\Cc$ has binary products and terminal object, then the $1$-category $\mathsf{Cla}(\Cc)$ has them too and that the $1$-functor \[Q:\Cc\to \mathsf{Cla}(\Cc),\;X\mapsto X,\;f\mapsto [f],\] preserves them.

\begin{invisible} Let us consider the binary product in $\Cc$.
Let $[f]:X\rightarrow A$ and $[g]:X\rightarrow B$ be morphisms in $\mathsf{Cla}(\Cc)$. Since they are represented by morphisms $f:X\rightarrow A$ and $g:X\rightarrow B$ in $\Cc$, we know that there exists a morphism $h:X\rightarrow A\times B$ and
isomorphisms $ph\cong f$ and $qh\cong g$, where $p:A\times B\to A$ and $q:A\times B\to B$. Thus, $[p]\circ[h]=[ph]=[f]$ and $[q]\circ [h]=[qh]=[g]$.
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrix{&X\ar[ld]_{[f]}\ar[rd]^{[g]}\ar@{.>}[d]|{[h]}\\ A&
A\times B\ar[l]_{[p]}\ar[r]^{[q]}& B}
\end{equation*}%
Assume now there is another morphism $[k]:X\to A\times B$ such that $[p]\circ[k]=[f]$ and $[q]\circ [k]=[g]$. Then $pk\cong f\cong ph$ and $qk\cong g\cong qh $ so that 
there are invertible $2$-cells 
$\alpha:ph\Rightarrow pk$ and $\beta:qh\Rightarrow qk$. Thus, there is a unique $\gamma:h\Rightarrow k$ such that $p\gamma=\alpha$ and $q\gamma=\beta.$ Similarly, there is a unique $\gamma':k\Rightarrow h$ such that $p\gamma'=\alpha^{-1}$ and $q\gamma'=\beta^{-1}.$ Thus 
\[
p(\gamma\vc\gamma')=\id_p\hc(\gamma\vc\gamma')=(\id_p\vc\id_p)\hc(\gamma\vc\gamma')=(\id_p\hc\gamma)\vc(\id_p\hc\gamma')=p\gamma\vc p\gamma'=\id 
\]
and hence $\gamma\circ \gamma'=\id$. Similarly $\gamma'\circ \gamma=\id$. Thus $\gamma:h\Rightarrow k$ is invertible and hence $[h]=[k]$. 

Let us now consider the terminal object. Given an object $X$ in $\mathsf{Cla}(\Cc)$, then $X$ is an object in $\Cc$ so that there is a 1-cell $f:X\rightarrow \mathbf{1}$ and hence a 1-cell $[f]:X\rightarrow \mathbf{1}$.  If there is another 1-cell $[g]:X\rightarrow \mathbf{1}$, then it is represented by the 1-cell $g:X\rightarrow \mathbf{1}$. Therefore, there is a unique necessarily invertible $2$-cell $f\Rightarrow g$ so that $[f]=[g]$.

Explicitly, this means that for any 0-cell $X$ there is a 1-cell $X\rightarrow \mathbf{1}$ and for any two 1-cells $f,g:X\rightarrow \mathbf{1}$ there is a unique $2$-cell $f\Rightarrow g$. Note that this $2$-cell is necessarily  an isomorphism.

We deal with the functor $Q$. The diagonal morphism $<Q(p),Q(q)>:Q(A\times B)\to Q(A)\times Q(B)$ is uniquely determined by the equalities $[p]\circ <Q(p),Q(q)>=Q(p)$ and $[q]\circ <Q(p),Q(q)>=Q(p)$
and hence it is necessarily $[\id_{A\times B}]$. This shows that $Q$ preserves binary products. The terminal morphism $Q(\mathbf{1})\to \mathbf{1}$ is necessarily $\id_{\mathbf{1}}$. Thus, $Q$ preserves the terminal object.
\end{invisible}


Therefore, in view of \cref{thm:Tw2cart}, the classifying category 
$\mathsf{Cla}(\TwTrBialg)$ of $\TwTrBialg$ has binary products and terminal object, hence it is a cartesian monoidal category. Explicitly, its objects are triangular bialgebras and its morphisms are equivalence classes $[f,\Ff]:(H,\Rr)\to (H',\Rr')$ of morphisms $(f,\Ff):(H,\Rr)\to (H',\Rr')$ in $\TwTrBialg$. Note that $[f,\Ff]=[f',\Ff']$ if, and only if, there is an invertible gauge transformation $a:(f,\Ff)\Rightarrow (f',\Ff')$. Thus, $[f,\Ff]$ can be called the \emph{gauge class} of $(f,\Ff)$ %\as{[Potremmo anche non introdurre $\GaTwTrBialg$. In alternativa lo farei qui.]} \rd{[OK!]}

Given morphisms $(f_1,\Ff_1):(H,\Ss)\to (H_1,\Rr_1)$ and $(f_2,\Ff_2):(H,\Ss)\to (H_2,\Rr_2)$ in $\TwTrBialg$, the diagonal morphism  $\langle[f_1,\Ff_1],[f_2,\Ff_2]\rangle$ in $\mathsf{Cla}(\TwTrBialg)$ is given by the gauge class $[f,\Ff]$ where $(f,\Ff)=\langle(f_1,\Ff_1),(f_2,\Ff_2)\rangle$ is the diagonal morphism in $\TwTrBialg$ of \cref{thm:Tw2cart}.

