We will now provide a characterization, in terms of binary products in $\TwTrBialg$, of the notion recalled in \cref{def:twtensprod} for an arbitrary twist, still in the triangular case. To this aim, we first need \cref{lem:iso&2cell} which is based on the following remark.

\begin{remark} 
\label{rmk:partial}
Consider two morphisms $(f',\Ff'),(f,\Ff):(H,\Rr)\to (H',\Rr')$ in $\TwTrBialg$ and let $a:(f,\Ff)\Rightarrow (f',\Ff')$ be a gauge transformation, with $a\in H'$. If it is invertible, then $a\in (H') ^\times_{\varepsilon'}\coloneqq\{a\in (H')^\times\mid \varepsilon'(a)=1\}.$
As in \cite[\S4.2]{Davydov}, we note that any $a\in H^\times_\varepsilon$ defines an automorphism $\partial(a):(H,\Rr)\to (H,\Rr)$, by setting $\partial(a)\coloneqq (\hat{a},(1\otimes 1)^a)$,
where $(1\otimes 1)^a$ is given as in \eqref{def:F^h}, for $\Ff=1\otimes 1$. Hence one obtains 
%$\Ff^a\coloneqq (a\otimes a)\Ff\Delta(a^{-1})$, whence 
the map \[\partial:H^\times_\varepsilon\to \mathrm{Aut}_{\TwTrBialg}(H,\Rr)\] which  is a group homomorphism. 
By definition of composition, one gets $\partial(a)\circ (f,\Ff)=(f',\Ff').$ 
\end{remark}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:iso&2cell}
   Let $a:(f,\Ff)\Rightarrow (f',\Ff')$ be an invertible $2$-cell in $\TwTrBialg$. Then, the $1$-cell $(f,\Ff)$ is invertible if, and only if, so is $(f',\Ff').$
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By \cref{rmk:partial}, since $a$ is invertible, then $\partial(a)\circ (f,\Ff)=(f',\Ff')$, with $\partial(a)$  invertible.
